(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e4d.8df0dd4388.js"]={"46":[{"id":"sdl46_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define visceral motor pathways and distinguish them from somatic motor pathways.","batch":1,"stem":"Which feature distinguishes a visceral motor pathway from a somatic motor pathway at the most basic anatomic level?","choices":{"A":"A visceral pathway uses a single neuron that projects directly from the spinal cord to its skeletal muscle target","B":"A visceral pathway uses two neurons in series, joined together by a synapse in a peripheral ganglion","C":"A visceral pathway releases only norepinephrine at its final synapse regardless of the target tissue involved","D":"A visceral pathway originates exclusively from cranial nerve nuclei rather than from the spinal cord itself","E":"A visceral pathway innervates only glandular tissue and never smooth muscle or cardiac muscle tissue"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A visceral motor pathway is built from two neurons in series, a preganglionic neuron with its cell body in the CNS and a postganglionic neuron with its cell body in a peripheral autonomic ganglion, whereas a somatic motor pathway uses a single lower motor neuron whose axon travels directly from the spinal cord or brainstem to skeletal muscle without an intervening peripheral synapse. The single-neuron description in the first wrong choice is the somatic, not visceral, arrangement. Not every visceral pathway releases norepinephrine, parasympathetic and some sympathetic postganglionic fibers are cholinergic. Visceral outflow arises from both spinal and cranial levels, not exclusively cranial nuclei. Visceral targets include smooth muscle and cardiac muscle in addition to glands.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the two-neuron autonomic pathway versus the one-neuron somatic pathway is classic first-pass exam content. COMLEX: OMM viscerosomatic reflexes rely on this same two-neuron autonomic architecture converging with somatic afferents at the spinal cord.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — the medullary visceral motor nuclei catalogued there sit alongside the somatic motor nuclei this objective now distinguishes them from","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define visceral motor pathways and distinguish them from somatic motor pathways.","batch":1,"stem":"The postganglionic neuron of a visceral motor pathway differs from the lower motor neuron of a somatic motor pathway in which of the following ways?","choices":{"A":"Its cell body is located within the intermediolateral cell column of the spinal cord, not a peripheral ganglion","B":"It releases acetylcholine directly onto nicotinic receptors of skeletal muscle fibers at the neuromuscular junction","C":"It receives its only synaptic input from primary sensory afferents rather than from preganglionic neurons","D":"It travels within a somatic peripheral nerve rather than within an autonomic nerve or plexus","E":"Its cell body is located outside the central nervous system, within a peripheral autonomic ganglion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The postganglionic autonomic neuron has its cell body outside the CNS, in a peripheral paravertebral, prevertebral, or terminal ganglion, giving the visceral pathway its two-neuron architecture, while the somatic lower motor neuron cell body sits within the spinal cord anterior horn or a cranial nerve motor nucleus and its single axon runs all the way to skeletal muscle. The intermediolateral cell column houses preganglionic, not postganglionic, cell bodies. Skeletal muscle nicotinic endplates are the somatic target, not the visceral postganglionic target. Postganglionic neurons receive their principal input from preganglionic neurons, not primarily from sensory afferents, and they travel within autonomic plexuses and nerves, not somatic peripheral nerves.","boardPrep":"Step 1: locating the postganglionic cell body outside the CNS is the anatomic detail examiners use to test the two-neuron concept. COMLEX: this peripheral ganglionic location is exactly what makes autonomic ganglia a target for surgical and pharmacologic sympathectomy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — the medullary visceral motor nuclei catalogued there sit alongside the somatic motor nuclei this objective now distinguishes them from","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define visceral motor pathways and distinguish them from somatic motor pathways.","batch":1,"stem":"Which target tissue is innervated by a two-neuron visceral motor pathway rather than a one-neuron somatic motor pathway?","choices":{"A":"Skeletal muscle of the diaphragm","B":"Skeletal muscle of the extraocular muscles","C":"Skeletal muscle of the tongue","D":"Cardiac muscle of the myocardium","E":"Skeletal muscle of the pharyngeal constrictors"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Cardiac muscle, along with smooth muscle and glands, is a visceral motor target reached by the two-neuron preganglionic-postganglionic pathway. The diaphragm, extraocular muscles, tongue, and pharyngeal constrictors are all skeletal muscle targets reached by a single somatic lower motor neuron, the phrenic nerve, cranial nerves III/IV/VI, cranial nerve XII, and the branchial motor components of cranial nerves IX/X/XI respectively, none of which synapse in a peripheral ganglion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sorting targets into \"skeletal muscle equals somatic, single neuron\" versus \"smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, or gland equals visceral, two neurons\" is a fast, reliable exam heuristic. COMLEX: recognizing cardiac muscle as a visceral target explains why cardiac dysfunction can arise from autonomic, not just somatic motor, disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — the medullary visceral motor nuclei catalogued there sit alongside the somatic motor nuclei this objective now distinguishes them from","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare sympathetic, parasympathetic, and enteric divisions by CNS origin, ganglion location, neurotransmitters, and target organs.","batch":1,"stem":"The preganglionic neurons of which autonomic division are found in the intermediolateral cell column of spinal cord segments T1 through L2?","choices":{"A":"The parasympathetic division, the craniosacral outflow","B":"The enteric division, classified as the intrinsic outflow","C":"Both the sympathetic and parasympathetic divisions equally","D":"Neither the sympathetic nor the parasympathetic division","E":"The sympathetic division, classified as the thoracolumbar outflow"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sympathetic preganglionic cell bodies occupy the intermediolateral cell column from T1 to L2, giving the sympathetic division its thoracolumbar designation. The parasympathetic division instead arises from cranial nerve nuclei of III, VII, IX, and X plus sacral segments S2 to S4, its craniosacral designation. The enteric division is intrinsic to the gut wall and does not arise from the intermediolateral cell column at all.","boardPrep":"Step 1: thoracolumbar equals sympathetic, craniosacral equals parasympathetic, is one of the highest-yield autonomic facts tested. COMLEX: this segmental pattern also explains why splanchnic nerve blocks target specific thoracolumbar levels for visceral pain control.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 1 — the six eye-movement classes already grouped there include the pupillary and accommodation reflexes this objective now assigns to opposing autonomic divisions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare sympathetic, parasympathetic, and enteric divisions by CNS origin, ganglion location, neurotransmitters, and target organs.","batch":1,"stem":"Compared with sympathetic ganglia, parasympathetic ganglia are typically located in which position relative to the target organ?","choices":{"A":"Immediately adjacent to the vertebral column, giving parasympathetic postganglionic fibers an unusually long course","B":"Near or within the target organ wall, giving parasympathetic postganglionic fibers a short course","C":"Within the intermediolateral cell column of the spinal cord itself, alongside preganglionic cell bodies","D":"Scattered diffusely along peripheral somatic nerves rather than grouped into any discrete ganglia","E":"Within the dorsal root ganglia themselves, alongside primary sensory neuron cell bodies"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Parasympathetic ganglia, the ciliary, pterygopalatine, submandibular, and otic ganglia for cranial outflow and terminal or intramural ganglia for thoracic, abdominal, and pelvic outflow, sit near or within the target organ wall, giving parasympathetic postganglionic fibers a short course. Sympathetic ganglia instead sit near the vertebral column as the paravertebral chain or along the aorta as prevertebral ganglia, far from the target, giving sympathetic postganglionic fibers a long course. Ganglion cell bodies never sit within the intermediolateral cell column, along somatic nerves, or within dorsal root ganglia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"parasympathetic ganglia sit near the target, sympathetic ganglia sit near the spine\" is a reliable rule for predicting relative preganglionic versus postganglionic fiber length. COMLEX: this proximity is why surgical parasympathetic denervation of a single organ is far more anatomically selective than sympathetic denervation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 1 — the six eye-movement classes already grouped there include the pupillary and accommodation reflexes this objective now assigns to opposing autonomic divisions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare sympathetic, parasympathetic, and enteric divisions by CNS origin, ganglion location, neurotransmitters, and target organs.","batch":1,"stem":"Which neurotransmitter is released by the majority of postganglionic sympathetic neurons onto their target tissue?","choices":{"A":"Acetylcholine, identical to the transmitter released by postganglionic parasympathetic neurons","B":"Epinephrine, identical to the hormone released by the adrenal medulla","C":"Norepinephrine, in contrast to the acetylcholine released by most postganglionic parasympathetic neurons","D":"Dopamine, in contrast to the norepinephrine released by postganglionic parasympathetic neurons","E":"Vasoactive intestinal peptide, in contrast to the norepinephrine released by postganglionic parasympathetic neurons"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Most postganglionic sympathetic neurons are noradrenergic, releasing norepinephrine, while most postganglionic parasympathetic neurons are cholinergic, releasing acetylcholine, a division that holds despite the shared cholinergic nature of every preganglionic synapse in both divisions. Epinephrine is the principal adrenal medulla hormone, not the typical sympathetic ganglionic neurotransmitter. Dopamine is not the default postganglionic sympathetic transmitter, and postganglionic parasympathetic neurons release acetylcholine, not norepinephrine. Vasoactive intestinal peptide is a parasympathetic neuropeptide cotransmitter, not the principal transmitter of either division.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sympathetic equals norepinephrine, parasympathetic equals acetylcholine, at the postganglionic synapse is a cornerstone autonomic pharmacology fact. COMLEX: this same distinction underlies the mechanism of every adrenergic and cholinergic drug tested on the exam.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 1 — the six eye-movement classes already grouped there include the pupillary and accommodation reflexes this objective now assigns to opposing autonomic divisions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Trace sympathetic outflow from the intermediolateral cell column to chain ganglia, prevertebral ganglia, adrenal medulla, head/neck structures, and pelvic organs.","batch":1,"stem":"A preganglionic sympathetic fiber exits the spinal cord at the T3 level and ascends through the sympathetic chain without synapsing until it reaches the superior cervical ganglion. Which of the following also describes a possible course taken by a preganglionic sympathetic fiber through the chain?","choices":{"A":"Synapsing exclusively within the dorsal root ganglion at its exact spinal level of origin, bypassing the chain","B":"Passing through the chain without synapsing, forming a splanchnic nerve that reaches a prevertebral ganglion instead","C":"Traveling retrograde into the posterior horn of the spinal cord to synapse on visceral sensory neurons","D":"Bypassing all ganglia entirely to synapse directly on the vascular smooth muscle of its target organ","E":"Synapsing within the ciliary ganglion regardless of its actual spinal level of preganglionic origin"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A preganglionic sympathetic fiber can take one of three courses: entering through the white ramus and synapsing in the chain ganglion at its own level, with postganglionic fibers leaving via the gray ramus, ascending or descending the chain to synapse at a different level, or passing through the chain entirely without synapsing to form a splanchnic nerve that terminates in a prevertebral ganglion. The dorsal root ganglion houses sensory, not sympathetic, neurons. Preganglionic fibers do not travel retrograde into the posterior horn, and they always synapse on a postganglionic neuron rather than bypassing ganglia to reach smooth muscle directly. The ciliary ganglion is a parasympathetic structure associated with cranial nerve III, not a target of sympathetic fibers.","boardPrep":"Step 1: knowing all three possible routes through the sympathetic chain explains why a single spinal segment can influence targets far from its own level. COMLEX: this chain architecture is the anatomic basis for paravertebral sympathetic block procedures.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 7 — the vertebral artery and PICA territory already mapped there border the same lateral medulla that carries the descending sympathetic fibers this objective now traces peripherally","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Trace sympathetic outflow from the intermediolateral cell column to chain ganglia, prevertebral ganglia, adrenal medulla, head/neck structures, and pelvic organs.","batch":1,"stem":"The chromaffin cells of the adrenal medulla are functionally most analogous to which structure of the sympathetic division?","choices":{"A":"Postganglionic sympathetic neurons, since chromaffin cells synapse with preganglionic fibers and secrete catecholamines","B":"Primary visceral afferent neurons, since chromaffin cells convey sensory information back to the intermediolateral cell column","C":"Enteric plexus neurons, since chromaffin cells generate reflexes entirely independent of central input","D":"Preganglionic sympathetic neurons, since chromaffin cells project axons that synapse within paravertebral ganglia","E":"Parasympathetic postganglionic neurons, since chromaffin cells release acetylcholine onto vascular smooth muscle"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Chromaffin cells share a neural crest origin with sympathetic ganglion neurons and receive direct cholinergic, nicotinic synaptic input from preganglionic sympathetic fibers, functionally making them equivalent to postganglionic sympathetic neurons, except that instead of synapsing on a discrete target they secrete catecholamines, mostly epinephrine, into the bloodstream for a widespread, endocrine-like effect. Chromaffin cells do not themselves project axons to synapse in paravertebral ganglia, are not sensory neurons, and do not generate independent reflexes like enteric neurons or release acetylcholine like parasympathetic postganglionic neurons.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the adrenal medulla as a modified sympathetic ganglion, with chromaffin cells equivalent to postganglionic neurons, is a classic testable analogy. COMLEX: this same mechanism explains why nicotinic ganglionic stimulants and blockers also affect adrenal catecholamine release.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 7 — the vertebral artery and PICA territory already mapped there border the same lateral medulla that carries the descending sympathetic fibers this objective now traces peripherally","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Trace sympathetic outflow from the intermediolateral cell column to chain ganglia, prevertebral ganglia, adrenal medulla, head/neck structures, and pelvic organs.","batch":1,"stem":"Postganglionic fibers arising from the superior cervical ganglion travel with the internal and external carotid arteries to innervate which of the following structures?","choices":{"A":"The pupillary dilator muscle, superior tarsal muscle, and sweat glands of the face and scalp","B":"The sphincter pupillae muscle and the ciliary muscle responsible for near-vision accommodation","C":"The parotid gland exclusively, reached via the auriculotemporal nerve branch of the mandibular nerve","D":"The submandibular and sublingual salivary glands exclusively, sparing the parotid gland","E":"The detrusor muscle of the urinary bladder and the pelvic reproductive organs"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The superior cervical ganglion, the largest paravertebral ganglion, sends postganglionic fibers along the internal and external carotid arteries to the pupillary dilator muscle, the superior and inferior tarsal muscles, and the sweat glands of the face and scalp, along with modulating salivary, lacrimal, and nasal gland vasculature. The sphincter pupillae and ciliary muscle are instead parasympathetic targets reached through the ciliary ganglion. The parotid gland is reached by the otic ganglion via cranial nerve IX, and the submandibular and sublingual glands by the submandibular ganglion via cranial nerve VII. The bladder detrusor muscle is reached by sacral parasympathetic outflow, not the superior cervical ganglion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the superior cervical ganglion supplies the entire sympathetic territory of the eye, face, and scalp, the anatomic basis of Horner syndrome. COMLEX: palpating for a mass along the carotid sheath is relevant here, since lesions at this level classically produce oculosympathetic findings.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 7 — the vertebral artery and PICA territory already mapped there border the same lateral medulla that carries the descending sympathetic fibers this objective now traces peripherally","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Trace craniosacral parasympathetic outflow through CN III, VII, IX, X, and S2-S4 pelvic splanchnic nerves.","batch":1,"stem":"Postganglionic parasympathetic neurons supplying cranial structures sit in the ciliary, pterygopalatine, submandibular, and otic ganglia. Although they are counted as visceral efferent parts of cranial nerves III, VII, and IX, their postganglionic axons generally reach their targets by traveling with branches of which nerve?","choices":{"A":"The vagus nerve","B":"The facial nerve","C":"The oculomotor nerve","D":"The trigeminal nerve","E":"The glossopharyngeal nerve"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Postganglionic parasympathetic axons from the cranial ganglia generally travel distally with branches of the trigeminal nerve; for example, otic ganglion fibers join the auriculotemporal nerve to reach the parotid, and ciliary ganglion fibers run in the short ciliary nerves. The vagus has no discrete cranial ganglia because its postganglionic neurons lie in intramural ganglia, and the facial, oculomotor, and glossopharyngeal nerves carry the preganglionic fibers to these ganglia rather than the postganglionic distribution.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cranial parasympathetics: III to ciliary ganglion (pupil, ciliary muscle); VII to pterygopalatine (lacrimal) and submandibular (submandibular and sublingual glands); IX to otic (parotid); X to intramural ganglia. COMLEX: Trigeminal branches serve as the delivery routes, so facial surgery along V branches can disturb gland secretion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 5 — facial and glossopharyngeal nerve branches that carry taste and secretomotor fibers","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Trace craniosacral parasympathetic outflow through CN III, VII, IX, X, and S2-S4 pelvic splanchnic nerves.","batch":1,"stem":"Preganglionic parasympathetic fibers of the facial nerve that travel via the chorda tympani synapse in which ganglion?","choices":{"A":"The pterygopalatine ganglion, supplying the lacrimal gland and the mucous glands of the nasal cavity","B":"The submandibular ganglion, supplying the submandibular and sublingual salivary glands","C":"The otic ganglion, supplying the parotid gland via the auriculotemporal nerve","D":"The ciliary ganglion, supplying the sphincter pupillae and the ciliary muscle","E":"The celiac ganglion, supplying foregut derivatives within the abdominal cavity"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The facial nerve carries two separate preganglionic parasympathetic routes from the superior salivatory nucleus: fibers via the greater petrosal nerve synapse in the pterygopalatine ganglion, supplying the lacrimal and nasal glands, while fibers via the chorda tympani synapse in the submandibular ganglion, supplying the submandibular and sublingual salivary glands. The otic ganglion belongs to cranial nerve IX, the ciliary ganglion to cranial nerve III, and the celiac ganglion is a sympathetic prevertebral ganglion unrelated to cranial nerve VII.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the facial nerve's two parasympathetic branch points, greater petrosal to pterygopalatine and chorda tympani to submandibular, are a classic source of exam confusion worth memorizing precisely. COMLEX: a proximal facial nerve lesion affecting both branches produces combined dry eye and dry mouth, a useful localizing clue.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 6 — the pupillary light reflex efferent limb already traced there begins at the same Edinger-Westphal nucleus this objective now places within the full craniosacral outflow","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Trace craniosacral parasympathetic outflow through CN III, VII, IX, X, and S2-S4 pelvic splanchnic nerves.","batch":1,"stem":"Preganglionic parasympathetic fibers from spinal cord segments S2 through S4 travel via the pelvic nerves to innervate which structures?","choices":{"A":"Foregut and midgut derivatives, along with the heart and all structures of the entire respiratory system","B":"The parotid gland along with associated structures of the external and middle ear","C":"The lacrimal gland along with the mucous glands of the nasal and palatal cavity","D":"The sphincter pupillae along with the ciliary muscle responsible for near accommodation","E":"Hindgut derivatives distal to the left colic flexure, the urinary bladder, and reproductive organs"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sacral parasympathetic preganglionic neurons at S2 to S4 form the pelvic nerves, the nervi erigentes, supplying hindgut derivatives distal to the left colic flexure along with the urinary bladder and reproductive organs. Foregut, midgut, the heart, and the respiratory system are instead vagal territory. The parotid gland and ear structures belong to cranial nerve IX, the lacrimal and nasal glands to cranial nerve VII, and the eye to cranial nerve III.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the left colic flexure as the dividing line between vagal and sacral parasympathetic gut territory is a frequently tested landmark. COMLEX: this same sacral outflow is the target of pelvic floor and bladder-focused osteopathic techniques.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 6 — the pupillary light reflex efferent limb already traced there begins at the same Edinger-Westphal nucleus this objective now places within the full craniosacral outflow","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the major brainstem autonomic nuclei and their functions.","batch":1,"stem":"Which brainstem nucleus receives primary visceral sensory input from the glossopharyngeal and vagus nerves and is considered the most important structure coordinating brainstem autonomic function because of its widespread connections?","choices":{"A":"The dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus, which projects preganglionic parasympathetic fibers to thoracic and abdominal viscera","B":"The solitary nucleus, which projects to vagal motor neurons, salivatory nuclei, and reticular cell groups","C":"The nucleus ambiguus, which provides branchial motor innervation to the pharynx and larynx","D":"The rostral ventrolateral medulla, which provides tonic excitatory drive to sympathetic preganglionic neurons","E":"The Edinger-Westphal nucleus, which provides preganglionic parasympathetic fibers to the ciliary ganglion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The solitary nucleus receives primary visceral sensory afferents via cranial nerves IX and X and, because of its extensive reciprocal connections with vagal motor neurons, salivatory nuclei, reticular groups, and the broader central autonomic network, is considered the single most important brainstem structure for coordinating autonomic function. The dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus, nucleus ambiguus, rostral ventrolateral medulla, and Edinger-Westphal nucleus are all motor output structures rather than the primary sensory-integrating relay.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the solitary nucleus as the central visceral sensory relay is a foundational fact underlying the baroreceptor and chemoreceptor reflexes tested elsewhere. COMLEX: lesions here explain otherwise-puzzling combinations of dysautonomia following medullary strokes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — the dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus, nucleus ambiguus, and solitary nucleus already catalogued as medullary landmarks there are the same nuclei this objective now organizes by autonomic function","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the major brainstem autonomic nuclei and their functions.","batch":1,"stem":"Neurons of the rostral ventrolateral medulla are referred to as the vasopressor center because they project to which structure to increase peripheral vascular resistance?","choices":{"A":"The dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus, driving preganglionic parasympathetic neurons involved in cardiovascular regulation","B":"The intermediolateral cell column, driving preganglionic sympathetic neurons involved in cardiovascular regulation","C":"The nucleus ambiguus, driving preganglionic parasympathetic neurons that slow heart rate","D":"The sacral parasympathetic nucleus, driving preganglionic parasympathetic neurons of the pelvic nerves","E":"The Edinger-Westphal nucleus, driving preganglionic parasympathetic neurons of the ciliary ganglion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Rostral ventrolateral medulla neurons have intrinsic pacemaker activity and send a major descending projection to the intermediolateral cell column, providing tonic excitatory drive to preganglionic sympathetic neurons that raises vascular resistance and cardiac output. None of the parasympathetic nuclei listed are targets of this sympathetic vasopressor pathway.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the rostral ventrolateral medulla to intermediolateral cell column pathway is the descending limb of the baroreceptor reflex, essential for orthostatic blood pressure control. COMLEX: this pathway is what fails in multiple system atrophy, producing severe orthostatic hypotension.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — the dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus, nucleus ambiguus, and solitary nucleus already catalogued as medullary landmarks there are the same nuclei this objective now organizes by autonomic function","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the major brainstem autonomic nuclei and their functions.","batch":1,"stem":"The nucleus ambiguus is unusual among brainstem autonomic nuclei because it provides which combination of functions?","choices":{"A":"Preganglionic parasympathetic innervation to the gastrointestinal tract, together with somatic motor innervation to the diaphragm","B":"Preganglionic sympathetic innervation to the heart, together with branchial motor innervation to the tongue and pharynx","C":"Branchial motor innervation to the palate, pharynx, and larynx, plus preganglionic parasympathetic supply to the heart","D":"Special sensory innervation for taste, together with preganglionic parasympathetic innervation to the salivary glands","E":"Postganglionic sympathetic innervation to the eye, together with somatic motor innervation to the extraocular muscles"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The nucleus ambiguus is classically a branchial motor nucleus supplying skeletal muscle of the palate, pharynx, and larynx via cranial nerves IX, X, and XI, but it unusually also contributes the preganglionic parasympathetic fibers of the vagus that specifically slow heart rate, a dual identity distinct from purely sensory or purely autonomic nuclei. It is not sympathetic, does not innervate the tongue or diaphragm, carries no taste fibers, and has no role in ocular innervation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the nucleus ambiguus's dual branchial motor and cardiac parasympathetic role is a favorite exam distinguishing point from the dorsal motor vagal nucleus. COMLEX: this explains why isolated cardiac vagal dysfunction can occur without any accompanying gastrointestinal parasympathetic deficit.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — the dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus, nucleus ambiguus, and solitary nucleus already catalogued as medullary landmarks there are the same nuclei this objective now organizes by autonomic function","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain how the hypothalamus coordinates autonomic, endocrine, behavioral, and emotional responses.","batch":1,"stem":"The hypothalamus is considered the highest integrator of autonomic and endocrine function because it sends direct projections to which of the following preganglionic visceral motor structures?","choices":{"A":"Postganglionic neurons of the superior cervical ganglion exclusively, sparing brainstem nuclei","B":"The postganglionic neurons of the ciliary ganglion exclusively, sparing all preganglionic nuclei","C":"The dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus, the nucleus ambiguus, and the intermediolateral cell column","D":"The primary sensory neurons of the dorsal root ganglia exclusively, sparing all motor nuclei","E":"The chromaffin cells of the adrenal medulla exclusively, bypassing all preganglionic relays"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Certain hypothalamic nuclei project directly to preganglionic visceral motor neurons in the dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus, the nucleus ambiguus, and the intermediolateral cell column, allowing the hypothalamus to shape autonomic outflow at its origin. The hypothalamus does not send direct projections to postganglionic ganglion neurons, primary sensory ganglia, or adrenal chromaffin cells while bypassing the preganglionic relay.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this direct hypothalamic-to-preganglionic projection is the anatomic basis for hypothalamic lesions producing prominent autonomic disturbance. COMLEX: this same connection underlies stress-induced autonomic symptoms commonly reported in clinical practice.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 → Obj 5 — the brainstem autonomic nuclei catalogued above are the direct targets of the hypothalamic projections this objective now describes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain how the hypothalamus coordinates autonomic, endocrine, behavioral, and emotional responses.","batch":1,"stem":"In addition to its direct projections to brainstem and spinal preganglionic neurons, the hypothalamus integrates autonomic function with endocrine function through which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Direct regulation of adrenal cortical secretion, bypassing the anterior pituitary","B":"Direct regulation of thyroid hormone release, bypassing the anterior pituitary","C":"Direct innervation of peripheral endocrine glands through postganglionic sympathetic fibers exclusively","D":"Direct regulation of anterior and posterior pituitary secretory activity","E":"Indirect regulation of pituitary secretion mediated entirely through the solitary nucleus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The hypothalamus directly regulates the secretory activity of both the anterior pituitary, through hypophysiotropic hormones delivered by the hypophyseal portal system, and the posterior pituitary, through direct neurosecretory axons, linking autonomic and endocrine integration at a single anatomic hub. It does not bypass the anterior pituitary to control the adrenal cortex or thyroid directly, does not rely exclusively on postganglionic sympathetic fibers for peripheral endocrine control, and does not depend entirely on the solitary nucleus as an intermediary for pituitary regulation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the hypothalamic-pituitary axis is one of the highest-yield endocrine topics and depends on this direct hypothalamic control. COMLEX: hypothalamic-pituitary dysfunction from trauma or tumor often presents with combined endocrine and autonomic complaints, reflecting this shared integration point.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 → Obj 5 — the brainstem autonomic nuclei catalogued above are the direct targets of the hypothalamic projections this objective now describes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain how the hypothalamus coordinates autonomic, endocrine, behavioral, and emotional responses.","batch":1,"stem":"Although visceral motor activity is generally beyond conscious voluntary control, the central autonomic network integrates input from higher cortical centers, allowing which of the following to influence visceral function?","choices":{"A":"Emotional and cognitive activity, mediated through hypothalamic ties to the central autonomic network","B":"Only spinal reflex arcs, since higher cortical centers have no access to the central autonomic network","C":"Only somatic sensory input from peripheral nerves, since emotional states cannot influence visceral function","D":"Only direct volitional control equivalent to that exerted over the skeletal muscles of the limbs","E":"Only pharmacologic modulation, since the central autonomic network lacks any endogenous cortical input"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The central autonomic network integrates diverse visceral and somatic sensory input together with input from higher CNS centers involved in cognition and complex behavior, so emotional status and mental activity clearly influence visceral structures even though autonomic control overall remains outside conscious volitional command. Spinal reflex arcs and somatic sensory input alone do not account for this cognitive-emotional influence, autonomic function is not under the same direct volitional control as skeletal muscle, and the network's cortical input is endogenous, not merely pharmacologic.","boardPrep":"Step 1: recognizing that emotional and cognitive states genuinely alter autonomic output, not just subjective symptoms, is tested through stress-related cardiovascular and gastrointestinal vignettes. COMLEX: this mind-body integration underlies the osteopathic emphasis on the interconnection of structure, function, and emotional health.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 → Obj 5 — the brainstem autonomic nuclei catalogued above are the direct targets of the hypothalamic projections this objective now describes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q19","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Localize Horner syndrome, autonomic dysreflexia, neurogenic bladder, orthostatic hypotension, and common pupillary disorders.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient presents with ptosis, miosis, and facial anhidrosis on the right side. Which mechanism explains the miosis component of this triad?","choices":{"A":"Direct parasympathetic overactivity of the ciliary muscle, causing an accommodative spasm of near vision","B":"Loss of parasympathetic input to the pupillary sphincter, causing fixed and unreactive pupillary constriction","C":"Unopposed parasympathetic action on the pupillary sphincter after loss of sympathetic dilator tone","D":"Compression of the optic nerve, reducing the afferent limb of the pupillary light reflex pathway","E":"Direct sympathetic overactivity of the pupillary sphincter, causing an exaggerated reflex constriction"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Horner syndrome miosis results from loss of sympathetic tone to the pupillary dilator muscle, leaving the parasympathetically innervated pupillary sphincter unopposed and producing a relatively small pupil. The ciliary muscle governs accommodation, not the pupil size itself, and is not the source of this finding. Parasympathetic input to the sphincter is intact, not lost, in Horner syndrome. The optic nerve and afferent visual pathway are unaffected in Horner syndrome, which is a purely efferent, sympathetic problem.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"unopposed parasympathetic action\" is the exact mechanistic phrase examiners expect for Horner miosis. COMLEX: pairing miosis with ptosis and anhidrosis on the same side is the fastest bedside route to the Horner syndrome diagnosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 7 — nystagmus, diplopia, and pupil findings were already used there to localize visual motor lesions; this objective now extends that localizing framework across the autonomic nervous system","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q20","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Localize Horner syndrome, autonomic dysreflexia, neurogenic bladder, orthostatic hypotension, and common pupillary disorders.","batch":1,"stem":"A 29-year-old man with a complete T4 spinal cord injury develops a pounding headache, profuse sweating and flushing above the level of his injury, and severe hypertension with reflex bradycardia after his urinary catheter becomes blocked. Which mechanism best explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"A noxious stimulus below the injury level triggers a massive sympathetic reflex uninhibited by descending supraspinal control","B":"A noxious stimulus below the level of injury triggers a massive parasympathetic reflex mediated entirely by the sacral spinal cord","C":"Loss of all spinal reflex activity below the level of injury produces a compensatory increase in vagal tone above the injury","D":"Chronic denervation of the enteric nervous system produces unopposed secretion of vasoactive intestinal peptide throughout the gut","E":"The bladder distension directly compresses the intermediolateral cell column, producing a local pressure-induced sympathetic discharge"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Autonomic dysreflexia arises in spinal cord injury at T6 or above once spinal shock resolves, when denervation hypersensitivity leaves the isolated sympathetic reflex circuit below the lesion massively overactive, and a noxious stimulus such as bladder distension triggers unregulated sympathetic outflow that descending inhibitory pathways can no longer dampen because the injury has severed them. The hypertension itself, not a primary parasympathetic reflex, is what triggers reflex bradycardia via the still-intact baroreflex above the lesion. The mechanism is not a compensatory vagal increase, enteric denervation, or direct mechanical compression of the intermediolateral cell column.","boardPrep":"Step 1: autonomic dysreflexia above T6, triggered by bladder or bowel distension, with hypertension and reflex bradycardia, is a classic and dangerous board vignette. COMLEX: prompt catheter or impaction relief is the emergency management priority before treating the blood pressure directly.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 7 — nystagmus, diplopia, and pupil findings were already used there to localize visual motor lesions; this objective now extends that localizing framework across the autonomic nervous system","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q21","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Localize Horner syndrome, autonomic dysreflexia, neurogenic bladder, orthostatic hypotension, and common pupillary disorders.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with a complete spinal cord lesion above the sacral micturition center develops a spastic, reflex bladder with involuntary detrusor contractions against a closed sphincter. This pattern differs from the flaccid, atonic bladder seen with a cauda equina lesion because of which key anatomic difference?","choices":{"A":"A suprasacral lesion destroys the sacral reflex arc entirely, abolishing all detrusor contraction permanently","B":"A cauda equina lesion spares the sacral reflex arc, allowing the detrusor reflex to persist and become hyperactive","C":"A suprasacral lesion selectively damages the pelvic splanchnic nerves while sparing the spinal cord itself","D":"A cauda equina lesion selectively spares the pudendal nerve while extensively damaging the sacral spinal cord itself","E":"A suprasacral lesion spares the sacral reflex arc, letting the detrusor reflex become hyperactive over time"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A suprasacral lesion leaves the sacral micturition reflex arc, the preganglionic parasympathetic neurons and their connections, intact, so once spinal shock resolves the detrusor reflex becomes hyperactive and spastic, often with detrusor-sphincter dyssynergia. A cauda equina or conus lesion instead destroys the sacral reflex arc itself, producing a flaccid, areflexic bladder with overflow incontinence, the opposite of the two mismatched wrong choices describing reflex arc preservation or destruction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: spastic upper motor neuron bladder above the sacral center versus flaccid lower motor neuron bladder at or below it is a frequently tested localization pair. COMLEX: bladder pattern, alongside reflex and sensory level findings, helps localize the exact spinal segment of injury.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 7 — nystagmus, diplopia, and pupil findings were already used there to localize visual motor lesions; this objective now extends that localizing framework across the autonomic nervous system","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q22","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Use clinical findings to distinguish preganglionic from postganglionic and central from peripheral autonomic lesions.","batch":1,"stem":"Following denervation of a postganglionic autonomic pathway, the target tissue characteristically develops which physiologic change that can be exploited diagnostically?","choices":{"A":"Denervation resistance, a blunted response to exogenously applied neurotransmitter or agonist","B":"Complete and permanent loss of receptor expression on the denervated target tissue itself","C":"A shift from receptor-mediated signaling to a purely mechanical, non-receptor response","D":"Denervation supersensitivity, an exaggerated response to any exogenous agonist","E":"An exaggerated response that occurs only with preganglionic, never postganglionic, denervation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Denervation of a postganglionic autonomic pathway produces denervation supersensitivity, an exaggerated target-tissue response to locally applied or circulating neurotransmitter or agonist, likely reflecting upregulated receptor density, and this principle is used diagnostically to distinguish a postganglionic lesion from a preganglionic or central one, which does not produce this exaggerated peripheral response. Receptor expression is not permanently lost, and target-tissue signaling remains receptor-mediated rather than becoming purely mechanical.","boardPrep":"Step 1: denervation supersensitivity is the mechanistic key behind every pharmacologic pupil-testing question that follows in this objective. COMLEX: this same principle explains why chronically denervated smooth muscle can become hyper-responsive to standard-dose autonomic medications.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 8 — clinical findings were already used there to localize a lesion to a specific medullary structure; this objective now applies that same localizing logic across the entire autonomic pathway","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q23","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Use clinical findings to distinguish preganglionic from postganglionic and central from peripheral autonomic lesions.","batch":1,"stem":"An isolated autonomic finding without any accompanying cranial nerve, sensory, or motor deficit most strongly suggests a lesion at which level of the autonomic pathway?","choices":{"A":"A central level within the hypothalamus, which is densely packed with other functional systems","B":"A central level within the brainstem, which is densely packed with other functional systems","C":"A peripheral postganglionic or ganglionic level, distant from other central nervous system structures","D":"A central level within the spinal cord, which is densely packed with other functional systems","E":"A preganglionic level within the intermediolateral cell column, which lies adjacent to somatic motor pathways"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Central autonomic structures, the hypothalamus, brainstem, and spinal cord, sit immediately adjacent to numerous other functional pathways, so central lesions typically produce additional neurologic findings alongside the autonomic deficit. A peripheral postganglionic or ganglionic lesion is anatomically isolated from these other systems and more often produces an isolated autonomic finding without accompanying neurologic signs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: \"isolated finding equals peripheral, accompanying findings equal central\" is the single most efficient localizing rule for autonomic vignettes. COMLEX: this same principle guides the physical exam sequence when working up any new autonomic complaint.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 8 — clinical findings were already used there to localize a lesion to a specific medullary structure; this objective now applies that same localizing logic across the entire autonomic pathway","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q24","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Use clinical findings to distinguish preganglionic from postganglionic and central from peripheral autonomic lesions.","batch":1,"stem":"A child with familial dysautonomia, also called Riley-Day syndrome or hereditary sensory and autonomic neuropathy type III, faints when standing because of orthostatic hypotension. What is the underlying pathophysiology of the failed baroreceptor reflex in this condition?","choices":{"A":"Loss of visceral afferent fibers carrying baroreceptor input","B":"Progressive degeneration of central autonomic neurons","C":"Autoantibodies against ganglionic nicotinic receptors","D":"Destruction of the rostral ventrolateral medulla by infarct","E":"Denervation hypersensitivity of the intermediolateral column"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In familial dysautonomia the loss of visceral afferent fibers leaves inadequate baroreceptor input reaching the solitary nucleus, so standing does not trigger the reflex rise in vascular tone and cardiac output. Progressive central neurodegeneration describes multiple system atrophy, ganglionic nicotinic receptor antibodies describe autoimmune autonomic ganglionopathy, and an RVLM infarct is a focal central lesion rather than a hereditary sensory neuropathy. Denervation hypersensitivity underlies autonomic dysreflexia after spinal cord injury.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Baroreceptor reflex: carotid and aortic sinus afferents (IX, X) to the solitary nucleus, which drives vagal cardioinhibition and inhibits RVLM vasopressor neurons. COMLEX: Afferent failure (familial dysautonomia) and central degeneration (MSA) both present with orthostatic hypotension, so look for the accompanying sensory or motor features.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 → Obj 7 — baroreceptor afferents and the cardiovascular reflexes they drive","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q25","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define visceral motor pathways and distinguish them from somatic motor pathways.","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher administers a ganglionic blocking agent that antagonizes nicotinic acetylcholine receptors located specifically on postganglionic autonomic neurons. Which of the following effects would be expected following administration of this agent?","choices":{"A":"Interruption of neuromuscular transmission at skeletal muscle, while autonomic ganglionic transmission remains unaffected","B":"Interruption of transmission only at sympathetic ganglia, while parasympathetic ganglionic transmission remains unaffected","C":"Interruption of transmission only at parasympathetic ganglia, while sympathetic ganglionic transmission remains unaffected","D":"Interruption of transmission at both sympathetic and parasympathetic ganglia, sparing the neuromuscular junction","E":"Interruption of postganglionic norepinephrine release, while ganglionic transmission itself remains unaffected"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Nicotinic acetylcholine receptors at autonomic ganglia are pharmacologically distinct from the nicotinic receptors at the skeletal muscle neuromuscular junction, so a ganglionic blocker interrupts acetylcholine transmission at both sympathetic and parasympathetic ganglia, since both preganglionic synapses are cholinergic, without affecting the neuromuscular junction. The skeletal muscle receptor subtype is the wrong target for this drug class. Both autonomic divisions, not just one, are affected because both rely on the same ganglionic nicotinic receptor subtype. The blockade occurs at the ganglionic synapse itself, not at the postganglionic norepinephrine release step.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ganglionic blockers test both sympathetic and parasympathetic outflow simultaneously since both preganglionic synapses share the same nicotinic receptor subtype, distinct from the neuromuscular junction. COMLEX: older ganglionic-blocker antihypertensives caused severe orthostatic hypotension together with dry mouth and constipation, reflecting this combined blockade.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 46 Obj 2 — the ganglionic nicotinic receptor blocked here sits at the same synapse this objective's sympathetic-versus-parasympathetic comparison distinguishes by postganglionic neurotransmitter","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q26","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define visceral motor pathways and distinguish them from somatic motor pathways.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman receives an injection of botulinum toxin into the axillary skin to treat severe focal hyperhidrosis. The toxin cleaves SNARE proteins and prevents acetylcholine release at the injected site. At which structure does this toxin exert its therapeutic effect in this patient?","choices":{"A":"The preganglionic sympathetic synapse located within the paravertebral ganglion, well proximal to this site","B":"The postganglionic parasympathetic synapse onto eccrine sweat glands, which receive no parasympathetic fibers","C":"The somatic neuromuscular junction of the arrector pili muscles, an entirely different synapse type","D":"The postganglionic sympathetic synapse onto eccrine sweat glands, a cholinergic exception to typical transmission","E":"The postganglionic sympathetic synapse onto vascular smooth muscle of the axillary skin, not the sweat glands"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Eccrine sweat glands are innervated by sympathetic postganglionic fibers that are, unusually, cholinergic rather than noradrenergic, so a locally injected acetylcholine-release blocker reduces sweating precisely at this exceptional synapse. Vascular smooth muscle and arrector pili muscles receive standard noradrenergic sympathetic input, not cholinergic. Eccrine sweat glands receive no parasympathetic innervation at all. Blocking the preganglionic synapse would produce far more widespread autonomic effects than the focal, localized action seen clinically with an intradermal injection.","boardPrep":"Step 1: eccrine sweat glands are the single most tested exception to \"sympathetic equals noradrenergic.\" COMLEX: recognizing this cholinergic sympathetic synapse explains both the mechanism and the site-specific, non-systemic nature of botulinum toxin therapy for hyperhidrosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 46 Obj 2 — the eccrine sweat gland exception targeted here is the same cholinergic-sympathetic anomaly built into the divisional comparison of this objective","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q27","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define visceral motor pathways and distinguish them from somatic motor pathways.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with long-standing diabetes mellitus develops gastroparesis, resting tachycardia, and orthostatic hypotension, in addition to a symmetric loss of vibration and pinprick sensation in a stocking-glove distribution. Which additional finding would most specifically confirm involvement of the two-neuron visceral motor pathway rather than the somatic motor pathway?","choices":{"A":"Diminished cardiovagal response on the Valsalva maneuver, reflecting impaired autonomic function","B":"Reduced compound muscle action potential amplitude on nerve conduction study of the peroneal nerve bilaterally","C":"Diminished deep tendon reflexes at the ankle bilaterally on neurologic examination","D":"Weakness of ankle dorsiflexion demonstrated on manual muscle testing bilaterally","E":"Atrophy of the intrinsic muscles of the foot noted on physical examination bilaterally"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Diabetic autonomic neuropathy affects the two-neuron visceral pathway, both preganglionic and postganglionic fibers, and Valsalva-based cardiovagal reflex testing is the standard bedside way to demonstrate this. Reduced compound muscle action potential amplitude, diminished deep tendon reflexes, weakness, and muscle atrophy are all findings reflecting the single-neuron somatic motor pathway rather than the two-neuron visceral pathway.","boardPrep":"Step 1: diabetic autonomic neuropathy is classically assessed with heart rate variability and Valsalva testing, a distinguishing point from the more commonly tested somatic peripheral neuropathy findings. COMLEX: gastroparesis and orthostatic hypotension are frequent vignette clues pointing toward autonomic rather than purely somatic peripheral nerve disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 46 Obj 7 — this same two-neuron visceral pathway failure is what produces the orthostatic hypotension localized later in this SDL","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q28","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare sympathetic, parasympathetic, and enteric divisions by CNS origin, ganglion location, neurotransmitters, and target organs.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old man sustains a complete spinal cord transection at the T4 level in a motor vehicle collision. Despite the loss of supraspinal and extrinsic autonomic input below the lesion, basic peristaltic reflexes in his small intestine continue to function. Which feature of the enteric division best explains this preserved function?","choices":{"A":"Sympathetic postganglionic fibers below the lesion continue to receive intact input from the intermediolateral cell column","B":"Postganglionic parasympathetic neurons of the vagus nerve are located within the spinal cord rather than within the gut wall","C":"The enteric division receives its preganglionic input directly from sacral spinal cord segments unaffected by a thoracic lesion","D":"The enteric nervous system contains an intrinsic neuron network generating reflexes independent of central regulation","E":"Interstitial cells of Cajal require ongoing input from extrinsic autonomic nerves to generate their pacemaker activity"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The enteric nervous system, comprising an estimated 100 million neurons organized into myenteric and submucosal plexuses, contains its own sensory neurons, interneurons, and motor neurons capable of generating the peristaltic reflex independently of central nervous system input, though it is normally modulated by extrinsic parasympathetic, generally enhancing, and sympathetic, generally inhibitory, input. A T4 transection disrupts descending control of sympathetic outflow rather than leaving it intact. Postganglionic vagal neurons sit within the gut wall, not the spinal cord. The enteric division does not receive preganglionic input from the sacral cord in the way true parasympathetic outflow does. Interstitial cells of Cajal are intrinsic pacemakers that do not require extrinsic autonomic input to generate their rhythmic activity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the enteric nervous system's capacity for independent reflex activity, sometimes called the \"second brain,\" is a frequently tested concept in gastrointestinal physiology. COMLEX: this intrinsic autonomy is why bowel function often persists, at least in basic form, after high spinal cord injury.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 46 Obj 1 — the enteric division's independence from the two-neuron preganglionic and postganglionic architecture defined at the start of this SDL is exactly what explains its resilience here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q29","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare sympathetic, parasympathetic, and enteric divisions by CNS origin, ganglion location, neurotransmitters, and target organs.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient is given a muscarinic acetylcholine receptor antagonist to reduce excessive perspiration prior to a surgical procedure. Blockade of muscarinic receptors would be expected to reduce secretion from which structure, despite that structure receiving sympathetic rather than parasympathetic innervation?","choices":{"A":"Apocrine sweat glands, whose postganglionic sympathetic innervation is cholinergic rather than noradrenergic","B":"Salivary glands, whose postganglionic sympathetic innervation is noradrenergic rather than cholinergic","C":"Lacrimal glands, whose postganglionic parasympathetic innervation is cholinergic and unaffected by sympathetic status","D":"Eccrine sweat glands, whose postganglionic sympathetic innervation is cholinergic rather than noradrenergic","E":"Adrenal medulla, whose innervation is preganglionic and unaffected by muscarinic blockade"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Stimulation of secretion by eccrine sweat glands, specifically, is mediated by muscarinic acetylcholine receptors, since their postganglionic sympathetic fibers are cholinergic rather than noradrenergic, the classic exception to the sympathetic pattern. Apocrine sweat glands are not the ones specifically noted for this muscarinic, cholinergic-sympathetic mechanism. Lacrimal glands are driven by parasympathetic, not sympathetic, cholinergic input and are unaffected by this sympathetic exception framing. Salivary glands do receive sympathetic input, but it is noradrenergic, not muscarinic-mediated. The adrenal medulla is driven by preganglionic nicotinic, not muscarinic, transmission.","boardPrep":"Step 1: eccrine sweat gland secretion is the specific muscarinic-sympathetic exception tested repeatedly on autonomic pharmacology questions. COMLEX: this explains why anticholinergic medications can cause anhidrosis and heat intolerance despite acting on a nominally sympathetic target.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 46 Obj 3 — this same sympathetic pathway to facial and body sweat glands is traced anatomically through the superior cervical ganglion later in this SDL","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q30","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare sympathetic, parasympathetic, and enteric divisions by CNS origin, ganglion location, neurotransmitters, and target organs.","batch":2,"stem":"Which neuropeptide is co-released with the principal neurotransmitter by postganglionic sympathetic vasoconstrictor fibers and potentiates the effect of circulating epinephrine at the adrenergic synapse?","choices":{"A":"Vasoactive intestinal peptide, co-released with acetylcholine by parasympathetic postganglionic fibers","B":"Substance P, co-released with acetylcholine by preganglionic sympathetic fibers","C":"Neuropeptide Y, co-released with norepinephrine by sympathetic vasoconstrictor fibers","D":"Enkephalin, co-released with acetylcholine by preganglionic parasympathetic fibers","E":"Calcitonin gene-related peptide, co-released with acetylcholine by enteric sensory neurons"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Neuropeptide Y is the most prevalent neuropeptide of the sympathetic division, co-released with norepinephrine specifically by vasoconstrictor postganglionic fibers, where it stimulates vascular smooth muscle contraction, potentiates epinephrine's effect, and paradoxically inhibits further norepinephrine release. Vasoactive intestinal peptide is instead the characteristic parasympathetic neuropeptide. Substance P and enkephalins can be coexpressed by some preganglionic neurons, but neither is the defining vasoconstrictor-associated peptide described here, and calcitonin gene-related peptide is primarily associated with sensory, not vasoconstrictor motor, fibers.","boardPrep":"Step 1: neuropeptide Y as the signature sympathetic cotransmitter, paired against vasoactive intestinal peptide as the parasympathetic cotransmitter, is a clean high-yield pairing. COMLEX: neuropeptide cotransmission explains why autonomic responses can outlast the fast synaptic effects of the primary small-molecule transmitter alone.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 46 Obj 5 — the vasoconstrictor fibers defined here by their neuropeptide ultimately originate from the rostral ventrolateral medulla catalogued among the brainstem autonomic nuclei","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q31","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Trace sympathetic outflow from the intermediolateral cell column to chain ganglia, prevertebral ganglia, adrenal medulla, head/neck structures, and pelvic organs.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man undergoes resection of a tumor arising from the descending colon. During the procedure, the surgeon identifies and preserves the prevertebral ganglion that provides postganglionic sympathetic innervation to this segment of bowel. Which ganglion is most likely being preserved?","choices":{"A":"The celiac ganglion, supplying foregut derivatives throughout the territory of the celiac artery","B":"The aorticorenal ganglion, supplying the renal vasculature within the territory of the renal arteries","C":"The superior mesenteric ganglion, supplying midgut derivatives within the territory of the superior mesenteric artery","D":"The inferior mesenteric ganglion, supplying hindgut derivatives within the inferior mesenteric artery territory","E":"The stellate ganglion, supplying cervicothoracic structures within the territory of the subclavian artery"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Postganglionic fibers from each prevertebral ganglion supply the same visceral territory as the corresponding branch of the aorta, so the descending colon, a hindgut derivative supplied by the inferior mesenteric artery, receives its postganglionic sympathetic innervation from the inferior mesenteric ganglion. The celiac ganglion supplies foregut derivatives, the aorticorenal ganglion supplies renal vasculature, the superior mesenteric ganglion supplies midgut derivatives, and the stellate ganglion is a paravertebral, not prevertebral, ganglion supplying the upper limb and head and neck rather than abdominal viscera.","boardPrep":"Step 1: matching each prevertebral ganglion to its corresponding aortic branch and embryologic gut segment is a reliable framework for abdominal autonomic anatomy questions. COMLEX: this same arterial-territory matching explains referred visceral pain patterns from bowel segments to specific abdominal regions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 46 Obj 2 — prevertebral ganglion topography follows the same target-organ-matching principle used earlier to compare the autonomic divisions by ganglion location","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q32","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Trace sympathetic outflow from the intermediolateral cell column to chain ganglia, prevertebral ganglia, adrenal medulla, head/neck structures, and pelvic organs.","batch":2,"stem":"A 42-year-old woman presents with episodic headaches, palpitations, diaphoresis, and severe hypertension. Imaging reveals a vascular adrenal mass, and biochemical testing confirms markedly elevated plasma metanephrines. A tumor arising from which cell population best explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Cells of the adrenal cortex zona fasciculata, which secrete cortisol under preganglionic sympathetic control","B":"Postganglionic parasympathetic neurons of the celiac ganglion, which secrete acetylcholine into the bloodstream","C":"Chromaffin cells of the adrenal medulla, functionally equivalent to postganglionic sympathetic neurons","D":"Preganglionic sympathetic neurons of the intermediolateral cell column that have migrated ectopically","E":"Enteric neurons of the myenteric plexus that have undergone neoplastic transformation within the adrenal capsule"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This is a classic pheochromocytoma presentation, a tumor of the chromaffin cells of the adrenal medulla, a neural crest-derived population that receives direct preganglionic sympathetic input and, functioning as an equivalent to postganglionic sympathetic neurons, secretes catecholamines into the bloodstream to produce episodic hypertension, palpitations, diaphoresis, and headache. The adrenal cortex secretes cortisol under anterior pituitary, not sympathetic, control. Parasympathetic ganglia do not secrete catecholamines into the blood. Preganglionic neuron cell bodies remain in the CNS by definition rather than forming an ectopic adrenal tumor, and the adrenal gland does not contain enteric neurons.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pheochromocytoma is a high-yield vignette built entirely on the chromaffin cell as a modified postganglionic sympathetic neuron. COMLEX: episodic symptom pattern, not constant hypertension, is the key clue distinguishing pheochromocytoma from more common causes of hypertension.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 46 Obj 1 — chromaffin cell physiology depends on the same preganglionic-to-postganglionic-equivalent architecture defined at the very start of this SDL","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q33","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Trace sympathetic outflow from the intermediolateral cell column to chain ganglia, prevertebral ganglia, adrenal medulla, head/neck structures, and pelvic organs.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with a Pancoast tumor at the apex of the right lung develops right-sided ptosis, miosis, and facial anhidrosis. The tumor most likely compresses preganglionic sympathetic fibers before they can synapse in which structure?","choices":{"A":"The superior cervical ganglion, the paravertebral ganglion supplying postganglionic fibers to the eye and face","B":"The stellate ganglion, formed by fusion of the inferior cervical and first thoracic ganglia, supplying the upper extremity","C":"The celiac ganglion, the prevertebral ganglion supplying foregut derivatives of the abdomen","D":"The ciliary ganglion, the parasympathetic ganglion supplying the sphincter pupillae muscle","E":"The pterygopalatine ganglion, the parasympathetic ganglion supplying the lacrimal gland itself"},"correct":"A","explanation":"An apical lung tumor compresses preganglionic sympathetic fibers near their T1 origin before they ascend the sympathetic chain to synapse in the superior cervical ganglion, producing a preganglionic Horner syndrome with ptosis, miosis, and anhidrosis of the entire ipsilateral face. The stellate ganglion instead supplies the upper limb, the celiac ganglion is an abdominal prevertebral ganglion, and the ciliary and pterygopalatine ganglia are parasympathetic structures unrelated to this sympathetic pathway.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pancoast tumor causing preganglionic Horner syndrome by compressing fibers before the superior cervical ganglion is one of the highest-yield oncologic-neurologic crossover vignettes. COMLEX: associated arm pain and hand weakness from brachial plexus involvement often accompany this same lesion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 46 Obj 7 — this same preganglionic Horner mechanism is localized against its postganglionic and central counterparts later in this SDL","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q34","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Trace craniosacral parasympathetic outflow through CN III, VII, IX, X, and S2-S4 pelvic splanchnic nerves.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman develops a complete right facial nerve palsy after a temporal bone fracture, with hyperacusis and diminished taste over the anterior two-thirds of the tongue. She also reports that her right eye feels persistently dry and irritated, with reduced tearing when peeling onions compared with before the injury. Given a lesion proximal to the geniculate ganglion, which nerve branch specifically accounts for her dry eye?","choices":{"A":"The chorda tympani, carrying preganglionic parasympathetic fibers to the submandibular ganglion","B":"The greater petrosal nerve, carrying preganglionic fibers to the pterygopalatine ganglion","C":"The tympanic nerve, carrying preganglionic parasympathetic fibers to the otic ganglion","D":"The lesser petrosal nerve, carrying preganglionic parasympathetic fibers to the otic ganglion","E":"The nervus intermedius distal to its exit from the facial canal, carrying only taste fibers"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A lesion proximal to the geniculate ganglion interrupts the entire facial nerve trunk before any of its branches split off, so it also disrupts the more distally branching chorda tympani and nerve to stapedius, explaining her hyperacusis and taste loss, but the dry, irritated eye specifically results from interrupting the greater petrosal nerve, which carries preganglionic parasympathetic fibers from the superior salivatory nucleus to the pterygopalatine ganglion for lacrimation. The chorda tympani carries preganglionic parasympathetic fibers to the submandibular ganglion for salivation and taste over the anterior two-thirds of the tongue, not tearing, so while it is also damaged by this proximal lesion, it does not explain the dry eye finding specifically. The tympanic nerve and lesser petrosal nerve belong to the glossopharyngeal, not facial, parasympathetic pathway to the otic ganglion, and are unrelated to this patient's facial nerve injury. The nervus intermedius carries the preganglionic parasympathetic fibers themselves as a shared bundle, not only taste fibers, and is not the specific branch responsible for lacrimation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: greater petrosal nerve injury causing dry eye is a classic facial nerve localization pearl, distinguishing a proximal from a distal lesion. COMLEX: Schirmer testing for tear production is a useful bedside maneuver to confirm greater petrosal nerve involvement in a facial palsy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 45 Obj 6 — the efferent pupillary and lacrimal parasympathetic pathways traced there share their cranial nerve VII branch point with the outflow map built in this objective","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q35","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Trace craniosacral parasympathetic outflow through CN III, VII, IX, X, and S2-S4 pelvic splanchnic nerves.","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old man with poorly controlled diabetes mellitus develops acute-onset diplopia and ptosis of the right eye. Examination shows the eye deviated down and out, but pupillary size and reactivity to light are normal. Which explanation best accounts for the sparing of pupillary function in this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Diabetic microvascular disease characteristically spares the Edinger-Westphal nucleus while affecting the oculomotor fascicle","B":"The parasympathetic fibers of cranial nerve III travel within the trochlear nerve rather than the oculomotor nerve itself","C":"Compression from an adjacent aneurysm characteristically spares pupillary fibers while damaging only the somatic motor fibers","D":"The pupillary light reflex in this scenario is mediated by cranial nerve IV rather than cranial nerve III","E":"Ischemic injury to the core of the nerve spares the parasympathetic fibers that run peripherally within the nerve"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Within the oculomotor nerve, the parasympathetic fibers destined for the ciliary ganglion run superficially, supplied by pial vessels, while the somatic motor fibers occupy the central core, supplied by vasa nervorum; microvascular ischemic disease, as in diabetes, preferentially damages the central core and spares the peripheral parasympathetic fibers, producing a pupil-sparing palsy, the opposite pattern from a compressive lesion such as an aneurysm, which characteristically affects the peripheral parasympathetic fibers first and produces early pupillary involvement. The Edinger-Westphal nucleus itself is not selectively spared by microvascular disease affecting the peripheral nerve. Parasympathetic fibers of cranial nerve III travel within the oculomotor nerve, not the trochlear nerve, and the pupillary light reflex here remains mediated by cranial nerve III.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pupil-sparing ischemic CN III palsy versus pupil-involving compressive CN III palsy, based on core-versus-peripheral fiber location, is one of the highest-yield localization facts in neurology. COMLEX: any new pupil-involving CN III palsy warrants urgent vascular imaging to exclude a compressive posterior communicating artery aneurysm.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 46 Obj 8 — this same peripheral-versus-central fiber arrangement within a single nerve is generalized later in this SDL to localize autonomic lesions broadly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q36","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Trace craniosacral parasympathetic outflow through CN III, VII, IX, X, and S2-S4 pelvic splanchnic nerves.","batch":2,"stem":"A lesion selectively destroys the nucleus ambiguus while sparing the dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus. Which of the following functions would be most affected?","choices":{"A":"Parasympathetic slowing of heart rate, since cardiac preganglionic fibers of the vagus arise specifically from the nucleus ambiguus","B":"Parasympathetic stimulation of gastric acid secretion, since gastric preganglionic fibers arise specifically from the nucleus ambiguus","C":"Parasympathetic stimulation of pancreatic enzyme secretion, since pancreatic preganglionic fibers arise specifically from the nucleus ambiguus","D":"Parasympathetic stimulation of peristalsis in the small intestine, since intestinal preganglionic fibers arise specifically from the nucleus ambiguus","E":"Parasympathetic bronchoconstriction, since preganglionic fibers to the bronchial tree arise specifically from the nucleus ambiguus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The majority of vagal preganglionic parasympathetic fibers arise from the dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus and target thoracic and abdominal viscera, but the specific subset innervating the heart unusually arises from the nucleus ambiguus instead, so a selective nucleus ambiguus lesion sparing the dorsal motor nucleus would primarily impair parasympathetic slowing of heart rate. Gastric acid secretion, pancreatic enzyme secretion, intestinal peristalsis, and bronchial tone are all governed by preganglionic fibers from the dorsal motor nucleus, which remains intact in this scenario.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the cardiac exception to the dorsal motor nucleus's dominance of vagal outflow is a favorite way to test the nucleus ambiguus's dual identity. COMLEX: this explains why isolated brainstem lesions can occasionally spare gut motility while producing striking cardiac vagal dysfunction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 46 Obj 5 — the nucleus ambiguus introduced here as a parasympathetic outflow nucleus is catalogued alongside the other brainstem autonomic nuclei next in this SDL","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q37","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the major brainstem autonomic nuclei and their functions.","batch":2,"stem":"A healthy 25-year-old volunteer rises quickly from a seated to a standing position. Baroreceptor discharge from the carotid sinus decreases transiently, reducing excitatory input from the solitary nucleus to two brainstem targets. Which sequence of downstream changes best accounts for the compensatory rise in heart rate and vascular tone that follows?","choices":{"A":"Reduced excitatory drive to vagal preganglionic neurons decreases parasympathetic inhibition of the heart, while reduced inhibitory drive to the rostral ventrolateral medulla disinhibits sympathetic outflow","B":"Increased excitatory drive to vagal preganglionic neurons increases parasympathetic inhibition of the heart, while increased inhibitory drive to the rostral ventrolateral medulla further suppresses sympathetic outflow","C":"Reduced excitatory drive to the nucleus ambiguus decreases branchial motor tone, while increased inhibitory drive to the intermediolateral cell column suppresses sympathetic outflow","D":"Reduced inhibitory drive to vagal preganglionic neurons increases parasympathetic stimulation of the heart, while increased excitatory drive to the rostral ventrolateral medulla further increases sympathetic outflow","E":"Increased inhibitory drive to the dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus decreases gastrointestinal motility, while reduced excitatory drive to the rostral ventrolateral medulla suppresses sympathetic outflow"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The solitary nucleus normally provides excitatory drive to vagal preganglionic neurons, a tonic parasympathetic brake on heart rate, and inhibitory drive to the rostral ventrolateral medulla, a tonic restraint on sympathetic outflow. Standing reduces baroreceptor discharge, which reduces both of these solitary nucleus outputs: less excitation of vagal neurons releases the heart from parasympathetic inhibition, raising heart rate, while less inhibition of the rostral ventrolateral medulla disinhibits it, raising sympathetic outflow and vascular tone. The remaining choices reverse the direction of these changes or misassign the pathway to the wrong nuclei.","boardPrep":"Step 1: tracing the exact excitatory-versus-inhibitory wiring of the baroreceptor reflex, not just its end result, is what separates a strong from a superficial answer on this topic. COMLEX: this same reflex arc is what fails in orthostatic hypotension from autonomic disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 46 Obj 7 — this same reflex failure, rather than a discrete nuclear lesion, is what produces the orthostatic hypotension localized later in this SDL","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q38","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the major brainstem autonomic nuclei and their functions.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient develops a brainstem lesion that selectively destroys the dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus bilaterally while sparing the nucleus ambiguus. Which clinical finding would be most expected?","choices":{"A":"Resting bradycardia with fully preserved gastrointestinal motility, secretion, and tone","B":"Loss of the gag reflex with fully preserved gastrointestinal motility and normal heart rate","C":"Dysphagia from pharyngeal weakness with fully preserved gastrointestinal motility and tone","D":"Hoarseness from vocal cord paralysis with fully preserved gastrointestinal motility and tone","E":"Severe gastroparesis with reduced gut secretion, though heart rate remains normal"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus supplies the majority of preganglionic parasympathetic fibers to thoracic and abdominal viscera, including the stomach and intestine, so its selective destruction would primarily impair gastrointestinal motility and secretion while sparing heart rate, since cardiac vagal control arises from the nucleus ambiguus, which remains intact here. Bradycardia, loss of the gag reflex, pharyngeal weakness causing dysphagia, and vocal cord paralysis are all functions of the nucleus ambiguus, spared in this scenario, not the dorsal motor nucleus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this question flips the usual nucleus ambiguus emphasis to test the dorsal motor nucleus's own dominant role in gut, not cardiac, parasympathetic control. COMLEX: severe gastroparesis out of proportion to other cranial nerve findings should raise suspicion for a selective dorsal motor nucleus process.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 46 Obj 4 — the dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus damaged here supplies the same craniosacral outflow traced peripherally earlier in this SDL","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q39","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the major brainstem autonomic nuclei and their functions.","batch":2,"stem":"A brainstem infarct destroys the solitary nucleus bilaterally. Which of the following autonomic disturbances would be most expected as a direct consequence?","choices":{"A":"Isolated loss of pupillary constriction to light with preserved blood pressure regulation","B":"Isolated loss of salivary secretion with preserved blood pressure regulation","C":"Isolated loss of detrusor muscle contraction with preserved blood pressure regulation","D":"Labile blood pressure with loss of baroreceptor and chemoreceptor reflex buffering","E":"Isolated loss of pupillary dilation with preserved blood pressure regulation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The solitary nucleus is the central relay for baroreceptor and chemoreceptor afferents carried by cranial nerves IX and X, so its bilateral destruction removes the reflex buffering of blood pressure and blood gas homeostasis, producing markedly labile, unbuffered blood pressure. Isolated loss of pupillary constriction, salivary secretion, detrusor contraction, or pupillary dilation would instead reflect damage to the Edinger-Westphal nucleus and ciliary ganglion, the salivatory nuclei, the sacral parasympathetic nucleus, or the superior cervical ganglion respectively, not the solitary nucleus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: labile blood pressure as the signature of solitary nucleus failure ties together the baroreceptor and chemoreceptor reflexes into one testable concept. COMLEX: this same lability can complicate perioperative management in patients with brainstem stroke.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 46 Obj 6 — the solitary nucleus disrupted here is one of the same structures the hypothalamus depends on for coordinated autonomic regulation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q40","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain how the hypothalamus coordinates autonomic, endocrine, behavioral, and emotional responses.","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old woman develops acute chest pain, dyspnea, and apical ballooning of the left ventricle on echocardiography within hours of receiving news of her spouse's sudden death. Coronary angiography reveals no significant obstructive disease. Which mechanism best explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Acute emotional stress selectively activates the sacral parasympathetic nucleus, producing reflex coronary vasospasm","B":"Acute emotional stress selectively activates the nucleus ambiguus, producing reflex bradycardia and myocardial stunning","C":"Acute emotional stress selectively activates the enteric nervous system, producing a humoral catecholamine surge independent of the hypothalamus","D":"Acute emotional stress selectively activates the pterygopalatine ganglion, producing reflex vasodilation of the coronary circulation","E":"Acute emotional stress activates the central autonomic network, driving a sympathetic catecholamine surge that stuns the myocardium"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This presentation describes stress cardiomyopathy, a well-recognized clinical illustration that acute emotional stress, processed through the central autonomic network's hypothalamic and forebrain connections, can drive a sympathetic catecholamine surge, through descending projections to brainstem and spinal preganglionic neurons and to the adrenal medulla, severe enough to stun the myocardium and mimic ischemia without any coronary obstruction. The sacral parasympathetic nucleus, nucleus ambiguus, enteric nervous system, and pterygopalatine ganglion have no plausible role in generating this cardiac stress response.","boardPrep":"Step 1: stress cardiomyopathy is a favorite illustration that the \"involuntary\" autonomic nervous system is directly modulated by emotional and cognitive input via the hypothalamus. COMLEX: recognizing this entity prevents an unnecessary or delayed workup when angiography does not show obstructive disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 46 Obj 3 — the sympathetic outflow driving this catecholamine surge travels through the same intermediolateral cell column and adrenal medulla traced anatomically earlier in this SDL","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q41","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain how the hypothalamus coordinates autonomic, endocrine, behavioral, and emotional responses.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with a craniopharyngioma compressing the hypothalamus develops amenorrhea, poor thermoregulation with alternating fever and hypothermia, disrupted sleep-wake cycles, and blood pressure lability. Which explanation best accounts for this constellation of findings arising from a single lesion location?","choices":{"A":"The findings represent three anatomically unrelated lesions that coincidentally occurred simultaneously","B":"The hypothalamus regulates only endocrine function, so the autonomic and behavioral findings must reflect a separate brainstem lesion","C":"The hypothalamus regulates only thermoregulation, so the endocrine and autonomic findings must reflect direct pituitary gland destruction","D":"The hypothalamus integrates autonomic, endocrine, and behavioral regulation, so one lesion can disrupt all three domains at once","E":"The hypothalamus regulates only circadian rhythm, so the endocrine and autonomic findings must reflect a separate thalamic lesion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The hypothalamus's integrative role spans autonomic function, reflected here by blood pressure lability, endocrine function, reflected by amenorrhea from disrupted gonadotropin-releasing hormone secretion, and behavioral or homeostatic regulation, reflected by impaired thermoregulation and disrupted sleep-wake cycling, so a single compressive hypothalamic lesion parsimoniously explains this entire multi-domain presentation. Invoking multiple coincidental lesions, or restricting the hypothalamus to only one of these three functions, is inconsistent with its well-established integrative anatomy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a hypothalamic lesion producing combined endocrine, autonomic, and behavioral disturbance together is a classic integrative vignette pattern. COMLEX: recognizing this pattern early can guide appropriate endocrine and neurosurgical referral for a single unifying lesion rather than a fragmented workup.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 46 Obj 5 — the brainstem nuclei this lesion indirectly disconnects are the same ones catalogued as direct hypothalamic projection targets","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q42","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain how the hypothalamus coordinates autonomic, endocrine, behavioral, and emotional responses.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with a hypothalamic lesion involving the paraventricular nucleus develops impaired vasopressin release in response to hypotension, in addition to blunted sympathetic vasoconstriction. This presentation illustrates which principle of hypothalamic organization?","choices":{"A":"The hypothalamus regulates neuroendocrine output exclusively through the anterior pituitary, independent of any autonomic projection","B":"Vasopressin release and sympathetic vasoconstriction are mediated by entirely separate, non-hypothalamic structures that were coincidentally both damaged","C":"The paraventricular nucleus has no role in cardiovascular regulation and only regulates gastrointestinal secretion","D":"The hypothalamus coordinates neuroendocrine output with direct autonomic projections, so both respond together to hypotension","E":"Sympathetic vasoconstriction is regulated exclusively by the solitary nucleus, independent of any hypothalamic input"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The paraventricular nucleus illustrates hypothalamic integration well: it directly triggers posterior pituitary vasopressin release, a neuroendocrine mechanism promoting water retention and vasoconstriction, while also sending descending projections that influence brainstem and spinal sympathetic outflow, so a single hypothalamic lesion can blunt both compensatory mechanisms for hypotension together. Vasopressin release depends on the posterior, not anterior, pituitary pathway. The two mechanisms are not mediated by unrelated structures but converge on the same hypothalamic origin. The paraventricular nucleus does have a direct cardiovascular role, and sympathetic vasoconstriction is not regulated exclusively by the solitary nucleus independent of hypothalamic input.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the paraventricular nucleus is a favorite example of combined neuroendocrine and direct autonomic output from a single hypothalamic structure. COMLEX: this dual mechanism is part of why hypothalamic injury can produce disproportionately severe hemodynamic instability.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 46 Obj 5 — the sympathetic vasoconstriction blunted here depends on the same rostral ventrolateral medulla and intermediolateral cell column pathway catalogued among the brainstem autonomic nuclei","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q43","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Localize Horner syndrome, autonomic dysreflexia, neurogenic bladder, orthostatic hypotension, and common pupillary disorders.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman develops sudden neck pain followed by right-sided ptosis and miosis after a minor motor vehicle collision. Facial sweating is notably preserved, though the remainder of the examination is consistent with Horner syndrome. Imaging reveals a right internal carotid artery dissection. Which localization best explains the preserved facial sweating in this patient?","choices":{"A":"A preganglionic lesion proximal to the superior cervical ganglion, since all facial sweat fibers travel together with the internal carotid artery","B":"A postganglionic lesion distal to the superior cervical ganglion, since facial sweat fibers travel via the external carotid artery","C":"A central lesion within the hypothalamus, since central Horner syndrome characteristically spares facial sweating","D":"A postganglionic lesion within the ciliary ganglion, since parasympathetic and sympathetic sweat fibers travel together to the face","E":"A preganglionic lesion within the intermediolateral cell column, since spinal cord lesions characteristically spare facial sweating"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Postganglionic sympathetic fibers from the superior cervical ganglion split along two carotid routes: fibers to the facial sweat glands travel with the external carotid artery, while fibers to the pupil and eyelid travel with the internal carotid artery. A postganglionic lesion along the internal carotid artery, as in carotid dissection, therefore produces ptosis and miosis with preserved facial sweating. Preganglionic and central lesions, including those of the intermediolateral cell column, interrupt sympathetic outflow before this split and typically produce anhidrosis of the entire ipsilateral face rather than sparing it, and the ciliary ganglion is a parasympathetic relay that carries no sudomotor fibers.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Horner syndrome with preserved facial sweating is the classic postganglionic (third-neuron) pattern of carotid dissection, built on the external-versus-internal carotid fiber split. COMLEX: neck pain preceding Horner syndrome after minor trauma should raise suspicion for carotid dissection and prompt urgent vascular imaging.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 46 Obj 3 — the superior cervical ganglion and its carotid-associated postganglionic fibers were already traced anatomically earlier in this SDL","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q44","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Localize Horner syndrome, autonomic dysreflexia, neurogenic bladder, orthostatic hypotension, and common pupillary disorders.","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman with a complete C6 spinal cord injury sustained three years ago is brought to the emergency department with a severe throbbing headache, blood pressure of 200/120 mm Hg, heart rate of 48 beats per minute, and profuse sweating limited to her face and upper chest. Her urinary catheter is patent and draining well. Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate next step?","choices":{"A":"Administer intravenous atropine to treat the bradycardia as the primary abnormality","B":"Administer a sympathomimetic agent to further raise blood pressure and relieve the headache","C":"Perform a rectal examination to check for fecal impaction as the triggering noxious stimulus","D":"Reassure the patient that this presentation is expected and requires no further evaluation","E":"Discontinue the urinary catheter permanently to prevent bladder-triggered episodes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"With the catheter already patent and draining, the next most common trigger of autonomic dysreflexia is bowel distension or fecal impaction, so a rectal examination to search for and relieve this stimulus is the appropriate immediate step. The bradycardia here is a secondary baroreflex response to severe hypertension, not the primary abnormality to treat with atropine, and further raising the blood pressure or dismissing the episode risks serious complications, including stroke or seizure. The catheter should not be permanently discontinued, since bladder distension remains the single most common overall trigger and still requires ongoing monitoring and management, not removal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: systematically searching for and relieving the triggering noxious stimulus, bladder first, then bowel, is the correct first step in managing autonomic dysreflexia before treating the blood pressure pharmacologically. COMLEX: untreated autonomic dysreflexia can progress to seizure, stroke, or death, making this a genuine emergency despite the patient's baseline spinal cord injury.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 46 Obj 6 — the unopposed sympathetic reflex seen here escapes the same supraspinal, hypothalamically coordinated inhibition described earlier in this SDL","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q45","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Localize Horner syndrome, autonomic dysreflexia, neurogenic bladder, orthostatic hypotension, and common pupillary disorders.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man presents with recurrent syncope on standing, urinary incontinence, erectile dysfunction, and a new-onset resting tremor with rigidity and cerebellar gait ataxia. Autonomic testing confirms a sustained drop in systolic blood pressure of 35 mm Hg on standing without a compensatory rise in heart rate. Which diagnosis best explains this combination of autonomic failure with additional neurologic findings?","choices":{"A":"Isolated postganglionic Horner syndrome from a carotid dissection, unrelated to the additional movement and cerebellar findings","B":"Familial dysautonomia, a hereditary sensory and autonomic neuropathy caused by loss of visceral afferent fibers alone","C":"Multiple system atrophy, combining intermediolateral cell column loss with central motor and cerebellar degeneration","D":"Diabetic autonomic neuropathy, a peripheral process that does not affect central motor or cerebellar pathways","E":"Acute autonomic dysreflexia from an undiagnosed spinal cord injury sustained above the T6 level"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Multiple system atrophy, also called Shy-Drager syndrome, is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder combining orthostatic hypotension, from loss of intermediolateral cell column and ventrolateral medullary neurons, with parkinsonism and cerebellar signs, matching this patient's combined autonomic and central motor and cerebellar findings. Familial dysautonomia is a congenital, purely peripheral afferent disorder without parkinsonism or cerebellar signs. Isolated postganglionic Horner syndrome would not explain incontinence, erectile dysfunction, or movement and cerebellar findings. Diabetic autonomic neuropathy remains a peripheral process and does not produce central motor or cerebellar disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: multiple system atrophy as central autonomic failure combined with parkinsonism and cerebellar signs is a classic higher-order autonomic diagnosis. COMLEX: the lack of a compensatory heart rate rise despite a significant blood pressure drop is a key clue distinguishing a central autonomic failure pattern from simple volume depletion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 46 Obj 5 — the ventrolateral medullary and intermediolateral cell column neuron loss underlying this disorder targets structures catalogued among the brainstem autonomic nuclei","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q46","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Use clinical findings to distinguish preganglionic from postganglionic and central from peripheral autonomic lesions.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with suspected Horner syndrome undergoes pharmacologic testing. Topical cocaine, which blocks presynaptic norepinephrine reuptake, fails to dilate the affected pupil, confirming the diagnosis. Topical hydroxyamphetamine, which triggers norepinephrine release from intact postganglionic terminals, is then applied and also fails to dilate the affected pupil. Which localization is most consistent with these results?","choices":{"A":"A preganglionic lesion within the intermediolateral cell column, since postganglionic terminals remain intact and would still release stored norepinephrine","B":"A central lesion within the hypothalamus, since postganglionic terminals remain intact and would still release stored norepinephrine","C":"A central lesion within the lateral brainstem, since postganglionic terminals remain intact and would still release stored norepinephrine","D":"A preganglionic lesion within the sympathetic chain proximal to the superior cervical ganglion, since postganglionic terminals remain intact and would still release stored norepinephrine","E":"A postganglionic lesion distal to the superior cervical ganglion, since intact norepinephrine release requires intact postganglionic terminals"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Hydroxyamphetamine only dilates the pupil if the postganglionic neuron and its norepinephrine stores are intact, a condition satisfied in central and preganglionic Horner syndrome, so failure to dilate with hydroxyamphetamine localizes the lesion to the postganglionic neuron itself, distal to the superior cervical ganglion. Preganglionic and central lesions all leave the postganglionic terminal intact, so hydroxyamphetamine would be expected to produce dilation in each of those scenarios, unlike the postganglionic case described here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the cocaine-then-hydroxyamphetamine pharmacologic testing sequence for Horner syndrome is a classic, if now less commonly performed, board pharmacology vignette. COMLEX: understanding the underlying denervation supersensitivity principle is more clinically transferable today than memorizing the specific drug sequence itself.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 46 Obj 7 — this same three-level Horner localization is applied clinically without pharmacologic testing earlier in this SDL","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q47","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Use clinical findings to distinguish preganglionic from postganglionic and central from peripheral autonomic lesions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman is found to have a unilateral dilated pupil that reacts poorly to light but constricts slowly and sustains that constriction with prolonged near effort. Application of dilute pilocarpine, a concentration too low to affect a normal pupil, produces marked constriction of the affected pupil. Which mechanism best explains this exaggerated response to dilute pilocarpine?","choices":{"A":"Denervation supersensitivity of the iris sphincter after a postganglionic ciliary ganglion lesion","B":"Denervation supersensitivity of the iris sphincter following a preganglionic lesion of the Edinger-Westphal nucleus","C":"Upregulation of nicotinic receptors at the neuromuscular junction of the iris sphincter following a central lesion","D":"A compensatory increase in sympathetic tone to the iris dilator muscle following a peripheral nerve lesion","E":"Loss of the afferent limb of the pupillary light reflex without any efferent parasympathetic lesion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This presentation describes a tonic pupil, caused by a postganglionic lesion of the ciliary ganglion or short ciliary nerves, which produces denervation supersensitivity of the iris sphincter's muscarinic receptors, an exaggerated constriction to dilute pilocarpine that would not occur with a preganglionic or central lesion, since the postganglionic neuron itself remains intact in those cases. The iris sphincter is smooth muscle governed by muscarinic, not nicotinic, receptors, and this is a parasympathetic, not sympathetic, phenomenon.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the tonic pupil's exaggerated response to dilute pilocarpine is a direct clinical application of denervation supersensitivity localizing a lesion to the postganglionic parasympathetic neuron. COMLEX: light-near dissociation, poor light reactivity with preserved slow near constriction, is the defining bedside feature to recognize before confirmatory pharmacologic testing.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 45 Obj 6 — the ciliary ganglion and pupillary parasympathetic pathway traced there is the same postganglionic structure whose denervation produces this tonic pupil","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_q48","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Use clinical findings to distinguish preganglionic from postganglionic and central from peripheral autonomic lesions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man presents with new-onset left-sided ptosis and miosis. Examination also reveals left-sided limb ataxia, decreased pain and temperature sensation on the left face, and decreased pain and temperature sensation on the right body. Based on this combination of findings, which statement correctly distinguishes the level of this lesion from a peripheral postganglionic Horner syndrome?","choices":{"A":"The presence of miosis alone is sufficient to localize this lesion to a peripheral postganglionic level regardless of any other findings","B":"The crossed sensory and cerebellar findings localize this lesion centrally, whereas isolated Horner syndrome alone would favor a peripheral lesion","C":"Ptosis is only ever caused by central lesions, so this finding alone excludes any peripheral level of injury","D":"Anhidrosis is required to distinguish a central lesion from a peripheral one, and its absence here confirms a peripheral postganglionic lesion","E":"The combination of findings described is equally consistent with a lesion at any level of the sympathetic pathway, so no further localization is possible"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The combination of Horner syndrome with ipsilateral facial sensory loss, ipsilateral ataxia, and crossed body sensory loss is the classic pattern of a central lateral brainstem lesion, since these accompanying deficits arise from adjacent brainstem structures that a peripheral postganglionic lesion could never affect. Peripheral postganglionic Horner syndrome typically presents as an isolated finding without these additional central neurologic deficits, reinforcing the general principle that accompanying non-autonomic findings point toward a central, not peripheral, localization; the presence of miosis, ptosis, or anhidrosis alone does not by itself determine the anatomic level without considering the full accompanying examination.","boardPrep":"Step 1: using the full constellation of accompanying findings, not any single sign in isolation, to localize a Horner syndrome to central versus peripheral level is the integrative skill this objective builds toward. COMLEX: this same reasoning process, \"what else does this finding travel with,\" applies broadly across nearly every neurologic localization question on the exam.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 7 — the same lateral medullary vascular territory mapped there explains why this crossed sensory and autonomic pattern localizes to the central nervous system","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl46_bloom_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define visceral motor pathways and distinguish them from somatic motor pathways.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient with myasthenia gravis has autoantibodies directed against nicotinic acetylcholine receptors at the skeletal neuromuscular junction, producing fatigable limb weakness, but has entirely normal blood pressure regulation, sweating, and bowel and bladder function. Which principle explains this preserved autonomic function despite the autoimmune attack on nicotinic receptors?","choices":{"A":"Neuromuscular junction and autonomic ganglionic nicotinic receptors are identical, so the autoimmune process here should have affected both structures equally","B":"Autonomic ganglia lack any nicotinic acetylcholine receptors, relying entirely on muscarinic transmission at every preganglionic synapse","C":"Neuromuscular junction nicotinic receptors are a distinct molecular subtype from autonomic ganglionic nicotinic receptors, so an antibody selective for one spares the other","D":"The blood-brain barrier prevents this specific circulating autoantibody from ever reaching any peripheral autonomic ganglion in the body","E":"Autoimmune attack on any nicotinic receptor subtype always spares skeletal muscle specifically while affecting only autonomic structures"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Skeletal muscle neuromuscular junction nicotinic receptors and autonomic ganglionic nicotinic receptors are pharmacologically and molecularly distinct subtypes, so myasthenia gravis, which specifically targets the neuromuscular junction subtype, produces fatigable weakness while sparing autonomic ganglionic transmission, unlike an autoimmune process targeting the ganglionic subtype instead. The two receptor subtypes are not identical, that is precisely why antibody selectivity produces such different clinical pictures. Autonomic ganglia rely on fast nicotinic, not exclusively muscarinic, transmission at the preganglionic synapse. The blood-brain barrier is not relevant here, since both target structures are peripheral, and this is a receptor subtype issue, not a barrier issue. This autoimmune attack does not always spare skeletal muscle, myasthenia gravis is defined by its skeletal muscle involvement.","boardPrep":"Step 1: distinguishing neuromuscular junction from autonomic ganglionic nicotinic receptor subtypes explains why myasthenia gravis spares autonomic function despite both being nicotinic cholinergic synapses. COMLEX: this same subtype distinction underlies why nondepolarizing neuromuscular blockers used in anesthesia have comparatively limited autonomic ganglionic side effects.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 → Obj 1 — the two-neuron visceral architecture, tested here as a board-style receptor-subtype and reflex-arc vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl46_bloom_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define visceral motor pathways and distinguish them from somatic motor pathways.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient develops subacute, diffuse autonomic failure, orthostatic hypotension, fixed and dilated pupils, dry mouth, and bladder dysfunction, but has entirely normal muscle strength and reflexes on neurologic examination. Serum testing reveals autoantibodies against ganglionic nicotinic acetylcholine receptors. Which condition best fits this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Autoimmune autonomic ganglionopathy, in which antibodies against ganglionic nicotinic receptors impair transmission at autonomic ganglia while sparing somatic motor function","B":"Myasthenia gravis, in which antibodies against ganglionic nicotinic receptors impair transmission at autonomic ganglia while sparing somatic motor function","C":"Guillain-Barre syndrome, in which antibodies against ganglionic nicotinic receptors impair transmission at autonomic ganglia while sparing somatic motor function","D":"Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome, in which antibodies against ganglionic nicotinic receptors impair transmission at autonomic ganglia while sparing somatic motor function","E":"Multiple sclerosis, in which antibodies against ganglionic nicotinic receptors impair transmission at autonomic ganglia while sparing somatic motor function"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Autoimmune autonomic ganglionopathy results from antibodies directed against the ganglionic nicotinic acetylcholine receptor subtype, impairing both sympathetic and parasympathetic ganglionic transmission and producing diffuse autonomic failure, pupillary, salivary, cardiovascular, and bladder dysfunction, while sparing the pharmacologically distinct neuromuscular junction receptor subtype and thus somatic strength. Myasthenia gravis targets the neuromuscular junction subtype instead and characteristically produces fatigable weakness, not this isolated autonomic picture. Guillain-Barre syndrome is a demyelinating polyradiculoneuropathy producing ascending weakness and areflexia, not this isolated autonomic failure with normal strength. Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome targets presynaptic voltage-gated calcium channels at the neuromuscular junction, producing proximal weakness that improves with repeated activity, not this pattern. Multiple sclerosis is a central demyelinating disease that typically produces focal central neurologic deficits, not this diffuse peripheral ganglionic autoimmune picture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: autoimmune autonomic ganglionopathy is a recognized, if uncommon, cause of severe subacute dysautonomia that directly illustrates ganglionic nicotinic receptor pathology distinct from neuromuscular junction disease. COMLEX: recognizing normal strength alongside severe multisystem autonomic failure should prompt consideration of this specific ganglionic, rather than neuromuscular, autoimmune process.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 → Obj 1 — the two-neuron visceral architecture, tested here as a board-style receptor-subtype and reflex-arc vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl46_bloom_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Define visceral motor pathways and distinguish them from somatic motor pathways.","batch":3,"stem":"A physiologist compares the patellar tendon reflex, a somatic reflex, with the baroreceptor-mediated heart rate reflex, a visceral reflex. Which structural difference between these two reflex arcs best reflects the broader somatic-versus-visceral motor pathway distinction?","choices":{"A":"The somatic reflex arc requires an additional peripheral ganglionic synapse, while the visceral reflex arc synapses only within the central nervous system","B":"The somatic reflex arc synapses only within the central nervous system, while the visceral reflex arc requires an additional peripheral ganglionic synapse","C":"Both reflex arcs require an identical peripheral ganglionic synapse before reaching their respective final target tissues","D":"Neither reflex arc involves any central nervous system synapse, with both occurring entirely within peripheral structures","E":"The somatic reflex arc is entirely voluntary, while the visceral reflex arc is entirely involuntary in every circumstance"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The patellar reflex is a monosynaptic somatic arc completed entirely within the spinal cord, sensory afferent directly to a single lower motor neuron reaching skeletal muscle, while the baroreceptor heart rate reflex, a visceral reflex, requires its efferent limb to pass through an additional peripheral ganglionic synapse, the vagal or sympathetic ganglion, before reaching the heart, mirroring the broader two-neuron visceral versus one-neuron somatic motor architecture. Reversing which reflex requires the peripheral ganglionic synapse misattributes this structural distinction. Both reflexes do not require an identical peripheral ganglionic synapse, that is exactly the feature that differs between them. Both reflexes do involve central nervous system synapses, the sensory afferent connects centrally in both cases. Voluntary-versus-involuntary control is a separate concept from the anatomic number-of-synapses distinction being tested here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: comparing a classic somatic monosynaptic reflex with a visceral autonomic reflex reinforces the two-neuron peripheral ganglionic architecture that defines the visceral motor pathway. COMLEX: this same reflex-arc framework underlies the OMM concept of viscerosomatic and somatovisceral reflexes converging at the spinal cord.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 → Obj 1 — the two-neuron visceral architecture, tested here as a board-style receptor-subtype and reflex-arc vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl46_bloom_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare sympathetic, parasympathetic, and enteric divisions by CNS origin, ganglion location, neurotransmitters, and target organs.","batch":3,"stem":"Most peripheral blood vessels, sweat glands, piloerector muscles, and the adrenal medulla share which distinguishing feature of their autonomic innervation compared with organs like the heart, bronchi, or gastrointestinal tract?","choices":{"A":"They receive parasympathetic innervation only, lacking the dual, antagonistic sympathetic innervation present at these other organs listed","B":"They receive sympathetic innervation only, lacking the dual, antagonistic parasympathetic innervation present at these other organs","C":"They receive no autonomic innervation of any kind, functioning entirely independently of any central nervous system control","D":"They receive equally balanced dual innervation from both divisions, identical in every respect to the heart, bronchi, and gastrointestinal tract","E":"They receive innervation exclusively from the enteric division, entirely bypassing both the sympathetic and parasympathetic divisions"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Most blood vessels, sweat glands, piloerector muscles, and the adrenal medulla receive sympathetic innervation exclusively, without an opposing parasympathetic input, so their activity is regulated purely by varying sympathetic tone rather than by the push-pull balance seen at dually innervated organs like the heart, bronchi, and gastrointestinal tract, where sympathetic and parasympathetic inputs act antagonistically. These structures are not parasympathetic-only, that reverses the actual pattern. They do not receive equal dual innervation, that describes the dually innervated organs instead. They are not without any autonomic innervation, sympathetic tone actively regulates them. They are not enteric-division targets, the enteric division is largely confined to the gastrointestinal tract.","boardPrep":"Step 1: recognizing sympathetic-only targets, vessels, sweat glands, piloerectors, and the adrenal medulla, versus dually innervated organs is a high-yield distinction for predicting drug effects and disease patterns. COMLEX: this explains why sympathetic blockade alone can produce flushing and anhidrosis without any compensatory parasympathetic override at these specific tissues.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 → Obj 2 — the sympathetic/parasympathetic/enteric comparison, tested here as a board-style innervation-pattern vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl46_bloom_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare sympathetic, parasympathetic, and enteric divisions by CNS origin, ganglion location, neurotransmitters, and target organs.","batch":3,"stem":"A single sympathetic preganglionic neuron characteristically diverges to synapse on many postganglionic neurons across multiple ganglia, while a parasympathetic preganglionic neuron typically synapses on relatively few postganglionic neurons confined to one organ. Which functional consequence best follows from this difference in divergence ratio?","choices":{"A":"Sympathetic activation tends to produce localized, organ-specific effects, while parasympathetic activation tends to produce widespread, diffuse \"mass discharge\" effects","B":"Divergence ratio has no meaningful effect on the breadth of physiologic response produced by either autonomic division","C":"Higher divergence slows synaptic transmission speed considerably, making sympathetic responses characteristically much slower than parasympathetic ones","D":"Higher divergence requires additional neurotransmitters beyond acetylcholine at the ganglionic synapse itself for any effective transmission to occur","E":"Sympathetic activation tends to produce widespread, diffuse \"mass discharge\" effects, while parasympathetic activation tends to produce more localized, organ-specific effects"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Because a single sympathetic preganglionic neuron can synapse on postganglionic neurons distributed across many ganglia and organs, sympathetic activation can produce a widespread, coordinated \"mass discharge\" affecting the heart, vasculature, sweat glands, and adrenal medulla simultaneously, appropriate for a fight-or-flight response, while the lower divergence of parasympathetic preganglionic neurons supports more localized, organ-specific regulation, appropriate for a \"rest and digest\" function. Reversing which division shows greater divergence and which effect pattern results misattributes this distinction. Divergence ratio does meaningfully shape the breadth of the physiologic response, that is precisely the functional consequence being described. Higher divergence does not itself slow synaptic transmission speed, and it does not require additional neurotransmitters beyond acetylcholine at the ganglionic synapse.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sympathetic mass discharge versus parasympathetic localized action, rooted in differing divergence ratios, is a useful physiologic explanation for why sympathetic responses feel more global than parasympathetic ones. COMLEX: this same principle explains why isolated parasympathetic dysfunction, such as a single cranial nerve palsy, often produces a narrower symptom pattern than diffuse sympathetic failure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 → Obj 2 — the sympathetic/parasympathetic/enteric comparison, tested here as a board-style innervation-pattern vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl46_bloom_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare sympathetic, parasympathetic, and enteric divisions by CNS origin, ganglion location, neurotransmitters, and target organs.","batch":3,"stem":"Which cranial nerve, despite carrying substantial motor and sensory fibers, does not contribute any preganglionic parasympathetic fibers to the craniosacral outflow?","choices":{"A":"The oculomotor nerve, cranial nerve III, which carries only branchial and somatic motor fibers, no parasympathetic component","B":"The spinal accessory nerve, cranial nerve XI, which carries only branchial and somatic motor fibers, no parasympathetic component","C":"The facial nerve, cranial nerve VII, which carries only branchial and somatic motor fibers, no parasympathetic component","D":"The glossopharyngeal nerve, cranial nerve IX, which carries only branchial and somatic motor fibers, no parasympathetic component","E":"The vagus nerve, cranial nerve X, which carries only branchial and somatic motor fibers, no parasympathetic component"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The craniosacral parasympathetic outflow is carried specifically by cranial nerves III, VII, IX, and X, along with the sacral S2-S4 pelvic splanchnic nerves; the spinal accessory nerve, cranial nerve XI, despite its substantial motor role innervating the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius, carries no preganglionic parasympathetic fibers at all. Cranial nerve III does carry parasympathetic fibers via the Edinger-Westphal nucleus to the ciliary ganglion, cranial nerve VII via the superior salivatory nucleus to the pterygopalatine and submandibular ganglia, cranial nerve IX via the inferior salivatory nucleus to the otic ganglion, and cranial nerve X via the dorsal motor nucleus and nucleus ambiguus to thoracic and abdominal viscera, making each of these an incorrect answer to this specific exclusion question.","boardPrep":"Step 1: explicitly excluding cranial nerve XI from the craniosacral parasympathetic list, despite its prominent motor function, prevents a common confusion with the \"mixed\" cranial nerves that do carry parasympathetics. COMLEX: this distinction is relevant when evaluating isolated CN XI palsies, which should not produce any parasympathetic deficit.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 → Obj 2 — the sympathetic/parasympathetic/enteric comparison, tested here as a board-style innervation-pattern vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl46_bloom_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Trace sympathetic outflow from the intermediolateral cell column to chain ganglia, prevertebral ganglia, adrenal medulla, head/neck structures, and pelvic organs.","batch":3,"stem":"A preganglionic sympathetic fiber leaves the spinal cord and enters the sympathetic chain through a myelinated ramus present only at spinal levels T1 through L2, while a postganglionic fiber leaves the chain through an unmyelinated ramus present at every spinal level. Which two structures are being described, respectively?","choices":{"A":"The gray ramus communicans, carrying myelinated preganglionic fibers, and the white ramus communicans, carrying unmyelinated postganglionic fibers","B":"The dorsal root, carrying myelinated preganglionic fibers, and the ventral root, carrying unmyelinated postganglionic fibers","C":"The splanchnic nerve, carrying myelinated preganglionic fibers, and the gray ramus communicans, carrying unmyelinated postganglionic fibers","D":"The white ramus communicans, carrying myelinated preganglionic fibers, and the gray ramus communicans, carrying unmyelinated postganglionic fibers","E":"The white ramus communicans, carrying myelinated postganglionic fibers, and the gray ramus communicans, carrying unmyelinated preganglionic fibers"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The white ramus communicans carries myelinated preganglionic sympathetic fibers from the spinal nerve into the chain ganglion and is present only at T1 through L2, matching the intermediolateral cell column's segmental extent, while the gray ramus communicans carries unmyelinated postganglionic fibers back from the chain ganglion to the spinal nerve at every spinal level, since postganglionic fibers must reach vasculature and sweat glands throughout the entire body. Reversing which ramus is white versus gray misattributes their respective myelination and fiber types. The dorsal and ventral roots carry sensory and somatic motor fibers respectively, not this specific sympathetic ramus distinction. The splanchnic nerve carries preganglionic fibers that bypass the chain entirely to reach prevertebral ganglia, a different structure from the gray ramus. Reversing the myelination of the white ramus itself, describing it as carrying postganglionic fibers, is also incorrect, since the white ramus specifically carries preganglionic fibers.","boardPrep":"Step 1: white ramus, myelinated, preganglionic, T1-L2 only, versus gray ramus, unmyelinated, postganglionic, every level, is a classic anatomic pairing tested on autonomic anatomy exams. COMLEX: this same white-versus-gray ramus distinction is directly relevant to understanding paravertebral sympathetic block technique and its segmental limitations.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 → Obj 3 — sympathetic outflow tracing, tested here as a board-style ramus and splanchnic-nerve vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl46_bloom_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Trace sympathetic outflow from the intermediolateral cell column to chain ganglia, prevertebral ganglia, adrenal medulla, head/neck structures, and pelvic organs.","batch":3,"stem":"A surgeon dissecting the retroperitoneum identifies three separate thoracic splanchnic nerves, greater, lesser, and least, each terminating in a different prevertebral ganglion. Which pairing of splanchnic nerve to its corresponding ganglion is correct?","choices":{"A":"The greater splanchnic nerve terminates in the celiac ganglion, the lesser in the aorticorenal or superior mesenteric ganglion, and the least in the renal plexus","B":"The greater splanchnic nerve terminates in the renal plexus, the lesser in the celiac ganglion, and the least in the aorticorenal or superior mesenteric ganglion","C":"The greater splanchnic nerve terminates in the inferior mesenteric ganglion, the lesser in the renal plexus, and the least in the celiac ganglion","D":"All three splanchnic nerves terminate together in the single, shared inferior mesenteric ganglion regardless of their differing spinal levels of origin","E":"The greater splanchnic nerve terminates in the stellate ganglion, the lesser in the celiac ganglion, and the least in the aorticorenal ganglion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The greater thoracic splanchnic nerve, arising from roughly T5 through T9, terminates in the celiac ganglion, the lesser splanchnic nerve, from roughly T10 through T11, terminates in the aorticorenal or superior mesenteric ganglion, and the least splanchnic nerve, from T12, terminates in the renal plexus, each carrying preganglionic fibers that bypass the sympathetic chain entirely to synapse in these specific prevertebral targets. Scrambling these pairings, as in the other wrong choices, misassigns which splanchnic nerve reaches which structure. All three do not converge on a single shared inferior mesenteric ganglion, they terminate at three distinct prevertebral sites. The stellate ganglion is a paravertebral cervicothoracic structure, not a target of any thoracic splanchnic nerve.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the greater-lesser-least splanchnic nerve to celiac-aorticorenal-renal ganglion pairing is a detailed but testable extension of the prevertebral ganglion framework. COMLEX: this segmental organization underlies why splanchnic nerve blocks at different thoracic levels target different abdominal visceral pain territories.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 → Obj 3 — sympathetic outflow tracing, tested here as a board-style ramus and splanchnic-nerve vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl46_bloom_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Trace sympathetic outflow from the intermediolateral cell column to chain ganglia, prevertebral ganglia, adrenal medulla, head/neck structures, and pelvic organs.","batch":3,"stem":"Each sympathetic chain typically has 3 cervical, 10 to 11 thoracic, 3 to 5 lumbar, and 3 to 5 sacral ganglia, and the two chains meet caudally at the ganglion impar. Which ganglion typically forms from fusion of the inferior cervical ganglion with the first thoracic ganglion?","choices":{"A":"Superior cervical ganglion","B":"Stellate ganglion","C":"Celiac ganglion","D":"Ciliary ganglion","E":"Middle cervical ganglion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The inferior cervical ganglion and first thoracic ganglion typically fuse to form the stellate ganglion. The superior and middle cervical ganglia remain separate chain ganglia, the celiac ganglion is a prevertebral ganglion on the abdominal aorta, and the ciliary ganglion is a cranial parasympathetic ganglion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Paravertebral chain ganglia versus prevertebral ganglia (celiac, aorticorenal, superior and inferior mesenteric); white rami only at T1 to L2, gray rami at every spinal nerve. COMLEX: Compression near the stellate ganglion by an apical lung tumor can produce Horner syndrome.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 → Obj 7 — Horner syndrome localization along the sympathetic chain","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl46_bloom_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Trace craniosacral parasympathetic outflow through CN III, VII, IX, X, and S2-S4 pelvic splanchnic nerves.","batch":3,"stem":"Preganglionic parasympathetic fibers originating in the inferior salivatory nucleus travel with cranial nerve IX and, via the tympanic and lesser petrosal nerves, synapse in which ganglion to ultimately innervate the parotid gland?","choices":{"A":"The pterygopalatine ganglion, whose postganglionic fibers reach the parotid gland via the auriculotemporal nerve","B":"The submandibular ganglion, whose postganglionic fibers reach the parotid gland via the auriculotemporal nerve","C":"The ciliary ganglion, whose postganglionic fibers reach the parotid gland via the auriculotemporal nerve","D":"The celiac ganglion, whose postganglionic fibers reach the parotid gland via the auriculotemporal nerve","E":"The otic ganglion, whose postganglionic fibers reach the parotid gland via the auriculotemporal nerve"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The inferior salivatory nucleus sends preganglionic parasympathetic fibers with cranial nerve IX, which travel via the tympanic nerve and then the lesser petrosal nerve to synapse in the otic ganglion, whose postganglionic fibers hitch a ride on the auriculotemporal nerve, a branch of the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve, to reach and stimulate the parotid gland. The pterygopalatine and submandibular ganglia belong to cranial nerve VII's two separate parasympathetic routes, not cranial nerve IX. The ciliary ganglion belongs to cranial nerve III, and the celiac ganglion is a sympathetic prevertebral ganglion, neither is part of this glossopharyngeal parasympathetic pathway.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the complete inferior salivatory nucleus to tympanic/lesser petrosal nerve to otic ganglion to auriculotemporal nerve pathway is the fully traced route for parotid parasympathetic supply. COMLEX: Frey syndrome, gustatory sweating, results from aberrant regeneration of these same auriculotemporal-associated parasympathetic fibers after parotid gland injury.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 → Obj 4 — craniosacral parasympathetic outflow, tested here as a board-style branch-point and boundary vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl46_bloom_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Trace craniosacral parasympathetic outflow through CN III, VII, IX, X, and S2-S4 pelvic splanchnic nerves.","batch":3,"stem":"Several months after parotid gland surgery, a patient notices unexpected sweating and flushing over the cheek each time they eat, rather than the expected salivation. Which mechanism best explains this unusual gustatory sweating?","choices":{"A":"Normal, expected parasympathetic salivary function, since sweating and flushing over the cheek are a typical response to eating in healthy patients","B":"Aberrant regeneration of somatic motor facial nerve fibers, misdirecting a voluntary muscle contraction signal into a sudomotor gland response","C":"Compensatory sympathetic hyperactivity following parasympathetic gland loss, producing this regional sweating pattern through an unrelated mechanism","D":"A postganglionic sympathetic lesion of the superior cervical ganglion, producing cutaneous sweating and flushing in response to a meal","E":"Aberrant regeneration of severed parasympathetic salivary fibers along the pathway normally serving sweat glands, misdirecting a salivary signal into a sudomotor response"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Frey syndrome, gustatory sweating, results when parasympathetic fibers destined for the parotid gland are severed during surgery and, during regeneration, aberrantly reinnervate the cutaneous sweat glands and blood vessels that normally receive sympathetic cholinergic innervation, so a signal intended to trigger salivation instead triggers sweating and flushing whenever the patient eats. This gustatory sweating and flushing pattern is not a normal, expected eating response, it is a recognized surgical complication. The mechanism involves parasympathetic, not somatic motor facial nerve, fiber misdirection, and it is not simply compensatory sympathetic hyperactivity or an isolated postganglionic sympathetic lesion, it specifically reflects aberrant parasympathetic-to-sudomotor rewiring.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Frey syndrome as aberrant parasympathetic-to-sudomotor regeneration after parotid surgery is a classic, highly testable complication linking cranial nerve IX outflow to an unexpected clinical picture. COMLEX: recognizing this mechanism helps distinguish a benign, expected surgical sequela from a new, unrelated neurologic process.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 → Obj 4 — craniosacral parasympathetic outflow, tested here as a board-style branch-point and boundary vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl46_bloom_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Trace craniosacral parasympathetic outflow through CN III, VII, IX, X, and S2-S4 pelvic splanchnic nerves.","batch":3,"stem":"Postganglionic parasympathetic neurons for cranial nerves III, VII, and IX are grouped into discrete named ganglia. Where are the postganglionic neurons that receive vagal preganglionic fibers for the thoracic and abdominal viscera located?","choices":{"A":"In the nodose ganglion of the vagus nerve","B":"In the celiac and superior mesenteric ganglia","C":"In intramural ganglia within the organ walls","D":"In the paravertebral sympathetic chain ganglia","E":"In the dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus itself"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Vagal preganglionic fibers end on postganglionic neurons of intramural, or terminal, ganglia scattered in the plexuses and walls of thoracic and abdominal organs, where they intermingle with enteric neurons, so they are not aggregated into a discrete ganglion. The nodose ganglion holds vagal sensory cell bodies, the celiac and mesenteric ganglia are sympathetic prevertebral ganglia, the chain ganglia are sympathetic, and the dorsal motor nucleus contains the preganglionic cell bodies themselves.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Most vagal preganglionics arise in the dorsal motor nucleus; cardiac preganglionics arise in the nucleus ambiguus; all synapse in intramural ganglia. COMLEX: Sacral S2 to S4 outflow takes over from the vagus just proximal to the left colic flexure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 → Obj 3 — vagal visceral sensory input traced to the nodose ganglion","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl46_bloom_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the major brainstem autonomic nuclei and their functions.","batch":3,"stem":"When a person stands up, reduced baroreceptor firing releases pacemaker-like vasopressor neurons in the rostral ventrolateral medulla from solitary nucleus inhibition, increasing sympathetic outflow through the intermediolateral cell column. In which vascular beds does this response raise resistance?","choices":{"A":"Skeletal muscle and abdominal visceral beds","B":"Cerebral and coronary beds of the vital organs","C":"Cutaneous beds of the face and limbs","D":"Cerebral, coronary, and skin beds alike","E":"Pulmonary and renal medullary beds"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Release of RVLM vasopressor neurons increases cardiac output and resistance in the vascular beds of skeletal muscle and abdominal visceral organs, but not in the skin, heart, or brain, so cerebral and coronary flow are protected while blood is redirected. Some solitary input reaches the RVLM through vasodepressor cells of the caudal ventrolateral medulla. Pulmonary and renal medullary beds are not the described targets.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Standing: lower baroreceptor firing, less solitary excitation of vagal cardioinhibitory neurons, less solitary inhibition of RVLM, more sympathetic outflow. COMLEX: Failure of this reflex causes orthostatic hypotension and reflex syncope.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 → Obj 4 — the nucleus solitarius as the integrator of autonomic reflexes","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl46_bloom_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the major brainstem autonomic nuclei and their functions.","batch":3,"stem":"A pontine nucleus containing the brain's principal population of noradrenergic neurons projects broadly throughout the central nervous system to promote arousal and a stress-related activation state, distinct from the rostral ventrolateral medulla's more specific role in peripheral vasomotor tone. Which nucleus is being described?","choices":{"A":"The nucleus ambiguus, the principal noradrenergic nucleus contributing to arousal and central stress-response activation, distinct from peripheral vasomotor control","B":"The locus coeruleus, the principal noradrenergic nucleus contributing to arousal and central stress-response activation","C":"The dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus, the principal noradrenergic nucleus contributing to arousal and central stress-response activation, distinct from vasomotor control","D":"The solitary nucleus, the principal noradrenergic nucleus contributing to arousal and central stress-response activation, distinct from peripheral vasomotor control","E":"The area postrema, the principal noradrenergic nucleus contributing to arousal and central stress-response activation, distinct from peripheral vasomotor control"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The locus coeruleus, located in the pontine tegmentum, is the brain's principal source of noradrenergic neurons and projects widely throughout the cortex, limbic system, and brainstem to promote arousal, vigilance, and a broader central stress-activation state, functionally complementing rather than duplicating the rostral ventrolateral medulla's more specific descending role in peripheral sympathetic vasomotor tone. The nucleus ambiguus, dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus, solitary nucleus, and area postrema are each defined by distinct motor, sensory, or chemosensory autonomic functions rather than by serving as the brain's principal noradrenergic nucleus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the locus coeruleus as the brain's principal noradrenergic hub for arousal and stress response rounds out the brainstem autonomic nuclei list beyond the more classically emphasized vasomotor and visceral motor structures. COMLEX: locus coeruleus dysfunction is implicated in anxiety and stress-related conditions frequently referenced in behavioral medicine.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 → Obj 5 — the brainstem autonomic nuclei, tested here as a board-style additional-nucleus vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl46_bloom_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the major brainstem autonomic nuclei and their functions.","batch":3,"stem":"A student learns that the superior and inferior salivatory nuclei are two separate, paired brainstem nuclei rather than a single structure. Which distinction correctly separates their respective cranial nerve associations and targets?","choices":{"A":"The superior salivatory nucleus supplies cranial nerve IX's route to the otic ganglion, while the inferior salivatory nucleus supplies cranial nerve VII's route to the pterygopalatine and submandibular ganglia","B":"Both the superior and inferior salivatory nuclei supply the same cranial nerve VII route to the pterygopalatine and submandibular ganglia identically","C":"The superior salivatory nucleus supplies cranial nerve VII's route to the pterygopalatine and submandibular ganglia, while the inferior salivatory nucleus supplies cranial nerve IX's route to the otic ganglion","D":"The superior salivatory nucleus supplies cranial nerve III's route to the ciliary ganglion, while the inferior salivatory nucleus supplies cranial nerve VII's route to the pterygopalatine ganglion","E":"Both the superior and inferior salivatory nuclei are entirely nonfunctional vestigial structures with no genuine role in modern human salivary regulation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The superior salivatory nucleus provides preganglionic parasympathetic fibers to cranial nerve VII, reaching the pterygopalatine ganglion, lacrimal and nasal glands, and the submandibular ganglion, submandibular and sublingual glands, via its two separate branches, while the inferior salivatory nucleus provides preganglionic fibers to cranial nerve IX, reaching the otic ganglion and parotid gland, a clean division by cranial nerve of origin despite their similar names and adjacent locations. Reversing which nucleus supplies which cranial nerve misattributes this pairing. The two nuclei do not supply the identical cranial nerve VII pathway, that would eliminate the entire purpose of having two separate, distinctly named nuclei. Cranial nerve III's Edinger-Westphal nucleus is a separate structure entirely unrelated to either salivatory nucleus. Both salivatory nuclei are functionally active, not vestigial, structures essential to normal salivary and lacrimal regulation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: explicitly separating the superior salivatory nucleus, cranial nerve VII, from the inferior salivatory nucleus, cranial nerve IX, prevents a common naming-based confusion on exams. COMLEX: this distinction is clinically relevant when localizing a facial versus glossopharyngeal nerve lesion by its associated salivary and lacrimal deficits.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 → Obj 5 — the brainstem autonomic nuclei, tested here as a board-style additional-nucleus vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl46_bloom_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain how the hypothalamus coordinates autonomic, endocrine, behavioral, and emotional responses.","batch":3,"stem":"A hypothalamic nucleus promotes heat conservation and heat production through sympathetic activation, in contrast to the anterior hypothalamus, which promotes cooling through parasympathetic mechanisms. Which nucleus is this?","choices":{"A":"Lateral hypothalamic area","B":"Posterior hypothalamic nucleus","C":"Suprachiasmatic nucleus","D":"Ventromedial hypothalamic nucleus","E":"Paraventricular nucleus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The posterior hypothalamic nucleus controls heating and is associated with sympathetic activity, whereas the anterior nucleus mediates cooling and parasympathetic responses. The lateral hypothalamic area drives hunger, the suprachiasmatic nucleus sets circadian rhythm, the ventromedial nucleus mediates satiety, and the paraventricular nucleus synthesizes oxytocin and antidiuretic hormone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hypothalamic nuclei: anterior cooling, posterior heating, lateral hunger, ventromedial satiety, suprachiasmatic circadian rhythm, supraoptic and paraventricular ADH and oxytocin. COMLEX: The hypothalamus is the highest integrator of autonomic and endocrine function and projects directly to brainstem and spinal preganglionic neurons.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 4 — hypothalamic functions including temperature regulation","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl46_bloom_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain how the hypothalamus coordinates autonomic, endocrine, behavioral, and emotional responses.","batch":3,"stem":"A specific hypothalamic region functions as the body's thermostat, integrating input from central and peripheral thermoreceptors and coordinating autonomic responses such as sweating and cutaneous vasodilation when core temperature rises. Which region is being described?","choices":{"A":"The suprachiasmatic nucleus, the thermoregulatory center integrating thermoreceptor input with autonomic cooling responses","B":"The paraventricular nucleus, the thermoregulatory center integrating thermoreceptor input with autonomic cooling responses","C":"The preoptic area of the anterior hypothalamus, the thermoregulatory center integrating thermoreceptor input with autonomic cooling responses","D":"The lateral hypothalamic area, the thermoregulatory center integrating thermoreceptor input with autonomic cooling responses","E":"The mammillary bodies, the thermoregulatory center integrating thermoreceptor input with autonomic cooling responses"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The preoptic area of the anterior hypothalamus functions as the body's central thermostat, receiving input from central thermoreceptors and peripheral cutaneous thermoreceptors and triggering coordinated autonomic responses, sweating and vasodilation to promote heat loss when core temperature rises, or vasoconstriction and shivering when it falls, matching the impaired thermoregulation seen with hypothalamic lesions in this region. The suprachiasmatic nucleus governs circadian rhythm, not thermoregulation. The paraventricular nucleus contributes neuroendocrine and direct autonomic projections but is not the primary thermostat. The lateral hypothalamic area is associated with feeding behavior, and the mammillary bodies are associated with memory circuitry, neither serves this thermoregulatory role.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the preoptic anterior hypothalamus as the body's thermostat is a foundational fact explaining fever generation and heat-loss responses alike. COMLEX: this same region is the target of antipyretic mechanisms that reset the thermoregulatory set point during infection.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 → Obj 6 — hypothalamic coordination, tested here as a board-style named-nucleus vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl46_bloom_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain how the hypothalamus coordinates autonomic, endocrine, behavioral, and emotional responses.","batch":3,"stem":"A researcher creates two separate lesions in an experimental animal: one confined to the lateral hypothalamic area and a second, separate animal with a lesion confined to the ventromedial hypothalamic nucleus. Which pairing of resulting feeding behavior changes is correct?","choices":{"A":"The lateral hypothalamic lesion produces reduced feeding and weight loss, while the ventromedial lesion produces overeating and weight gain","B":"The lateral hypothalamic lesion produces overeating and weight gain, while the ventromedial lesion produces reduced feeding and weight loss instead in these two animals","C":"Both lesions produce an identical pattern of overeating and progressive weight gain regardless of which specific hypothalamic region was actually damaged","D":"Both lesions produce an identical pattern of reduced feeding and progressive weight loss regardless of which specific hypothalamic region was actually damaged","E":"Neither lesion produces any change in feeding behavior, since the hypothalamus has no meaningful role in appetite regulation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The lateral hypothalamic area functions as a feeding, or hunger, center, so its destruction produces reduced feeding and weight loss, while the ventromedial hypothalamic nucleus functions as a satiety center that normally signals fullness, so its destruction removes this braking signal and produces overeating and weight gain, a classic double dissociation in hypothalamic feeding regulation. Reversing which lesion produces which feeding pattern misattributes these opposing roles. The two lesions do not produce an identical pattern in either direction, that is precisely what makes this a useful double dissociation for testing hypothalamic functional localization. The hypothalamus clearly does have a well-established, major role in appetite and feeding regulation, contrary to the claim of no meaningful role.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lateral, hunger, versus ventromedial, satiety, hypothalamic double dissociation is a classic physiology experiment illustrating functional localization within a single integrative structure. COMLEX: this same framework underlies clinical hyperphagia syndromes seen with hypothalamic tumors or injury involving the ventromedial region.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 → Obj 6 — hypothalamic coordination, tested here as a board-style named-nucleus vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl46_bloom_q19","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Localize Horner syndrome, autonomic dysreflexia, neurogenic bladder, orthostatic hypotension, and common pupillary disorders.","batch":3,"stem":"A clinician evaluates a patient for suspected orthostatic hypotension by measuring blood pressure supine and then again after standing for three minutes. Which specific measured change meets the standard diagnostic threshold for this condition?","choices":{"A":"A drop in systolic blood pressure of at least 20 mm Hg, or in diastolic blood pressure of at least 10 mm Hg, within three minutes of standing","B":"A rise in systolic blood pressure of at least 20 mm Hg, or in diastolic blood pressure of at least 10 mm Hg, within three minutes of standing from a seated position","C":"A drop in heart rate alone of at least 20 beats per minute, regardless of any accompanying change in blood pressure, within three minutes of standing from a seated position","D":"A drop in systolic blood pressure of at least 5 mm Hg, or in diastolic blood pressure of at least 2 mm Hg, within three minutes of standing from a seated position","E":"Any measurable drop in blood pressure of any magnitude, regardless of degree, occurring at any time within the first hour after standing from a seated position"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Orthostatic hypotension is diagnostically defined as a sustained drop in systolic blood pressure of at least 20 mm Hg, or in diastolic blood pressure of at least 10 mm Hg, measured within three minutes of standing, a specific and reproducible threshold used to distinguish genuine orthostatic hypotension from normal blood pressure variability. A much smaller 5/2 mm Hg threshold would fail to distinguish pathologic drops from normal fluctuation. A rise in blood pressure describes the opposite, non-pathologic direction of change. A heart rate drop is not the diagnostic criterion, orthostatic hypotension is defined by the blood pressure change itself, and heart rate response instead helps distinguish neurogenic from non-neurogenic causes. Any measurable drop of any magnitude, without a defined threshold or time window, would be far too nonspecific to serve as a clinical diagnostic criterion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: knowing the exact 20 mm Hg systolic or 10 mm Hg diastolic threshold within three minutes of standing is the specific, testable diagnostic definition of orthostatic hypotension. COMLEX: this same bedside orthostatic vital sign measurement is a routine component of the osteopathic and general physical examination.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 → Obj 7 — the high-yield autonomic clinical patterns, tested here as a board-style diagnostic-criteria vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl46_bloom_q20","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Localize Horner syndrome, autonomic dysreflexia, neurogenic bladder, orthostatic hypotension, and common pupillary disorders.","batch":3,"stem":"Two patients both meet diagnostic criteria for orthostatic hypotension on standing. The first shows an appropriate compensatory rise in heart rate of 25 beats per minute, while the second shows almost no change in heart rate despite the same magnitude of blood pressure drop. Which underlying cause best distinguishes these two patients?","choices":{"A":"The first patient more likely has neurogenic orthostatic hypotension from impaired autonomic reflex function, while the second more likely has volume depletion with an intact baroreflex","B":"Both patients have an identical underlying mechanism, since the magnitude of the blood pressure drop alone is sufficient to establish the diagnosis","C":"The first patient more likely has volume depletion with an intact baroreflex, while the second more likely has neurogenic orthostatic hypotension from impaired autonomic reflex function","D":"The heart rate response carries no diagnostic significance in distinguishing between these two possible underlying mechanisms","E":"The first patient more likely has a structural cardiac arrhythmia, while the second more likely has volume depletion with an intact baroreflex response"},"correct":"C","explanation":"When the baroreflex arc remains intact, as in simple volume depletion, hemorrhage or dehydration, a falling blood pressure appropriately triggers a compensatory tachycardic response; when the baroreflex efferent limb itself is damaged, as in neurogenic causes such as autonomic neuropathy or multiple system atrophy, this compensatory heart rate rise is blunted or absent despite an equally severe blood pressure drop, making the heart rate response a key discriminating clue between these two categories. Reversing which pattern corresponds to which mechanism misattributes this key distinguishing feature. The magnitude of blood pressure drop alone is not sufficient to distinguish neurogenic from non-neurogenic causes, the heart rate response provides essential additional information. A structural cardiac arrhythmia is not the typical explanation for either of these two classic orthostatic hypotension patterns.","boardPrep":"Step 1: comparing compensatory heart rate response, appropriate rise favoring volume depletion versus blunted response favoring neurogenic disease, is the key bedside discriminator for orthostatic hypotension etiology. COMLEX: this same distinction guides whether fluid repletion or targeted autonomic-directed therapy is the more appropriate management approach.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 → Obj 7 — the high-yield autonomic clinical patterns, tested here as a board-style diagnostic-criteria vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl46_bloom_q21","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Localize Horner syndrome, autonomic dysreflexia, neurogenic bladder, orthostatic hypotension, and common pupillary disorders.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient experiences recurrent, severe episodes of unilateral periorbital pain lasting under two hours, each accompanied by ipsilateral tearing, nasal congestion, conjunctival injection, and a transient ipsilateral ptosis and miosis that resolve between episodes. Which mechanism best explains the episodic, reversible autonomic findings accompanying this pain syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Cluster headache produces a fixed, permanent structural lesion of the superior cervical ganglion, explaining why the ptosis and miosis persist between pain episodes as well","B":"Cluster headache produces a fixed, permanent structural lesion of the trigeminal nerve itself, explaining why the ptosis and miosis persist between pain episodes","C":"Cluster headache is unrelated to autonomic dysfunction, so the tearing and congestion must reflect a separate, coincidental process","D":"Cluster headache results from a fixed, permanent central brainstem infarct, explaining why the ptosis and miosis persist between pain episodes","E":"Cluster headache activates the trigeminal-autonomic reflex, producing transient parasympathetic overactivity plus reversible sympathetic dysfunction tied to each pain episode"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Cluster headache is a trigeminal autonomic cephalalgia in which trigeminal nerve activation reflexively engages the parasympathetic outflow via the pterygopalatine ganglion, producing lacrimation, nasal congestion, and conjunctival injection, while a transient, episode-linked disruption of sympathetic outflow produces reversible ptosis and miosis, a Horner-like picture that resolves between attacks rather than persisting, distinguishing this functional, episodic autonomic activation from a fixed structural lesion. A fixed, permanent lesion of the superior cervical ganglion or the trigeminal nerve itself would be expected to produce persistent, not episode-linked and reversible, findings, contradicting the pattern described. Cluster headache is very much associated with these characteristic autonomic features, they are a defining diagnostic component, not a coincidental, unrelated finding. A fixed permanent central brainstem infarct would similarly be expected to produce persistent, not reversible, autonomic findings.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cluster headache's trigeminal-autonomic reflex, combining parasympathetic overactivity with transient sympathetic dysfunction, is a classic, highly testable episodic autonomic pattern distinct from a fixed structural Horner syndrome. COMLEX: recognizing the episodic, reversible nature of these findings is key to distinguishing cluster headache from a structural cause of Horner syndrome requiring urgent imaging.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 → Obj 7 — the high-yield autonomic clinical patterns, tested here as a board-style diagnostic-criteria vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl46_bloom_q22","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Use clinical findings to distinguish preganglionic from postganglionic and central from peripheral autonomic lesions.","batch":3,"stem":"A clinician compares the distribution of anhidrosis across three patients with Horner syndrome: one with no facial anhidrosis (at most a small patch over the medial forehead), one with anhidrosis of the whole ipsilateral face, and one with anhidrosis of the entire ipsilateral side of the body. Which localization pattern correctly matches these three distributions?","choices":{"A":"Absent or forehead-only anhidrosis suggests a central lesion, whole-face anhidrosis a postganglionic lesion, and hemibody anhidrosis a preganglionic lesion","B":"Absent or forehead-only anhidrosis suggests a preganglionic lesion, whole-face anhidrosis a central lesion, and hemibody anhidrosis a postganglionic lesion","C":"All three distributions indicate the same postganglionic lesion level, since sweat gland distribution carries no localizing information","D":"Absent or forehead-only anhidrosis suggests a postganglionic lesion, whole-face anhidrosis a preganglionic lesion, and hemibody anhidrosis a central lesion","E":"Anhidrosis distribution depends on ambient temperature at the time of examination rather than on the level of the sympathetic lesion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Anhidrosis distribution helps localize Horner syndrome. A postganglionic lesion beyond the superior cervical ganglion, for example along the internal carotid artery, usually spares facial sweating, because the sudomotor fibers to the face leave with the external carotid artery; at most a small medial-forehead area supplied along the internal carotid route is affected. A preganglionic lesion interrupts the facial sudomotor fibers before they split at the carotid bifurcation, so the whole ipsilateral face is anhidrotic. A central lesion of the descending hypothalamospinal pathway can produce anhidrosis of the entire ipsilateral body. The scrambled pairings misassign these levels, the distribution does carry localizing information, and it reflects lesion level rather than ambient temperature.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Horner sweating rule: central lesion, hemibody anhidrosis; preganglionic lesion, whole-face anhidrosis; postganglionic lesion (carotid dissection), facial sweating usually spared. COMLEX: combining the sweating pattern with accompanying non-autonomic findings gives the most complete localization at the bedside.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 → Obj 8 — preganglionic/postganglionic and central/peripheral localization, tested here as a board-style multi-pattern synthesis vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl46_bloom_q23","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Use clinical findings to distinguish preganglionic from postganglionic and central from peripheral autonomic lesions.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient has a unilateral tonic pupil with light-near dissociation, absent deep tendon reflexes throughout, and patchy areas of diminished sweating over the trunk and limbs. Which diagnosis extends the isolated tonic pupil and areflexia pattern to include this additional sudomotor finding?","choices":{"A":"Holmes-Adie syndrome alone, combining a tonic pupil, generalized areflexia, and segmental anhidrosis from widespread postganglionic autonomic and sensory ganglion involvement","B":"Argyll Robertson pupil alone, combining a tonic pupil, generalized areflexia, and segmental anhidrosis from widespread postganglionic autonomic and sensory ganglion involvement","C":"Autoimmune autonomic ganglionopathy alone, combining a tonic pupil, generalized areflexia, and segmental anhidrosis from widespread postganglionic autonomic and sensory ganglion involvement","D":"Multiple system atrophy alone, combining a tonic pupil, generalized areflexia, and segmental anhidrosis from widespread postganglionic autonomic and sensory ganglion involvement","E":"Ross syndrome, combining a tonic pupil, generalized areflexia, and segmental anhidrosis from widespread postganglionic autonomic and sensory ganglion involvement"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ross syndrome extends the classic Holmes-Adie triad, tonic pupil plus diminished deep tendon reflexes, by adding segmental or progressive anhidrosis from more widespread postganglionic sudomotor and sensory ganglion involvement, making it a broader autonomic and sensory ganglionopathy than Holmes-Adie syndrome alone, which classically does not feature this prominent sweating abnormality as a defining component. Argyll Robertson pupil describes small, not tonic, pupils with light-near dissociation from tabes dorsalis and does not include this areflexia-plus-anhidrosis triad. Autoimmune autonomic ganglionopathy produces diffuse, typically more acute and severe autonomic failure affecting cardiovascular, pupillary, and bladder function broadly, rather than this specific, often more indolent tonic pupil, areflexia, and segmental anhidrosis presentation. Multiple system atrophy features orthostatic hypotension combined with parkinsonism and cerebellar signs, an entirely different combination of central, not this peripheral ganglionic, findings.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ross syndrome as an extension of Holmes-Adie syndrome, adding segmental anhidrosis, rounds out the family of tonic pupil-associated autonomic syndromes. COMLEX: recognizing this extended triad helps avoid mistaking a benign, chronic ganglionopathy for a more urgent, rapidly progressive autonomic failure syndrome.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 46 → Obj 8 — preganglionic/postganglionic and central/peripheral localization, tested here as a board-style multi-pattern synthesis vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl46_bloom_q24","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Use clinical findings to distinguish preganglionic from postganglionic and central from peripheral autonomic lesions.","batch":3,"stem":"A 24-year-old man has a complete spinal cord transection at C6 after a diving injury. In the first days after injury, before reflex activity returns, which autonomic picture is expected?","choices":{"A":"Severe hypertension with reflex bradycardia","B":"Low blood pressure with a slow heart rate","C":"Profuse sweating with piloerection below the lesion","D":"Unilateral Horner syndrome with facial anhidrosis","E":"Tachycardia from unopposed sympathetic outflow"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Acute injury at T6 or above interrupts descending reticulospinal and hypothalamospinal fibers that drive preganglionic sympathetic neurons, so the early picture is reduced sympathetic activity with low blood pressure, orthostatic hypotension, and bradycardia. Hypertension with reflex bradycardia, profuse sweating, and piloerection describe autonomic dysreflexia, which develops later from denervation hypersensitivity. A complete cervical transection affects sympathetic outflow bilaterally rather than causing a unilateral Horner pattern, and sympathetic outflow is reduced rather than unopposed.","boardPrep":"Step 1: High cord injury: early sympathetic hypofunction (hypotension, bradycardia), later autonomic dysreflexia triggered by noxious stimuli below the lesion. COMLEX: In dysreflexia, sit the patient up and remove the trigger, such as a blocked catheter.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 4 — spinal cord lesion patterns and the levels they affect","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"}],"47":[{"id":"sdl47_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the major epidemiologic patterns of primary and metastatic intracranial tumors, including typical age groups and anatomic locations.","batch":1,"stem":"Which statement best summarizes the anatomic distribution of intracranial tumors across the two major age groups in which they occur?","choices":{"A":"Roughly 70 percent of childhood CNS tumors arise above the tentorium, while a comparable proportion of adult tumors arise in the posterior fossa","B":"Both childhood and adult CNS tumors arise above the tentorium in roughly 70 percent of cases, with no meaningful age-related difference","C":"Both childhood and adult CNS tumors arise in the posterior fossa in roughly 70 percent of cases, with no meaningful age-related difference","D":"Roughly 70 percent of childhood CNS tumors arise in the posterior fossa, while a comparable proportion of adult tumors arise above the tentorium","E":"Childhood tumors show no consistent compartmental predilection, while adult tumors arise almost exclusively in the posterior fossa"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Roughly 70 percent of childhood CNS tumors arise in the posterior fossa, reflecting the predominance of pilocytic astrocytoma, medulloblastoma, and ependymoma in this age group, while a comparable proportion of adult tumors arise above the tentorium in the cerebral hemispheres, reflecting the predominance of gliomas, meningiomas, and metastases there. Reversing this pattern misstates the actual age-related compartmental predilection. Claiming both groups favor the supratentorial compartment equally ignores the well-established pediatric posterior fossa predominance. Claiming both groups favor the posterior fossa equally ignores the well-established adult supratentorial predominance. Childhood tumors do show a strong, consistent posterior fossa predilection rather than no predilection, and adult tumors are not confined almost exclusively to the posterior fossa.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pediatric posterior fossa versus adult supratentorial tumor distribution is one of the most reliably tested epidemiologic facts in neuro-oncology. COMLEX: this compartmental pattern directly predicts which specific tumor types belong on the differential for a pediatric versus an adult patient with a new intracranial mass.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 8 — telencephalic lesion-localization skills already built for aphasia, neglect, and seizure semiology, now extended to matching tumor type against age and anatomic location","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the major epidemiologic patterns of primary and metastatic intracranial tumors, including typical age groups and anatomic locations.","batch":1,"stem":"Which tumor type is the single most common primary intracranial neoplasm overall, though not the most common primary malignancy?","choices":{"A":"Glioblastoma, an astrocytic tumor","B":"Oligodendroglioma, a glial tumor","C":"Medulloblastoma, an embryonal tumor","D":"Primary CNS lymphoma, a B-cell tumor","E":"Meningioma, a meningothelial tumor"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Meningioma is the single most common primary intracranial neoplasm overall, arising from meningothelial cells of the arachnoid, but because the great majority of meningiomas are CNS WHO grade 1 and behave in a benign fashion, glioblastoma remains the most common primary intracranial malignancy specifically. Glioblastoma is the most common primary malignant tumor but not the most common primary tumor overall once benign meningiomas are counted. Oligodendroglioma constitutes only a small minority of gliomas and is far less common than meningioma. Medulloblastoma is largely restricted to the pediatric posterior fossa and is far less common overall than meningioma. Primary CNS lymphoma accounts for only about one percent of intracranial tumors, well below meningioma's share.","boardPrep":"Step 1: meningioma as the most common primary intracranial tumor overall, versus glioblastoma as the most common primary intracranial malignancy, is a frequently confused pairing worth memorizing precisely. COMLEX: most meningiomas are incidental findings on imaging obtained for unrelated reasons, given their typically indolent, benign behavior.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 8 — telencephalic lesion-localization skills already built for aphasia, neglect, and seizure semiology, now extended to matching tumor type against age and anatomic location","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the major epidemiologic patterns of primary and metastatic intracranial tumors, including typical age groups and anatomic locations.","batch":1,"stem":"Metastatic lesions account for what proportion of intracranial tumors among hospitalized patients, and which five primary sites account for roughly 80 percent of these metastases combined?","choices":{"A":"Approximately one-quarter to one-half, from lung, prostate, thyroid, bone, and gastrointestinal tract","B":"Approximately one-quarter to one-half, from lung, breast, skin melanoma, kidney, and gastrointestinal tract","C":"Less than five percent, from lung, breast, skin melanoma, kidney, and gastrointestinal tract","D":"Approximately one-quarter to one-half, from lung, breast, ovary, liver, and pancreas","E":"Nearly all intracranial tumors in hospitalized patients, from lung, breast, skin melanoma, kidney, and gastrointestinal tract"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Metastatic lesions, mostly carcinomas, account for approximately one-quarter to one-half of intracranial tumors among hospitalized patients, with lung, breast, skin melanoma, kidney, and gastrointestinal tract together accounting for roughly 80 percent of these metastases, while tumors such as prostatic adenocarcinoma almost never metastasize to the brain despite being common systemically. Substituting prostate, thyroid, and bone for the correct primary sites misstates which cancers actually favor CNS spread. Describing the overall proportion as less than five percent drastically understates how common metastatic disease is among intracranial tumors in this population. Substituting ovary, liver, and pancreas likewise misstates the actual top five primary sites. Describing metastases as nearly all intracranial tumors overstates their share, since primary CNS tumors still constitute a substantial proportion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: lung, breast, skin melanoma, kidney, and gastrointestinal tract as the top five sources of brain metastases, together with prostate cancer's notable near-absence from this list, is a classic epidemiologic fact pairing. COMLEX: a solitary brain lesion in a patient with a known primary malignancy should still prompt consideration of a second, unrelated primary process such as an abscess or a separate primary CNS tumor before assuming metastasis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 8 — telencephalic lesion-localization skills already built for aphasia, neglect, and seizure semiology, now extended to matching tumor type against age and anatomic location","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how intracranial tumors cause increased intracranial pressure, seizures, personality change, hydrocephalus, and focal neurologic deficits.","batch":1,"stem":"Which mechanism best distinguishes vasogenic cerebral edema from cytotoxic cerebral edema in a patient with a large intracranial tumor?","choices":{"A":"Vasogenic edema results from cell membrane injury allowing fluid to accumulate intracellularly, while cytotoxic edema results from blood-brain barrier disruption allowing fluid to shift into the extracellular space","B":"Vasogenic edema results from overproduction of cerebrospinal fluid by the choroid plexus, while cytotoxic edema results from impaired cerebrospinal fluid resorption at the arachnoid granulations","C":"Vasogenic edema results from obstruction of the fourth ventricle outflow, while cytotoxic edema results from obstruction of the cerebral aqueduct","D":"Vasogenic edema and cytotoxic edema both result from the identical mechanism of blood-brain barrier disruption, differing only in their anatomic distribution within the brain","E":"Vasogenic edema results from blood-brain barrier disruption allowing fluid to shift into the extracellular space, while cytotoxic edema results from cell membrane injury allowing fluid to accumulate intracellularly"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Vasogenic edema arises from disruption of the blood-brain barrier and increased vascular permeability, allowing fluid to shift from the intravascular compartment into the extracellular spaces of the brain, classically surrounding a tumor or area of inflammation, while cytotoxic edema arises from injury to neuronal, glial, or endothelial cell membranes, allowing fluid to accumulate intracellularly, as occurs with hypoxic-ischemic injury. Reversing these two mechanisms misattributes the defining pathophysiology of each edema type. Both mechanisms describe hydrocephalus, not cerebral edema, and are not the correct explanations for either vasogenic or cytotoxic edema. Both mechanisms describe obstructive hydrocephalus at specific ventricular sites, not the fluid-shift mechanisms that define cerebral edema. Vasogenic and cytotoxic edema are mechanistically distinct processes, not the same mechanism differing only by location.","boardPrep":"Step 1: distinguishing vasogenic edema, from blood-brain barrier breakdown around a mass lesion, from cytotoxic edema, from cellular membrane failure after hypoxic-ischemic injury, is a frequently tested mechanism pairing. COMLEX: corticosteroids reduce vasogenic edema by stabilizing the blood-brain barrier but have little effect on cytotoxic edema, a clinically important treatment distinction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 8 — the uncal herniation and CN III compression mechanism already introduced there, now generalized to the full spectrum of mass-effect complications tumors produce","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how intracranial tumors cause increased intracranial pressure, seizures, personality change, hydrocephalus, and focal neurologic deficits.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with a rapidly expanding temporal lobe mass develops ipsilateral pupillary dilation, and imaging later shows an infarct in the ipsilateral posterior cerebral artery territory. Which herniation pattern best explains both findings together?","choices":{"A":"Subfalcine herniation, in which the cingulate gyrus compresses the oculomotor nerve and the ipsilateral posterior cerebral artery against the falx","B":"Transtentorial herniation, in which the medial temporal lobe compresses the oculomotor nerve and the ipsilateral posterior cerebral artery against the tentorial edge","C":"Tonsillar herniation, in which the cerebellar tonsils compress the oculomotor nerve and the ipsilateral posterior cerebral artery against the foramen magnum","D":"Transtentorial herniation, in which the medial temporal lobe compresses the abducens nerve and the ipsilateral middle cerebral artery against the tentorial edge","E":"Subfalcine herniation, in which the cingulate gyrus compresses the anterior cerebral artery bilaterally against the falx without any oculomotor involvement"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Transtentorial, or uncal, herniation occurs when the medial temporal lobe is compressed against the free edge of the tentorium, compromising the oculomotor nerve to produce ipsilateral pupillary dilation and impaired ocular movement, while the ipsilateral posterior cerebral artery is compressed against the same tentorial edge, producing an infarct in its territory that manifests as contralateral homonymous hemianopia. Subfalcine herniation displaces the cingulate gyrus under the falx and characteristically compresses the anterior, not posterior, cerebral artery, and it does not produce oculomotor nerve compression. Tonsillar herniation displaces the cerebellar tonsils through the foramen magnum and threatens the brainstem's respiratory and cardiac centers rather than the oculomotor nerve or posterior cerebral artery. Attributing this presentation to the abducens nerve and middle cerebral artery misidentifies both the nerve and the vessel actually compressed in transtentorial herniation. Subfalcine herniation classically compresses the anterior cerebral artery bilaterally in severe cases, but this alone does not explain the oculomotor and posterior cerebral artery findings described here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: uncal herniation's ipsilateral blown pupil from oculomotor compression, paired with an ipsilateral posterior cerebral artery infarct producing contralateral homonymous hemianopia, is a classic single-vignette double-finding pattern. COMLEX: the Kernohan notch phenomenon, in which the contralateral cerebral peduncle is compressed against the tentorium producing a false-localizing hemiparesis on the same side as the herniation, is an important related pitfall to recognize.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 8 — the uncal herniation and CN III compression mechanism already introduced there, now generalized to the full spectrum of mass-effect complications tumors produce","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how intracranial tumors cause increased intracranial pressure, seizures, personality change, hydrocephalus, and focal neurologic deficits.","batch":1,"stem":"A mass in the posterior fossa focally obstructs the fourth ventricle. Which type of hydrocephalus results, and how does this differ from the type caused by arachnoid fibrosis following meningitis?","choices":{"A":"Noncommunicating hydrocephalus from focal obstruction, in contrast to communicating hydrocephalus from impaired resorption despite continuity between the ventricles and subarachnoid space","B":"Communicating hydrocephalus from focal obstruction, in contrast to noncommunicating hydrocephalus from impaired resorption despite continuity between the ventricles and subarachnoid space","C":"Hydrocephalus ex vacuo from focal obstruction, in contrast to noncommunicating hydrocephalus from impaired resorption despite continuity between the ventricles and subarachnoid space","D":"Noncommunicating hydrocephalus from focal obstruction, in contrast to hydrocephalus ex vacuo from a compensatory increase in ventricular volume after brain parenchymal loss","E":"Both mechanisms produce identical communicating hydrocephalus, since either process ultimately increases the total volume of cerebrospinal fluid within the ventricular system"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A posterior fossa mass focally obstructing the fourth ventricle produces noncommunicating, or obstructive, hydrocephalus, since cerebrospinal fluid flow is blocked at a discrete point, whereas arachnoid fibrosis following meningitis impairs resorption at the arachnoid granulations while the ventricular system remains in continuity with the subarachnoid space, producing communicating hydrocephalus with enlargement of the entire ventricular system. Reversing these two labels misattributes which mechanism corresponds to focal obstruction versus impaired resorption with preserved continuity. Hydrocephalus ex vacuo refers specifically to a compensatory increase in ventricular volume following loss of brain parenchyma, not focal CSF flow obstruction by a mass, so it does not fit this scenario. While the second half of that comparison correctly names hydrocephalus ex vacuo, arachnoid fibrosis after meningitis is a communicating, not ex vacuo, process, since it involves impaired resorption rather than parenchymal volume loss. These two mechanisms are not identical, since one involves discrete flow obstruction and the other involves impaired resorption with preserved ventricular continuity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: distinguishing noncommunicating hydrocephalus, from focal obstruction such as a posterior fossa mass or aqueductal stenosis, from communicating hydrocephalus, from impaired resorption such as post-meningitic arachnoid fibrosis, is a classic mechanism-based pairing. COMLEX: hydrocephalus developing before cranial suture closure produces head enlargement, whereas the same process after suture closure instead raises intracranial pressure without a head circumference change.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 8 — the uncal herniation and CN III compression mechanism already introduced there, now generalized to the full spectrum of mass-effect complications tumors produce","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate metastatic tumors from primary CNS tumors using lesion number, compartment, location, imaging pattern, and clinical history.","batch":1,"stem":"MRI of the brain shows three well-circumscribed, ring-enhancing lesions, each located precisely at the junction of gray and white matter and each surrounded by a disproportionately large zone of vasogenic edema. Which category of intracranial tumor does this imaging pattern most strongly suggest?","choices":{"A":"Diffuse astrocytoma, since multiple sharply demarcated lesions at the gray-white junction with disproportionate edema is the classic infiltrative glioma pattern","B":"Oligodendroglioma, since multiple sharply demarcated lesions at the gray-white junction with disproportionate edema is the classic calcified low-grade glioma pattern","C":"Metastatic tumor, since multiple sharply demarcated lesions at the gray-white junction with disproportionate edema is the classic metastatic pattern","D":"Meningioma, since multiple sharply demarcated lesions at the gray-white junction with disproportionate edema is the classic dural-based tumor pattern","E":"Primary CNS lymphoma, since multiple sharply demarcated lesions at the gray-white junction with disproportionate edema is the classic periventricular pattern"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Intraparenchymal metastases characteristically form multiple, sharply demarcated masses at the junction of gray and white matter, where the smaller caliber of penetrating vessels favors tumor embolus lodging, and are typically surrounded by a disproportionately large zone of vasogenic edema relative to the size of the lesion itself, matching this imaging description precisely. Diffuse astrocytomas are infiltrative and poorly demarcated rather than sharply circumscribed, and they are not classically described as arising specifically and repeatedly at the gray-white junction as multiple discrete masses. Oligodendrogliomas are also diffusely infiltrating low-grade gliomas of the cerebral hemispheres with a predilection for cortex and superficial white matter, not multiple sharply marginated gray-white junction lesions. Meningiomas are dural-based extra-axial masses that compress rather than infiltrate the brain, and they do not characteristically appear as multiple intraparenchymal gray-white junction lesions. Primary CNS lymphoma classically favors deep periventricular white matter rather than the gray-white junction, and its margins are typically less sharply demarcated than this metastatic pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: multiple, sharply demarcated, ring-enhancing lesions at the gray-white junction with prominent surrounding edema is the single highest-yield imaging pattern for brain metastases. COMLEX: this same imaging pattern in a patient without a known primary malignancy should still prompt a thorough search for an occult systemic cancer, most often lung or breast.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 34 → Obj 8 — the papilledema and mass-lesion clinical correlation skill already built there, now applied to distinguishing metastatic from primary CNS tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate metastatic tumors from primary CNS tumors using lesion number, compartment, location, imaging pattern, and clinical history.","batch":1,"stem":"A single, diffusely infiltrating, nonenhancing intracranial mass expands the white matter without a sharply defined margin and without any accompanying lesions elsewhere. Which category of intracranial tumor does this pattern most strongly suggest, and why?","choices":{"A":"A metastatic tumor, since infiltrative growth without a sharp margin reflects direct parenchymal invasion rather than embolic seeding from a distant site","B":"A meningioma, since infiltrative growth without a sharp margin reflects direct parenchymal invasion rather than embolic seeding from a distant site","C":"A choroid plexus papilloma, since infiltrative growth without a sharp margin reflects direct parenchymal invasion rather than embolic seeding from a distant site","D":"A primary diffuse glioma, since infiltrative growth without a sharp margin reflects direct parenchymal invasion rather than embolic seeding from a distant site","E":"A vestibular schwannoma, since infiltrative growth without a sharp margin reflects direct parenchymal invasion rather than embolic seeding from a distant site"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A single, diffusely infiltrating mass that expands the involved white matter without a sharply defined margin reflects direct, contiguous invasion of adjacent brain parenchyma by tumor cells originating from within the CNS itself, the defining growth pattern of a primary diffuse glioma, in contrast to the sharply marginated, multiple, embolic lesions typical of metastatic spread. Metastatic tumors instead characteristically form sharply demarcated, often multiple lesions from embolic seeding, the opposite growth pattern from what is described here. Meningiomas are extra-axial dural-based masses that compress but do not infiltrate the underlying brain parenchyma, so they would not produce this diffuse intraparenchymal infiltrative pattern. Choroid plexus papillomas are intraventricular tumors that recapitulate normal choroid plexus architecture and do not infiltrate surrounding brain parenchyma in this manner. Vestibular schwannomas arise from Schwann cells of the vestibular portion of cranial nerve VIII within the cerebellopontine angle and do not infiltrate cerebral white matter at all.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a single, infiltrative, poorly marginated white matter mass without a sharp border is the classic imaging signature that favors a primary diffuse glioma over metastatic disease. COMLEX: young patients presenting with new seizures and this specific infiltrative, nonenhancing pattern should raise suspicion for a lower-grade diffuse glioma rather than glioblastoma or metastasis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 34 → Obj 8 — the papilledema and mass-lesion clinical correlation skill already built there, now applied to distinguishing metastatic from primary CNS tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate metastatic tumors from primary CNS tumors using lesion number, compartment, location, imaging pattern, and clinical history.","batch":1,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman with a history of resected stage II breast cancer five years ago presents with a new headache, and MRI shows a solitary enhancing brain lesion. Which single piece of clinical history most strongly shifts the differential toward metastasis rather than a new primary CNS tumor?","choices":{"A":"Her remote history of breast cancer, a tumor type known to metastasize to the brain, even though it was previously treated and considered resected","B":"Her age of 61 years alone, independent of any prior cancer history, since this age is more typical of metastasis than of any primary CNS tumor","C":"The fact that the lesion enhances with contrast, since enhancement itself is a finding specific to metastatic rather than primary tumors","D":"The presence of a headache as her presenting symptom, since headache is a finding specific to metastatic rather than primary tumors","E":"Her female sex alone, independent of any prior cancer history, since metastatic tumors are known to occur far more often in women than in men"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A remote history of a cancer type known to metastasize to the brain, such as breast cancer, is the single most clinically useful piece of history for shifting the differential toward metastasis, since brain metastases can present years after apparently successful treatment of the primary tumor, and breast cancer is among the five leading sources of brain metastases. Age alone, without regard to cancer history, does not reliably distinguish metastasis from primary CNS tumors, since both glioblastoma and primary CNS lymphoma also increase in incidence with advancing age. Contrast enhancement is a nonspecific imaging finding seen in high-grade primary gliomas as well as metastases, so it does not by itself favor one category over the other. Headache is likewise a nonspecific presenting symptom common to essentially all mass lesions raising intracranial pressure, not a finding specific to metastatic disease. Female sex alone is not a recognized risk factor that specifically favors metastatic over primary CNS tumors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a known history of a metastasis-prone primary malignancy, even years in the past, is the single most powerful clue favoring brain metastasis over a new primary CNS tumor. COMLEX: brain metastases can occasionally be the first clinical manifestation of an undiagnosed systemic malignancy, so a negative cancer history does not fully exclude metastatic disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 34 → Obj 8 — the papilledema and mass-lesion clinical correlation skill already built there, now applied to distinguishing metastatic from primary CNS tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Compare the molecular and pathologic features of astrocytoma, oligodendroglioma, and glioblastoma using modern integrated diagnostic terminology.","batch":1,"stem":"Under the 2021 WHO classification, which single molecular feature is now used to separate glioblastoma from astrocytoma as two distinct diffuse glioma entities, rather than viewing them as points along one continuous grading spectrum?","choices":{"A":"ATRX mutational status, with glioblastoma defined as ATRX-mutant and astrocytoma defined as ATRX-wildtype","B":"MGMT methylation status, with glioblastoma defined as unmethylated and astrocytoma defined as methylated","C":"IDH1 or IDH2 mutational status, with glioblastoma defined as IDH-wildtype and astrocytoma defined as IDH-mutant","D":"1p and 19q codeletion status, with glioblastoma defined as codeleted and astrocytoma defined as non-codeleted","E":"TP53 mutational status, with glioblastoma defined as TP53-mutant and astrocytoma defined as TP53-wildtype"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The 2021 WHO classification uses IDH1 or IDH2 mutational status as the defining criterion separating these two entities, glioblastoma being IDH-wildtype by definition and astrocytoma being IDH-mutant, reflecting genuinely distinct genetic origins and clinical behaviors rather than a single continuous spectrum of increasing grade. ATRX mutation is instead a supporting feature seen alongside IDH mutation and TP53 mutation in IDH-mutant astrocytomas, not the primary criterion that separates the two tumor types from each other. MGMT promoter methylation predicts responsiveness to alkylating chemotherapy within glioblastoma but is not the defining criterion distinguishing glioblastoma from astrocytoma. 1p and 19q codeletion is the defining molecular feature of oligodendroglioma, a separate tumor type, not the criterion separating glioblastoma from astrocytoma. TP53 mutation is a common feature of IDH-mutant astrocytomas, similar to ATRX, but it is a supporting rather than the primary defining criterion for this specific distinction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IDH mutational status as the single defining criterion separating glioblastoma, IDH-wildtype, from astrocytoma, IDH-mutant, is the cornerstone concept of the modern integrated diffuse glioma classification. COMLEX: an IDH-mutant tumor with high-grade histologic features still carries a substantially better prognosis than an IDH-wildtype glioblastoma, an important point when counseling patients on expected outcomes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — telencephalic white matter fiber classification already built there, now the anatomic substrate where diffuse gliomas characteristically arise and infiltrate","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Compare the molecular and pathologic features of astrocytoma, oligodendroglioma, and glioblastoma using modern integrated diagnostic terminology.","batch":1,"stem":"Which combination of molecular alterations is diagnostic for oligodendroglioma under the current integrated classification scheme?","choices":{"A":"IDH1 or IDH2 mutation combined with EGFR gene amplification and loss of chromosome 10","B":"IDH1 or IDH2 mutation combined with whole-arm codeletion of chromosomes 1p and 19q","C":"ATRX inactivation combined with TP53 mutation, without any requirement for IDH mutation","D":"TERT promoter mutation combined with homozygous CDKN2A deletion, without any requirement for IDH mutation","E":"Combined gain of chromosome 7 with loss of chromosome 10, without any requirement for IDH mutation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Oligodendroglioma is molecularly defined by the combination of an IDH1 or IDH2 mutation together with whole-arm codeletion of chromosomes 1p and 19q, a genetic signature that is essentially exclusive to this tumor type among diffuse gliomas. EGFR amplification combined with chromosome 10 loss is instead a genetic signature characteristic of glioblastoma, not oligodendroglioma, and EGFR amplification is specifically not seen in oligodendrogliomas. ATRX inactivation together with TP53 mutation, without IDH mutation, describes neither oligodendroglioma nor IDH-mutant astrocytoma correctly, since ATRX and TP53 alterations in astrocytoma occur alongside, not instead of, IDH mutation. TERT promoter mutation and CDKN2A deletion without any IDH requirement describes neither the oligodendroglioma-defining signature nor a complete glioblastoma signature on their own. Combined chromosome 7 gain with chromosome 10 loss is one of the three defining genetic alterations of IDH-wildtype glioblastoma, not oligodendroglioma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IDH mutation plus 1p/19q codeletion as the required, essentially pathognomonic combination for oligodendroglioma is a heavily tested molecular diagnostic pairing. COMLEX: oligodendrogliomas have the best prognosis among diffuse gliomas when corrected for grade, a clinically relevant point that follows directly from this favorable molecular signature.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — telencephalic white matter fiber classification already built there, now the anatomic substrate where diffuse gliomas characteristically arise and infiltrate","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Compare the molecular and pathologic features of astrocytoma, oligodendroglioma, and glioblastoma using modern integrated diagnostic terminology.","batch":1,"stem":"Which three genetic alterations, any one of which is sufficient, allow a diffuse astrocytic tumor lacking classic high-grade histologic features to still be classified as glioblastoma under the 2021 WHO criteria?","choices":{"A":"Combined gain of chromosome 7 with loss of chromosome 10, TERT promoter mutation, or EGFR gene amplification, in an IDH-wildtype tumor","B":"Combined gain of chromosome 7 with loss of chromosome 10, TERT promoter mutation, or EGFR gene amplification, in an IDH-mutant tumor","C":"1p and 19q codeletion, ATRX inactivation, or TP53 mutation, in an IDH-wildtype tumor rather than an IDH-mutant one","D":"MGMT promoter methylation, ATRX inactivation, or homozygous CDKN2A deletion, in an IDH-wildtype tumor without any other requirement","E":"Combined gain of chromosome 7 with loss of chromosome 10, TERT promoter mutation, or EGFR gene amplification, regardless of IDH status"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Under the 2021 WHO criteria, an IDH-wildtype diffuse astrocytic tumor qualifies for a glioblastoma designation if it harbors any one of three key genetic alterations, combined whole-chromosome gain of 7 with loss of chromosome 10, TERT promoter mutation, or EGFR gene amplification, even in the absence of the classic histologic features of necrosis or microvascular proliferation. Requiring an IDH-mutant background is incorrect, since glioblastoma is specifically IDH-wildtype by definition; an IDH-mutant tumor with these same genetic features would instead be classified as a high-grade IDH-mutant astrocytoma. 1p/19q codeletion, ATRX inactivation, and TP53 mutation are features associated with oligodendroglioma and IDH-mutant astrocytoma respectively, not the molecular criteria that define glioblastoma in an IDH-wildtype tumor. MGMT methylation is a treatment-response predictor rather than a diagnostic criterion, and CDKN2A deletion defines CNS WHO grade 4 IDH-mutant astrocytoma, not glioblastoma specifically. Requiring these features regardless of IDH status is incorrect, since the entire molecular glioblastoma designation is contingent on the tumor first being IDH-wildtype.","boardPrep":"Step 1: knowing that any single one of chromosome 7 gain with chromosome 10 loss, TERT promoter mutation, or EGFR amplification is sufficient to molecularly diagnose glioblastoma in an IDH-wildtype tumor, even without necrosis or microvascular proliferation on histology, reflects the shift toward molecular-first CNS tumor diagnosis. COMLEX: this molecular approach means a small, non-representative biopsy that lacks classic high-grade histology can still be correctly diagnosed as glioblastoma if genetic testing identifies one of these alterations.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — telencephalic white matter fiber classification already built there, now the anatomic substrate where diffuse gliomas characteristically arise and infiltrate","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the major epidemiologic patterns of primary and metastatic intracranial tumors, including typical age groups and anatomic locations.","batch":2,"stem":"64-year-old man -> two-week history of progressively worsening headache and word-finding difficulty -> wife reports increasing personality change and forgetfulness -> exam shows mild expressive aphasia and no other focal deficit -> MRI shows a large, irregular, ring-enhancing mass with necrosis and surrounding edema centered in the left frontotemporal white matter. Which epidemiologic pattern best fits this presentation, and what is the single most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Pediatric posterior fossa location with necrosis in a patient over 55, most consistent with medulloblastoma","B":"Adult supratentorial location without necrosis in a patient over 55, most consistent with a low-grade oligodendroglioma","C":"Adult supratentorial location with necrosis in a patient over 55, most consistent with glioblastoma","D":"Pediatric posterior fossa location with necrosis in a patient over 55, most consistent with pilocytic astrocytoma","E":"Adult supratentorial location with necrosis in a patient over 55, most consistent with a solitary brain metastasis of unknown origin"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This presentation, an adult over 55 with a supratentorial, necrotic, ring-enhancing mass and progressive cognitive and personality change, matches the classic epidemiologic and imaging profile of glioblastoma, which is encountered mostly in the cerebral hemispheres of adults older than 55 and accounts for roughly 14 percent of all primary CNS tumors. A pediatric posterior fossa pattern with medulloblastoma does not fit either this patient's age, over 55 rather than a child, or the supratentorial frontotemporal location described. Describing the lesion as supratentorial without necrosis contradicts the vignette, which explicitly describes necrosis, and a low-grade oligodendroglioma would not typically present with this degree of necrosis or rapid two-week progression. A pediatric posterior fossa pattern with pilocytic astrocytoma again mismatches both this patient's age and the supratentorial location. While a solitary metastasis remains a reasonable differential consideration for any ring-enhancing mass, the vignette gives no history of a known primary malignancy, making glioblastoma the single most likely diagnosis given the epidemiologic and imaging pattern alone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: matching an older adult with a rapidly progressive, necrotic, supratentorial ring-enhancing mass to glioblastoma is one of the most frequently tested epidemiologic pattern-recognition exercises in neuro-oncology. COMLEX: rapid cognitive and personality change over weeks, rather than months to years, should raise suspicion for a high-grade rather than low-grade process.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 47 Obj 4 — This same glioblastoma diagnosis is revisited under the molecular classification objective, where its defining IDH-wildtype genetic signature is explored in detail","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the major epidemiologic patterns of primary and metastatic intracranial tumors, including typical age groups and anatomic locations.","batch":2,"stem":"7-year-old girl -> three months of morning headaches and vomiting -> parents report an unsteady, wide-based gait that has worsened over the same period -> exam shows truncal ataxia and papilledema on funduscopic examination -> MRI shows a well-circumscribed, cystic mass with an enhancing mural nodule in the cerebellum. Which epidemiologic pattern best fits this presentation, and what is the single most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Adult supratentorial location in a child under 10, most consistent with glioblastoma","B":"Pediatric posterior fossa location in a child under 10, most consistent with primary CNS lymphoma","C":"Pediatric posterior fossa location in a child under 10, most consistent with pilocytic astrocytoma","D":"Pediatric posterior fossa location in a child under 10, most consistent with a solitary metastasis of unknown origin","E":"Adult supratentorial location in a child under 10, most consistent with meningioma"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This presentation, a young child with a well-circumscribed, cystic cerebellar mass featuring an enhancing mural nodule and obstructive hydrocephalus symptoms, matches the classic epidemiologic and imaging profile of pilocytic astrocytoma, a CNS WHO grade 1 tumor that typically occurs in children and young adults and is most commonly located in the cerebellum. Describing this as an adult supratentorial pattern misapplies the epidemiology, since glioblastoma is an adult, not pediatric, tumor and does not match the well-circumscribed cystic appearance described here. Primary CNS lymphoma is overwhelmingly an adult tumor, particularly in immunosuppressed or older patients, and does not fit this child's posterior fossa cystic mass. Meningioma is uncommon in children and, as an adult supratentorial dural-based tumor, does not match either this patient's age or the intra-axial cerebellar location. A solitary metastasis is exceedingly rare in a previously healthy 7-year-old without any known primary malignancy, making pilocytic astrocytoma by far the most likely diagnosis given this classic epidemiologic and imaging pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a well-circumscribed cystic cerebellar mass with an enhancing mural nodule in a child is the single most classic imaging vignette for pilocytic astrocytoma. COMLEX: pilocytic astrocytomas often harbor a KIAA1549-BRAF fusion and grow slowly, so complete surgical resection alone is frequently curative.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 7 — Cerebellar lesion patterns already localized there for vascular and degenerative causes, now extended here to the classic pediatric cerebellar tumor","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the major epidemiologic patterns of primary and metastatic intracranial tumors, including typical age groups and anatomic locations.","batch":2,"stem":"68-year-old man -> known history of a kidney transplant three years ago, maintained on chronic immunosuppression -> six weeks of progressive confusion and left-sided weakness -> MRI shows a single, deep periventricular mass with homogeneous enhancement, without necrosis, and only mild surrounding edema. Which epidemiologic pattern best fits this presentation, and what is the single most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Immunosuppressed adult with a deep periventricular mass, most consistent with primary CNS lymphoma","B":"Immunosuppressed adult with a deep periventricular mass, most consistent with glioblastoma","C":"Immunosuppressed adult with a deep periventricular mass, most consistent with meningioma","D":"Immunocompetent child with a deep periventricular mass, most consistent with primary CNS lymphoma","E":"Immunosuppressed adult with a deep periventricular mass, most consistent with a solitary metastasis of unknown origin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This presentation, a chronically immunosuppressed transplant recipient with a single, deep periventricular, homogeneously enhancing mass and relatively mild surrounding edema, matches the classic epidemiologic and imaging profile of primary CNS lymphoma, which is the most common CNS neoplasm in immunosuppressed individuals, including transplant recipients, and characteristically favors deep periventricular white matter. Glioblastoma would be expected to show more prominent necrosis and a more heterogeneous, ring-enhancing rather than homogeneous appearance, and immunosuppression is not specifically associated with increased glioblastoma risk in the way it is with lymphoma. Meningioma is an extra-axial, dural-based tumor rather than a deep periventricular intraparenchymal mass, and immunosuppression is not a recognized risk factor for meningioma. Primary CNS lymphoma is overwhelmingly a disease of older or immunosuppressed adults, not immunocompetent children, so describing this as a pediatric pattern misapplies the epidemiology entirely. While metastasis remains part of the differential for any enhancing mass, the profound immunosuppression and classic periventricular homogeneous enhancement pattern make primary CNS lymphoma the single most likely diagnosis here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: primary CNS lymphoma as the leading CNS neoplasm in immunosuppressed patients, with its classic deep periventricular, homogeneously enhancing imaging pattern, is a frequently tested epidemiologic and radiologic pairing. COMLEX: in immunosuppressed patients, primary CNS lymphoma is usually latently infected with Epstein-Barr virus, a detail relevant to both diagnosis and underlying pathogenesis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 8 — Telencephalic deficit localization already built there, now applied to distinguishing this deep white matter lesion pattern from a cortical or subcortical stroke syndrome","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how intracranial tumors cause increased intracranial pressure, seizures, personality change, hydrocephalus, and focal neurologic deficits.","batch":2,"stem":"54-year-old woman -> six weeks of progressively worsening headache that is worst upon waking and improves somewhat after vomiting -> new onset of a single generalized tonic-clonic seizure yesterday -> husband reports she has become uncharacteristically apathetic and socially withdrawn over the same period -> funduscopic examination shows bilateral optic disc swelling. Which single unifying mechanism best explains this constellation of morning headache, vomiting, seizure, personality change, and papilledema?","choices":{"A":"A mass lesion raising intracranial pressure, which produces all of these findings exclusively through direct compression of the optic nerve, with no other mechanism contributing","B":"A primary seizure disorder unrelated to any mass lesion, in which morning headache, vomiting, personality change, and papilledema are all postictal phenomena from the seizure itself","C":"A mass lesion raising intracranial pressure, which produces all of these findings through a combination of direct cortical irritation, brainstem and diencephalic compression, and impaired venous and axoplasmic flow at the optic disc","D":"A chronic migraine syndrome, in which morning headache, vomiting, personality change, and papilledema are all recognized features of a single prolonged migrainous episode","E":"A primary psychiatric depressive episode, in which morning headache, vomiting, seizure, and papilledema are all somatic manifestations of the underlying mood disorder"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A single unifying mass lesion raising intracranial pressure explains this entire constellation: morning headache and vomiting reflect the classic diurnal pattern of elevated intracranial pressure, worsened by recumbency and relieved somewhat by vomiting-induced hyperventilation and reduced pressure; seizure reflects direct cortical irritation by the mass or surrounding edema; personality change and apathy reflect frontal lobe or diffuse cortical dysfunction from mass effect; and papilledema reflects impaired axoplasmic flow and venous stasis at the optic disc from the same elevated pressure transmitted along the optic nerve sheath. Attributing all findings exclusively to direct optic nerve compression ignores that headache, vomiting, seizure, and personality change are not explained by optic nerve compression alone and instead require the broader mechanism of elevated intracranial pressure and mass effect. A primary seizure disorder would not typically produce papilledema, which specifically reflects sustained elevated intracranial pressure rather than a single seizure event, and would not explain a six-week prodrome of headache and personality change preceding the seizure. Migraine does not produce papilledema or new-onset seizures as recognized features, making this an inconsistent unifying explanation. A primary depressive episode does not produce papilledema or seizures as somatic manifestations, making this an inconsistent unifying explanation as well.","boardPrep":"Step 1: recognizing morning headache, vomiting, new-onset seizure, personality change, and papilledema as a single unified presentation of a mass lesion raising intracranial pressure, rather than as separate unrelated problems, is a core neuro-oncology reasoning skill. COMLEX: papilledema is a relatively late sign of raised intracranial pressure, so its absence early in a patient's course does not exclude a mass lesion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 34 → Obj 8 — Papilledema already introduced there as a clinical correlate of raised intracranial pressure, now integrated with the full constellation of mass-effect findings produced by an intracranial tumor","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how intracranial tumors cause increased intracranial pressure, seizures, personality change, hydrocephalus, and focal neurologic deficits.","batch":2,"stem":"47-year-old man -> found unresponsive at home, brought in by ambulance -> CT shows a large right temporal lobe hemorrhage with midline shift -> over the next thirty minutes, the right pupil becomes progressively dilated and fixed -> shortly after, he develops left-sided hemiparesis, which then progressively worsens to include weakness on the right side as well. Which phenomenon best explains the eventual appearance of weakness on the same side as the original lesion, in addition to the expected contralateral weakness?","choices":{"A":"Duret hemorrhage, in which secondary brainstem bleeding directly destroys corticospinal fibers on the same side as the original mass, producing ipsilateral weakness","B":"Tonsillar herniation, in which cerebellar tonsil compression of the medulla directly produces bilateral corticospinal tract dysfunction affecting both sides equally","C":"Subfalcine herniation, in which the cingulate gyrus compresses the contralateral anterior cerebral artery, producing ipsilateral weakness through a contralateral cortical infarct","D":"Kernohan notch phenomenon, in which the contralateral cerebral peduncle is compressed against the tentorial edge, producing a false-localizing hemiparesis on the same side as the mass","E":"Kernohan notch phenomenon, in which the ipsilateral cerebral peduncle is compressed against the tentorial edge, producing weakness on the same side as the mass through direct ipsilateral fiber injury"},"correct":"D","explanation":"As transtentorial herniation progresses, the contralateral cerebral peduncle can be pushed against the opposite edge of the tentorium, injuring the corticospinal fibers there and producing hemiparesis on the same side as the original mass lesion, a false-localizing sign known as the Kernohan notch phenomenon, which explains the later appearance of weakness ipsilateral to the hemorrhage in addition to the expected contralateral weakness. Duret hemorrhages are secondary brainstem hemorrhages from stretched paramedian vessels during herniation, but they are typically midline and paramedian rather than a lateralized, corticospinal-fiber-specific injury explaining this ipsilateral weakness pattern. Subfalcine herniation compresses the anterior, not posterior, cerebral artery and produces its effects through cingulate gyrus displacement, not through the peduncle-compression mechanism described in this vignette. Tonsillar herniation threatens the medullary respiratory and cardiac centers through foramen magnum compression, not through a mechanism that would selectively add ipsilateral corticospinal weakness to an existing contralateral deficit. The Kernohan notch phenomenon specifically involves compression of the contralateral, not ipsilateral, cerebral peduncle, so attributing the ipsilateral weakness to direct ipsilateral peduncle injury misstates the actual mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the Kernohan notch phenomenon, a false-localizing ipsilateral hemiparesis from contralateral peduncle compression during transtentorial herniation, is a classic and frequently tested herniation pitfall. COMLEX: recognizing this phenomenon prevents the diagnostic error of assuming a new ipsilateral deficit must reflect a separate lesion on that side.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 8 — Uncal herniation and CN III compression already established there as the classic pupillary finding, now extended to the additional false-localizing motor sign that can accompany the same process","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how intracranial tumors cause increased intracranial pressure, seizures, personality change, hydrocephalus, and focal neurologic deficits.","batch":2,"stem":"5-year-old boy -> three weeks of early morning vomiting and increasing lethargy -> parents note his head circumference has grown noticeably faster than expected for his age over the same period -> exam shows bilateral papilledema and a wide-based, unsteady gait -> MRI shows a mass obstructing the fourth ventricle with marked dilation of the lateral and third ventricles. Why does this child show head enlargement as a prominent feature, a finding that would not be expected in an adult with the identical obstructing mass?","choices":{"A":"Because children uniquely develop communicating rather than noncommunicating hydrocephalus from fourth ventricle masses, unlike adults with the same lesion location","B":"Because children produce a much larger absolute volume of cerebrospinal fluid than adults do, independent of any difference in cranial suture fusion","C":"Because this particular mass is overproducing cerebrospinal fluid rather than obstructing its outflow, a mechanism unique to pediatric fourth ventricle tumors","D":"Because his cranial sutures have not yet fused, allowing the skull to expand in response to the rising intracranial pressure and ventricular dilation rather than pressure alone increasing","E":"Because children experience vasogenic edema in response to obstructive hydrocephalus, whereas adults experience only cytotoxic edema from this identical process"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Because this child's cranial sutures have not yet fused, the skull can expand to accommodate the rising intracranial pressure and ventricular dilation produced by the fourth ventricle obstruction, resulting in head enlargement, whereas in an adult with fused sutures, the same obstructive process would instead raise intracranial pressure and expand the ventricles without any change in head circumference. A fourth ventricle mass causing focal obstruction produces noncommunicating hydrocephalus in children just as it does in adults, so this is not a uniquely pediatric mechanism, and mislabeling it as communicating hydrocephalus is incorrect regardless of age. Children do not produce a substantially larger absolute volume of cerebrospinal fluid than adults in a way that would explain this finding, and this is not the actual mechanism behind pediatric head enlargement. This vignette describes an obstructing mass at the fourth ventricle, not a choroid plexus tumor overproducing cerebrospinal fluid, so this proposed mechanism does not match the described pathology. Obstructive hydrocephalus is not mechanistically distinguished from cytotoxic versus vasogenic edema in this age-dependent way, and this explanation misapplies the cerebral edema framework to a hydrocephalus scenario.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cranial suture fusion status as the single explanation for why pediatric, but not adult, hydrocephalus produces head enlargement is a classic developmental physiology fact tested alongside hydrocephalus mechanisms. COMLEX: measuring head circumference over time is a standard, low-cost pediatric screening tool precisely because of this age-dependent skull response to rising intracranial pressure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 8 — The embryonic ventricular system and its adult derivatives already mapped there, now the anatomic basis for understanding how and where obstructive hydrocephalus develops","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate metastatic tumors from primary CNS tumors using lesion number, compartment, location, imaging pattern, and clinical history.","batch":2,"stem":"59-year-old man -> known history of stage IV lung adenocarcinoma diagnosed eight months ago, currently on systemic chemotherapy -> new-onset headache and mild word-finding difficulty -> MRI shows four separate enhancing lesions, each located precisely at the gray-white junction in different lobes, each with a disproportionately large surrounding edema pattern. Which category of intracranial tumor best fits this imaging pattern and clinical history together, and what does the lesion count itself suggest?","choices":{"A":"A single primary diffuse glioma with satellite lesions, since a known metastasis-prone primary malignancy combined with multiple gray-white junction lesions strongly favors a primary CNS process over embolic spread","B":"Metastatic disease, since a known metastasis-prone primary malignancy combined with multiple gray-white junction lesions strongly favors embolic spread over a primary CNS process","C":"Multifocal primary CNS lymphoma, since a known metastasis-prone primary malignancy combined with multiple gray-white junction lesions strongly favors a primary CNS process over embolic spread","D":"Multiple meningiomas, since a known metastasis-prone primary malignancy combined with multiple gray-white junction lesions strongly favors a primary CNS process over embolic spread","E":"Metastatic disease, since the lesion count alone is irrelevant to this distinction and only the imaging appearance of each individual lesion matters"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The combination of a known primary malignancy that is a leading source of brain metastases, multiple discrete lesions rather than one, and the classic gray-white junction location with disproportionate edema together strongly favor metastatic disease over any primary CNS tumor category, since multiplicity itself is a hallmark of hematogenous embolic spread rather than the typically solitary growth pattern of primary gliomas. Diffuse gliomas typically present as a single infiltrative mass rather than multiple discrete satellite lesions at the gray-white junction, making this description a poor fit for a primary glioma explanation. While primary CNS lymphoma can occasionally be multifocal, it classically favors deep periventricular white matter rather than the gray-white junction, and this patient's known metastasis-prone primary cancer makes metastasis by far the more probable explanation. Meningiomas are extra-axial, dural-based lesions rather than intraparenchymal gray-white junction masses, making this an inconsistent fit regardless of multiplicity. The lesion count is, in fact, directly relevant to this distinction, since multiplicity is one of the most useful discriminating features between metastatic and primary CNS tumors, so dismissing it as irrelevant misstates its diagnostic value.","boardPrep":"Step 1: multiple lesions at the gray-white junction in a patient with a known metastasis-prone primary cancer is about as close to a \"gimme\" diagnosis of brain metastases as boards offer. COMLEX: new neurologic symptoms in a patient with known metastatic cancer should prompt brain imaging even in the absence of other systemic disease progression, since brain metastases do not always track with overall disease burden.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 47 → Obj 1 — Lung cancer's status as one of the five leading sources of brain metastases, already established under the epidemiology objective, is directly what makes this history so diagnostically useful here","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate metastatic tumors from primary CNS tumors using lesion number, compartment, location, imaging pattern, and clinical history.","batch":2,"stem":"32-year-old woman -> new-onset focal seizure with secondary generalization -> no prior medical history and no known malignancy of any kind -> MRI shows a single, poorly marginated, nonenhancing mass expanding the left temporal white matter without any surrounding edema. Which category of intracranial tumor best fits this imaging pattern and clinical history together, and why does the absence of enhancement matter here?","choices":{"A":"A brain metastasis, since the absence of enhancement in an infiltrative mass reflects a relatively intact blood-brain barrier typical of lower-grade gliomas rather than aggressive vascular leakage","B":"A primary low-grade diffuse glioma, since the absence of enhancement in an infiltrative mass reflects a relatively intact blood-brain barrier typical of lower-grade gliomas rather than aggressive vascular leakage","C":"Primary CNS lymphoma, since the absence of enhancement in an infiltrative mass reflects a relatively intact blood-brain barrier typical of lower-grade gliomas rather than aggressive vascular leakage","D":"Glioblastoma, since the absence of enhancement in an infiltrative mass reflects a relatively intact blood-brain barrier typical of lower-grade gliomas rather than aggressive vascular leakage","E":"A primary low-grade diffuse glioma, since the absence of enhancement in this setting instead indicates the lesion is not a true neoplasm"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A single, infiltrative, nonenhancing mass in a young adult presenting with new seizures fits the classic profile of a primary low-grade diffuse glioma, and the lack of enhancement specifically reflects a relatively intact blood-brain barrier, since lower-grade gliomas have not yet developed the abnormally leaky, permeable vasculature that produces contrast enhancement in higher-grade tumors. Brain metastases characteristically enhance and are typically sharply demarcated with prominent edema, making the nonenhancing, poorly marginated, edema-free appearance described here a poor fit, and this patient also lacks any known primary malignancy. Glioblastoma is IDH-wildtype, CNS WHO grade 4 by definition, and would be expected to show prominent, often ring-like enhancement from its abnormally leaky vasculature, unlike the nonenhancing pattern described here. Primary CNS lymphoma classically shows homogeneous enhancement and favors deep periventricular white matter, neither of which matches this nonenhancing temporal lobe lesion. The absence of enhancement here reflects tumor grade and vascular permeability, not an indication that the lesion is not a true neoplasm, so this explanation is incorrect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a young adult with new seizures and a nonenhancing, infiltrative white matter mass is the classic presentation of a lower-grade diffuse glioma, and understanding why lack of enhancement reflects blood-brain barrier integrity rather than absence of tumor is a frequently tested imaging-physiology link. COMLEX: these patients often have a comparatively favorable initial course, with some surviving over a decade, though eventual transformation to a higher grade remains a lifelong risk.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 47 Obj 4 — This same low-grade glioma imaging behavior connects directly to the molecular classification objective, where the specific IDH-mutant genetic signature underlying this favorable behavior is detailed","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate metastatic tumors from primary CNS tumors using lesion number, compartment, location, imaging pattern, and clinical history.","batch":2,"stem":"70-year-old man -> two months of progressive confusion and gait imbalance -> MRI shows a large, necrotic, ring-enhancing mass crossing the corpus callosum and involving both frontal lobes in a symmetric, butterfly-shaped pattern. Which category of intracranial tumor does this specific imaging pattern most strongly suggest, and why does crossing the corpus callosum favor this category over a solitary metastasis?","choices":{"A":"A primary diffuse glioma, since direct infiltration along commissural white matter tracts such as the corpus callosum is a growth pattern characteristic of gliomas rather than the embolic spread typical of metastases","B":"A brain metastasis, since direct infiltration along commissural white matter tracts such as the corpus callosum is a growth pattern characteristic of metastases rather than the parenchymal invasion typical of gliomas","C":"A meningioma, since direct infiltration along commissural white matter tracts such as the corpus callosum is a growth pattern characteristic of dural-based tumors rather than intraparenchymal gliomas","D":"A choroid plexus papilloma, since direct infiltration along commissural white matter tracts such as the corpus callosum is a growth pattern characteristic of intraventricular tumors rather than gliomas","E":"A primary diffuse glioma, since crossing the corpus callosum is actually irrelevant to distinguishing it from metastasis, and only necrosis and enhancement matter for this distinction"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A symmetric, necrotic, ring-enhancing mass crossing the corpus callosum to involve both frontal lobes in this classic butterfly pattern reflects direct tumor infiltration along commissural white matter fibers, a growth pattern characteristic of high-grade diffuse gliomas such as glioblastoma, since metastases spread hematogenously to separate, often multiple sites rather than infiltrating contiguously across a single white matter tract to produce this specific bihemispheric pattern. Attributing this butterfly pattern to metastatic spread is incorrect, since metastases reach the brain through the bloodstream and characteristically form separate, discrete lesions rather than a single contiguous mass tracking along a commissural fiber tract. Meningiomas are extra-axial dural-based tumors that compress the underlying brain rather than infiltrating white matter tracts, so this growth pattern would not be expected. Choroid plexus papillomas are intraventricular tumors that recapitulate normal choroid plexus architecture and do not infiltrate along white matter commissures in this manner. Crossing the corpus callosum is, in fact, directly relevant to this distinction, since it specifically reflects a contiguous infiltrative growth pattern rather than embolic spread, so dismissing it as irrelevant misstates its diagnostic significance.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a necrotic, ring-enhancing \"butterfly glioma\" crossing the corpus callosum is one of the most classic and heavily tested single imaging patterns for glioblastoma. COMLEX: this bihemispheric growth pattern often makes complete surgical resection impossible, so treatment typically centers on maximal safe resection followed by chemoradiation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — Commissural fiber classification already established there for the corpus callosum specifically, now the anatomic explanation for this classic contiguous glioma spread pattern","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Compare the molecular and pathologic features of astrocytoma, oligodendroglioma, and glioblastoma using modern integrated diagnostic terminology.","batch":2,"stem":"41-year-old man -> new-onset seizure -> MRI shows a poorly marginated, nonenhancing mass in the left frontal white matter -> biopsy shows hypercellular tissue with elongated, hyperchromatic nuclei; immunostaining is positive for the IDH1 p.R132H mutant protein, and immunohistochemistry additionally shows loss of ATRX expression and p53 overexpression within tumor cells. Which diagnosis best fits this combination of molecular findings, and what does the loss of ATRX expression specifically indicate?","choices":{"A":"Glioblastoma, IDH-wildtype, in which ATRX loss reflects one of the senescence-evasion mechanisms that occurs alongside the defining IDH mutation and typical TP53 mutation in this tumor type","B":"Astrocytoma, IDH-mutant, in which ATRX loss reflects one of the senescence-evasion mechanisms that occurs alongside the defining IDH mutation and typical TP53 mutation in this tumor type","C":"Oligodendroglioma, IDH-mutant and 1p/19q-codeleted, in which ATRX loss reflects one of the senescence-evasion mechanisms that occurs alongside the defining IDH mutation and typical TP53 mutation in this tumor type","D":"Pilocytic astrocytoma, in which ATRX loss reflects one of the senescence-evasion mechanisms that occurs alongside the defining IDH mutation and typical TP53 mutation in this tumor type","E":"Astrocytoma, IDH-mutant, in which ATRX loss instead indicates that the tumor has acquired an EGFR gene amplification specific to this subtype"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This combination, a positive IDH1 p.R132H immunostain together with ATRX loss and p53 overexpression, is the classic immunohistochemical triad defining astrocytoma, IDH-mutant, in which ATRX inactivation is one of the three simultaneous molecular alterations, alongside the earlier IDH mutation and accompanying TP53 mutation, that together drive this tumor's senescence-evasion and pathogenesis. Glioblastoma is specifically IDH-wildtype by definition, so a positive IDH1 p.R132H immunostain excludes this diagnosis outright, regardless of the ATRX and p53 findings. Oligodendroglioma requires 1p/19q codeletion as its defining feature and does not typically show ATRX loss, which is instead characteristic of the astrocytic, not oligodendroglial, lineage. Pilocytic astrocytoma is a CNS WHO grade 1 tumor defined by MAP kinase pathway alterations such as the KIAA1549-BRAF fusion, not by IDH mutation, ATRX loss, or p53 overexpression, making this an inconsistent diagnosis for the findings described. ATRX loss reflects a senescence-evasion mechanism intrinsic to this tumor's pathogenesis, not evidence of EGFR amplification, which is instead a glioblastoma-associated alteration not implicated by ATRX status.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the IDH1 p.R132H-positive, ATRX-loss, p53-overexpression immunohistochemical triad is the classic laboratory signature confirming astrocytoma, IDH-mutant, on a biopsy specimen. COMLEX: recognizing this molecular subtype matters clinically, since IDH-mutant astrocytomas carry a substantially better prognosis than IDH-wildtype glioblastoma even when histologically high grade.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 47 → Obj 3 — The nonenhancing, poorly marginated imaging pattern already linked there to lower-grade primary gliomas is now confirmed at the molecular level in this same tumor type","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Compare the molecular and pathologic features of astrocytoma, oligodendroglioma, and glioblastoma using modern integrated diagnostic terminology.","batch":2,"stem":"46-year-old woman -> several years of intermittent focal seizures -> MRI shows a gelatinous-appearing, partially calcified mass in the superficial cerebral cortex and white matter -> biopsy shows sheets of cells with round nuclei, finely granular chromatin, and clear perinuclear halos, set within a delicate, anastomosing capillary network described as resembling chicken wire; genetic testing confirms an IDH1 mutation together with whole-arm codeletion of chromosomes 1p and 19q. Which diagnosis best fits this combination of histologic and molecular findings, and what does the chicken-wire vascular pattern reflect?","choices":{"A":"Astrocytoma, IDH-mutant, in which the chicken-wire pattern refers to the tumor's characteristic delicate, anastomosing capillary network visible on routine histology","B":"Glioblastoma, IDH-wildtype, in which the chicken-wire pattern refers to the tumor's characteristic delicate, anastomosing capillary network visible on routine histology","C":"Ependymoma, in which the chicken-wire pattern refers to the tumor's characteristic delicate, anastomosing capillary network visible on routine histology","D":"Oligodendroglioma, in which the chicken-wire pattern instead refers to a specific genetic testing artifact seen only on 1p/19q codeletion assays, not a histologic finding","E":"Oligodendroglioma, in which the chicken-wire pattern refers to the tumor's characteristic delicate, anastomosing capillary network visible on routine histology"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This combination, round nuclei with clear perinuclear halos, frequent calcification, an IDH1 mutation, and 1p/19q codeletion, is diagnostic of oligodendroglioma, and the chicken-wire description refers specifically to the tumor's delicate network of anastomosing capillaries visible on routine histologic examination, a distinctive vascular pattern characteristic of this tumor type. Astrocytoma, IDH-mutant requires ATRX loss and TP53 mutation rather than 1p/19q codeletion, and it does not characteristically show this perinuclear halo and chicken-wire vascular appearance, making it an inconsistent diagnosis here. Glioblastoma is IDH-wildtype by definition, which directly contradicts the IDH1 mutation identified by genetic testing in this vignette, excluding this diagnosis. Ependymoma is characterized by perivascular pseudorosettes and true rosettes rather than perinuclear halos and a chicken-wire vascular pattern, and it is not defined by IDH mutation or 1p/19q codeletion. The chicken-wire description is a genuine histologic, not genetic-testing, finding visible directly under the microscope, so describing it as a testing artifact misstates what this term actually refers to.","boardPrep":"Step 1: perinuclear halos, or \"fried egg\" appearance, together with a chicken-wire capillary network and frequent calcification is the classic histologic triad for oligodendroglioma, paired with its defining IDH mutation and 1p/19q codeletion. COMLEX: oligodendrogliomas have the best prognosis among diffuse gliomas when matched for grade, information that is directly useful when counseling a patient on expected disease course.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 47 → Obj 3 — Metastatic-versus-primary imaging differentiation already built there now converges with this molecular diagnosis, since oligodendroglioma's calcified, superficial cortical growth pattern is itself a useful imaging clue favoring a primary tumor","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Compare the molecular and pathologic features of astrocytoma, oligodendroglioma, and glioblastoma using modern integrated diagnostic terminology.","batch":2,"stem":"67-year-old woman -> six weeks of headache and rapidly progressive left-sided weakness -> MRI shows a large, heterogeneous, ring-enhancing right frontal mass with a necrotic center and marked surrounding edema -> biopsy shows serpiginous zones of necrosis with tumor cells densely palisading along their edges, along with tufts of proliferating endothelial cells bulging into small vessel lumina -> genetic testing shows the tumor is IDH-wildtype with combined gain of chromosome 7 and loss of chromosome 10. Which diagnosis best fits this combination of histologic and molecular findings, and what process specifically drives the microvascular proliferation observed?","choices":{"A":"Glioblastoma, IDH-wildtype, in which vascular endothelial growth factor produced by hypoxic malignant astrocytes drives this distinctive proliferative vascular change","B":"Astrocytoma, IDH-mutant, in which vascular endothelial growth factor produced by hypoxic malignant astrocytes drives this distinctive proliferative vascular change","C":"Oligodendroglioma, in which vascular endothelial growth factor produced by hypoxic malignant astrocytes drives this distinctive proliferative vascular change","D":"Pilocytic astrocytoma, in which vascular endothelial growth factor produced by hypoxic malignant astrocytes drives this distinctive proliferative vascular change","E":"Glioblastoma, IDH-wildtype, in which the microvascular proliferation instead results directly from tumor cells physically invading and remodeling pre-existing vessel walls, unrelated to any growth factor signaling"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This combination, palisading necrosis, microvascular proliferation, an IDH-wildtype status, and combined gain of chromosome 7 with loss of chromosome 10, is diagnostic of glioblastoma, IDH-wildtype, and the characteristic microvascular proliferation is driven by vascular endothelial growth factor produced by hypoxic malignant astrocytes surrounding the necrotic zones, stimulating endothelial cells to proliferate and pile up within small vessel lumina. Astrocytoma, IDH-mutant is excluded by the IDH-wildtype status described here, since this molecular finding is specifically the defining feature separating glioblastoma from IDH-mutant astrocytoma. Oligodendroglioma requires 1p/19q codeletion and typically shows a chicken-wire capillary pattern rather than palisading necrosis and proliferative microvascular tufts, making this diagnosis inconsistent with the findings. Pilocytic astrocytoma is a CNS WHO grade 1 tumor in which microvascular proliferation, when present, does not indicate an unfavorable prognosis, unlike its ominous significance in glioblastoma, and it is not defined by IDH-wildtype status with chromosome 7 gain and chromosome 10 loss. The microvascular proliferation in glioblastoma is driven by vascular endothelial growth factor signaling rather than direct physical vessel wall invasion and remodeling by tumor cells, so this alternative mechanism misstates the actual underlying process.","boardPrep":"Step 1: palisading necrosis plus VEGF-driven microvascular proliferation, in an IDH-wildtype tumor with chromosome 7 gain and chromosome 10 loss, is the complete histologic-plus-molecular signature that confirms glioblastoma on both biopsy and genetic testing. COMLEX: bevacizumab, an anti-VEGF monoclonal antibody, is used clinically in recurrent glioblastoma precisely because of this VEGF-driven vascular biology.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 47 → Obj 2 — The mass-effect and edema mechanisms already established there are directly produced by this same glioblastoma biology, since its abnormally leaky, VEGF-driven vasculature is what generates the surrounding vasogenic edema","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl47_bloom_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the major epidemiologic patterns of primary and metastatic intracranial tumors, including typical age groups and anatomic locations.","batch":3,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy presents with morning headaches, vomiting, and truncal ataxia. MRI shows a midline cerebellar vermis mass, and cytologic examination of the cerebrospinal fluid reveals malignant small round blue cells, matching cells also found along the spinal cord surface on staging imaging. Which tumor type and epidemiologic feature best fit this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Pilocytic astrocytoma, an embryonal tumor of childhood that characteristically seeds the cerebrospinal fluid to produce distant spinal drop metastases","B":"Meningioma, an embryonal tumor of childhood that characteristically seeds the cerebrospinal fluid to produce distant spinal drop metastases","C":"Glioblastoma, an embryonal tumor of childhood that characteristically seeds the cerebrospinal fluid to produce distant spinal drop metastases","D":"Vestibular schwannoma, an embryonal tumor of childhood that characteristically seeds the cerebrospinal fluid to produce distant spinal drop metastases","E":"Medulloblastoma, an embryonal tumor of childhood that characteristically seeds the cerebrospinal fluid to produce distant spinal drop metastases"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Medulloblastoma is a highly cellular embryonal tumor arising in the cerebellar vermis in children, composed of small round blue cells, and is well known for seeding the subarachnoid space via the cerebrospinal fluid to produce distant \"drop metastases\" along the spinal cord, exactly the pattern described here, making CSF cytology and full spinal imaging a standard part of staging. Pilocytic astrocytoma is also a pediatric cerebellar tumor but is a low-grade, well-circumscribed glial tumor that does not characteristically produce this small round blue cell cytology or CSF drop metastasis pattern. Meningioma is an adult, dural-based tumor that does not occur as a pediatric cerebellar mass and does not seed the CSF in this manner. Glioblastoma is an adult, supratentorial tumor and would not fit this child's age or the embryonal small round blue cell cytology described. Vestibular schwannoma arises from the cerebellopontine angle, not the cerebellar vermis, and does not seed the CSF to produce spinal drop metastases.","boardPrep":"Step 1: medulloblastoma as the classic pediatric cerebellar embryonal tumor with CSF seeding and spinal drop metastases is a distinct, high-yield addition to the posterior fossa tumor differential beyond pilocytic astrocytoma. COMLEX: full craniospinal MRI staging is standard at diagnosis specifically because of this CSF-seeding behavior.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 47 → Obj 1 — tumor epidemiology, tested here as a board-style named-tumor and demographic vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl47_bloom_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the major epidemiologic patterns of primary and metastatic intracranial tumors, including typical age groups and anatomic locations.","batch":3,"stem":"A 38-year-old pregnant woman with a previously stable, incidentally discovered small meningioma is found to have significant tumor growth on repeat imaging during her third trimester, with symptoms resolving substantially after delivery. Which feature of meningioma biology best explains this pregnancy-related growth acceleration?","choices":{"A":"Meningiomas frequently express androgen receptors, so the markedly elevated androgen levels of pregnancy can accelerate tumor growth","B":"Meningiomas frequently express progesterone receptors, so the markedly elevated progesterone levels of pregnancy can accelerate tumor growth","C":"Meningiomas frequently express thyroid hormone receptors, so the markedly elevated thyroid hormone levels of pregnancy can accelerate tumor growth","D":"Meningiomas are entirely hormone-independent tumors, so this growth pattern must instead reflect an unrelated, coincidental change occurring during pregnancy itself","E":"Meningiomas frequently express insulin receptors, so the markedly elevated insulin levels of pregnancy can accelerate tumor growth"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Meningiomas show a strong female predominance and frequently express progesterone receptors, so the substantially elevated progesterone levels of pregnancy can drive accelerated tumor growth, with some regression of symptoms after delivery as hormone levels fall, a recognized clinical pattern tied directly to this tumor's hormone-receptor biology. Androgen and thyroid hormone receptor expression are not the well-established hormonal drivers of meningioma growth, progesterone receptor expression is the specific, clinically relevant feature. Meningiomas are not hormone-independent, their frequent progesterone receptor expression is a defining and clinically exploited biologic feature, so describing this as coincidental ignores well-established tumor biology. Insulin receptor expression is not the recognized mechanism underlying pregnancy-associated meningioma growth.","boardPrep":"Step 1: meningioma's female predominance and progesterone receptor expression, explaining pregnancy- and hormone-related growth acceleration, is a testable extension of its basic epidemiology. COMLEX: this same hormone sensitivity is why some patients report meningioma-related symptom fluctuation across the menstrual cycle.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 47 → Obj 1 — tumor epidemiology, tested here as a board-style named-tumor and demographic vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl47_bloom_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the major epidemiologic patterns of primary and metastatic intracranial tumors, including typical age groups and anatomic locations.","batch":3,"stem":"A 34-year-old man presents with progressive unilateral hearing loss and tinnitus, and MRI shows an enhancing mass in the cerebellopontine angle. Six months later, imaging of the opposite side reveals an identical mass. Which underlying condition should be strongly suspected given this bilateral pattern?","choices":{"A":"Neurofibromatosis type 1, since bilateral vestibular schwannomas are considered nearly pathognomonic for this specific genetic syndrome","B":"Von Hippel-Lindau disease, since bilateral vestibular schwannomas are considered nearly pathognomonic for this specific genetic syndrome","C":"Neurofibromatosis type 2, since bilateral vestibular schwannomas are considered nearly pathognomonic for this specific genetic syndrome","D":"Tuberous sclerosis complex, since bilateral vestibular schwannomas are considered nearly pathognomonic for this specific genetic syndrome","E":"Li-Fraumeni syndrome, since bilateral vestibular schwannomas are considered nearly pathognomonic for this specific genetic syndrome"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Vestibular schwannomas typically arise sporadically and unilaterally in the cerebellopontine angle, but bilateral vestibular schwannomas are considered nearly pathognomonic for neurofibromatosis type 2, a distinct genetic syndrome from neurofibromatosis type 1, driven by NF2 gene mutations affecting the protein merlin, warranting genetic counseling and broader screening for other associated tumors such as meningiomas and ependymomas. Neurofibromatosis type 1 is instead associated with neurofibromas, optic gliomas, and café-au-lait macules, not classically with bilateral vestibular schwannomas. Von Hippel-Lindau disease is associated with hemangioblastomas, renal cell carcinoma, and pheochromocytoma, not bilateral vestibular schwannomas. Tuberous sclerosis complex is associated with cortical tubers and subependymal giant cell astrocytomas, an entirely different tumor spectrum. Li-Fraumeni syndrome is associated with a broad range of malignancies including sarcomas and early-onset breast cancer through germline TP53 mutation, not specifically bilateral vestibular schwannomas.","boardPrep":"Step 1: bilateral vestibular schwannomas as the defining, nearly pathognomonic feature of neurofibromatosis type 2 is a high-yield distinction from the more commonly tested neurofibromatosis type 1. COMLEX: recognizing this pattern should prompt genetic counseling and lifelong surveillance imaging given the syndrome's associated tumor spectrum.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 47 → Obj 1 — tumor epidemiology, tested here as a board-style named-tumor and demographic vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl47_bloom_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how intracranial tumors cause increased intracranial pressure, seizures, personality change, hydrocephalus, and focal neurologic deficits.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient with a slowly growing meningioma remains entirely asymptomatic for years despite continued tumor growth, then develops a rapid onset of severe headache and vomiting once the tumor reaches a critical size. Which principle best explains this delayed but then abrupt symptomatic decline?","choices":{"A":"The Monro-Kellie doctrine, in which the fixed intracranial volume prevents any compensation, so pressure should instead have risen immediately and steadily from the very first day of tumor growth","B":"The Kernohan notch phenomenon, in which the fixed intracranial volume allows early compensation by displacing blood and cerebrospinal fluid, until this reserve is exhausted and pressure then rises steeply","C":"Cerebral autoregulation failure, in which the fixed intracranial volume allows early compensation by displacing blood and cerebrospinal fluid, until this reserve is exhausted and pressure then rises steeply","D":"The Monro-Kellie doctrine, in which the fixed intracranial volume allows early compensation by displacing blood and cerebrospinal fluid, until this reserve is exhausted and pressure then rises steeply","E":"The blood-brain barrier disruption principle, in which the fixed intracranial volume allows early compensation by displacing blood and cerebrospinal fluid, until this reserve is exhausted and pressure then rises steeply"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The Monro-Kellie doctrine holds that the skull encloses a fixed total volume shared by brain, blood, and cerebrospinal fluid, so a slowly growing mass can initially be compensated for by displacing venous blood and cerebrospinal fluid out of the cranial vault, keeping intracranial pressure relatively normal; once this compensatory reserve is exhausted, however, even small further increases in mass volume produce steep, exponential rises in intracranial pressure, explaining the abrupt clinical decline once a critical tumor size is reached. Describing the doctrine as preventing any compensation at all contradicts its actual principle, which specifically explains the compensatory phase that precedes decompensation. The Kernohan notch phenomenon describes a specific false-localizing motor sign from herniation, not this general volume-compensation principle. Cerebral autoregulation failure describes loss of cerebral blood flow control, a related but distinct concept from this volume-compensation framework. Blood-brain barrier disruption describes the mechanism of vasogenic edema specifically, not the broader fixed-volume compensatory principle being described here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the Monro-Kellie doctrine's compensated-then-decompensated pattern explains why slowly growing masses can remain silent for a surprisingly long time before abrupt clinical deterioration. COMLEX: this same principle underlies why even small volume changes, such as a modest hemorrhage, can be catastrophic once intracranial compliance is already exhausted by a pre-existing mass.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 47 → Obj 2 — mass-effect and ICP mechanisms, tested here as a board-style compensatory-physiology vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl47_bloom_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how intracranial tumors cause increased intracranial pressure, seizures, personality change, hydrocephalus, and focal neurologic deficits.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient with a rapidly enlarging intracranial tumor develops severe hypertension, bradycardia, and irregular, gasping respirations. Which principle best explains this specific triad, and what does its appearance indicate about the severity of the underlying process?","choices":{"A":"A primary cardiac arrhythmia unrelated to intracranial pressure, an early and reassuring sign that critically elevated intracranial pressure has triggered a brainstem response signaling low herniation risk","B":"The Cushing reflex, an early and reassuring sign in which critically elevated intracranial pressure triggers a brainstem response to preserve cerebral perfusion, signaling that herniation is unlikely to occur","C":"The baroreceptor reflex functioning entirely normally, an early and reassuring sign in which critically elevated intracranial pressure triggers a brainstem response, signaling that herniation is unlikely to occur","D":"The Cushing reflex, a late and ominous sign in which critically elevated intracranial pressure triggers a brainstem response to preserve cerebral perfusion, signaling impending herniation","E":"Autonomic dysreflexia from an unrelated spinal cord process, an early and reassuring sign that critically elevated intracranial pressure has triggered a brainstem response signaling low herniation risk"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The Cushing reflex, hypertension, bradycardia, and irregular respirations together, represents a brainstem-mediated attempt to raise systemic blood pressure above the critically elevated intracranial pressure in order to preserve cerebral perfusion, but it is a late, ominous finding indicating that intracranial pressure has risen to a dangerous level and that brainstem herniation is imminent or already underway, demanding immediate intervention. Describing this as an early, reassuring sign is incorrect, it is specifically a late finding reflecting severe decompensation, not an early or reassuring one. This is not simply the baroreceptor reflex functioning normally, it reflects a pathologic, brainstem-driven response to critically high intracranial pressure rather than routine blood pressure homeostasis. It is not a primary, unrelated cardiac arrhythmia, the bradycardia here is a direct physiologic consequence of the elevated intracranial pressure itself. It is not autonomic dysreflexia, which is a distinct phenomenon related to spinal cord injury above T6, not intracranial mass effect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the Cushing reflex, hypertension with bradycardia and irregular respirations, is a critical, must-recognize late sign of impending herniation requiring emergent management. COMLEX: recognizing this triad should immediately prompt urgent neuroimaging and neurosurgical consultation rather than routine antihypertensive treatment of the elevated blood pressure alone.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 47 → Obj 2 — mass-effect and ICP mechanisms, tested here as a board-style compensatory-physiology vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl47_bloom_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how intracranial tumors cause increased intracranial pressure, seizures, personality change, hydrocephalus, and focal neurologic deficits.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient with a large frontal lobe mass develops severe subfalcine herniation, and imaging subsequently shows a wedge-shaped infarct involving the medial surfaces of both frontal lobes, producing bilateral leg-predominant weakness. Which vessel and mechanism best explain this specific complication?","choices":{"A":"Bilateral posterior cerebral artery compression against the tentorial edge, producing bilateral medial frontal infarction with leg-predominant weakness from the medial homunculus location","B":"Bilateral middle cerebral artery compression against the sphenoid wing, producing bilateral medial frontal infarction with leg-predominant weakness from the medial homunculus location","C":"Bilateral anterior cerebral artery compression against the falx cerebri, producing bilateral medial frontal infarction with leg-predominant weakness from the medial homunculus location","D":"Bilateral internal carotid artery compression against the clinoid process, producing bilateral medial frontal infarction with leg-predominant weakness from the medial homunculus location","E":"Bilateral superior cerebellar artery compression against the tentorial edge, producing bilateral medial frontal infarction with leg-predominant weakness from the medial homunculus location"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Severe subfalcine herniation can push the cingulate gyrus and adjacent medial frontal lobe far enough under the falx cerebri to compress both anterior cerebral arteries against its rigid free edge, producing bilateral medial frontal lobe infarction; because the leg representation of the motor homunculus lies on the medial cortical surface supplied by the anterior cerebral artery, this specific infarct pattern produces leg-predominant, rather than arm- or face-predominant, weakness. The posterior cerebral artery is instead compressed against the tentorial edge during transtentorial, not subfalcine, herniation, and its territory does not include the medial frontal motor cortex. The middle cerebral artery supplies the lateral cerebral convexity, including the more lateral arm and face motor cortex, not the medial leg-representing cortex, and it is not the vessel classically compressed by subfalcine herniation. The internal carotid artery is not the vessel directly compressed against a specific bony or dural landmark in either subfalcine or transtentorial herniation. The superior cerebellar artery supplies the cerebellum and midbrain, an entirely different vascular territory unrelated to this bilateral medial frontal infarct pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: bilateral anterior cerebral artery compression from severe subfalcine herniation, producing leg-predominant weakness via the medial motor homunculus, is a useful complement to the more commonly tested uncal herniation and Kernohan notch complications. COMLEX: recognizing this specific weakness pattern helps distinguish subfalcine-related ACA infarction from a primary parasagittal mass lesion presenting with similar leg-predominant findings.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 47 → Obj 2 — mass-effect and ICP mechanisms, tested here as a board-style compensatory-physiology vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl47_bloom_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate metastatic tumors from primary CNS tumors using lesion number, compartment, location, imaging pattern, and clinical history.","batch":3,"stem":"A radiologist notes that a dural-based mass compressing but not invading the underlying brain is classified as extra-axial, while a mass arising within and infiltrating the brain parenchyma itself, whether primary or metastatic, is classified as intra-axial. Which pairing of tumor type to compartment is correct?","choices":{"A":"Meningioma is intra-axial, while both diffuse glioma and metastatic tumor are extra-axial","B":"Meningioma and diffuse glioma are both extra-axial, while metastatic tumor alone is intra-axial","C":"Meningioma is extra-axial, while both diffuse glioma and metastatic tumor are intra-axial by this same standard classification","D":"Meningioma and metastatic tumor are both extra-axial, while diffuse glioma alone is intra-axial","E":"All three tumor types, meningioma, diffuse glioma, and metastatic tumor, are classified as intra-axial regardless of their actual site of origin or growth pattern"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Meningioma arises from meningothelial cells of the arachnoid and grows as a dural-based mass that compresses rather than invades the adjacent brain, making it the classic extra-axial tumor, while diffuse gliomas arise from and infiltrate directly within brain parenchyma, and metastatic tumors, though arising elsewhere in the body, lodge and grow within brain parenchyma after hematogenous spread, making both intra-axial, despite their very different origins. Reversing this classification, or grouping glioma with meningioma as extra-axial, misapplies the compartment framework, since diffuse gliomas are defined by their intraparenchymal, infiltrative growth. Grouping metastatic tumor with meningioma as extra-axial similarly misapplies the framework, since metastases characteristically grow within brain parenchyma itself, not along the dura. Classifying all three tumor types as intra-axial ignores meningioma's distinct dural-based, extra-axial origin and growth pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the intra-axial versus extra-axial compartment distinction is a foundational neuroradiology framework that groups both metastatic and primary diffuse gliomas together against meningioma, despite gliomas and metastases having very different underlying mechanisms. COMLEX: this same compartment framework guides the initial differential diagnosis whenever a new intracranial mass is identified on imaging.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 47 → Obj 3 — metastatic-versus-primary differentiation, tested here as a board-style compartment and pattern vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl47_bloom_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate metastatic tumors from primary CNS tumors using lesion number, compartment, location, imaging pattern, and clinical history.","batch":3,"stem":"A patient with known metastatic breast cancer develops diffuse headache, multiple cranial neuropathies, and communicating hydrocephalus, but MRI shows no discrete parenchymal mass, only diffuse, thin enhancement coating the surface of the brain and spinal cord. Which specific metastatic pattern, distinct from the more commonly tested solid parenchymal metastasis, best explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Leptomeningeal carcinomatosis, in which malignant cells diffusely seed the cerebrospinal fluid and coat the meningeal surfaces rather than forming discrete parenchymal masses","B":"Solitary parenchymal metastasis, in which malignant cells diffusely seed the cerebrospinal fluid and coat the meningeal surfaces rather than forming a discrete parenchymal mass","C":"Multiple gray-white junction metastases, in which malignant cells diffusely seed the cerebrospinal fluid and coat the meningeal surfaces rather than forming discrete parenchymal masses","D":"A primary diffuse glioma, in which malignant cells diffusely seed the cerebrospinal fluid and coat the meningeal surfaces rather than forming discrete parenchymal masses","E":"A dural-based meningioma, in which malignant cells diffusely seed the cerebrospinal fluid and coat the meningeal surfaces rather than forming discrete parenchymal masses"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Leptomeningeal carcinomatosis is a distinct metastatic pattern in which malignant cells spread diffusely through the cerebrospinal fluid and coat the pia-arachnoid surfaces of the brain and spinal cord, producing communicating hydrocephalus from impaired CSF resorption, multiple cranial neuropathies from nerve root involvement as they traverse the subarachnoid space, and diffuse, thin meningeal enhancement rather than a discrete parenchymal mass, a pattern requiring CSF cytology for definitive diagnosis. Solitary parenchymal metastasis and multiple gray-white junction metastases both describe discrete intraparenchymal masses, the opposite imaging pattern from this diffuse, mass-free meningeal coating. A primary diffuse glioma is an intraparenchymal, infiltrative tumor, not a diffuse leptomeningeal process, and would not typically produce this specific communicating hydrocephalus and multi-cranial-neuropathy pattern without a discrete mass. A dural-based meningioma is a solid, discrete extra-axial mass, not a diffuse, thin coating of the pia-arachnoid surfaces.","boardPrep":"Step 1: leptomeningeal carcinomatosis as a distinct, mass-free metastatic pattern producing communicating hydrocephalus and multiple cranial neuropathies broadens the differentiation framework beyond solid parenchymal metastases alone. COMLEX: CSF cytology, sometimes requiring repeated lumbar punctures for adequate sensitivity, is the key diagnostic test when this pattern is suspected.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 47 → Obj 3 — metastatic-versus-primary differentiation, tested here as a board-style compartment and pattern vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl47_bloom_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate metastatic tumors from primary CNS tumors using lesion number, compartment, location, imaging pattern, and clinical history.","batch":3,"stem":"A student reasons that because metastases are classically taught as multiple lesions at the gray-white junction, a patient with a known metastasis-prone cancer and a single, solitary enhancing brain lesion cannot have a brain metastasis. Which correction to this reasoning is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"A solitary lesion in this setting always excludes metastatic disease, so it should be assumed to represent a new primary CNS tumor","B":"Multiplicity is entirely irrelevant to this distinction in every clinical scenario, so lesion count should never factor into the differential diagnosis","C":"A solitary lesion occurs only in metastases from gastrointestinal primary tumors specifically, making this reasoning valid for every other cancer type","D":"A substantial proportion of brain metastases present as a solitary lesion, so a single lesion does not exclude metastasis, especially with a metastasis-prone primary","E":"A solitary lesion is diagnostic of primary CNS lymphoma specifically whenever a known systemic malignancy happens to also be present in the same patient"},"correct":"D","explanation":"While multiplicity is a useful clue favoring metastasis when present, a substantial proportion of brain metastases actually present as a solitary lesion, so a single lesion does not reliably exclude metastatic disease, particularly in a patient with a known metastasis-prone primary cancer, where metastasis should remain high on the differential even with just one lesion, often warranting biopsy or resection to confirm the diagnosis. Assuming a solitary lesion always excludes metastasis and must represent a new primary tumor overstates the diagnostic value of lesion count alone and would lead to a significant number of missed metastases. Multiplicity is not entirely irrelevant, it remains a genuinely useful, just not absolute, discriminating feature when it is present. Solitary presentation is not restricted to gastrointestinal primary tumors specifically, it occurs across many metastasis-prone cancer types. A solitary lesion in a patient with known systemic malignancy is not diagnostic of primary CNS lymphoma, that diagnosis instead depends on its own characteristic periventricular location and immunosuppression risk factors, not simply lesion count in this context.","boardPrep":"Step 1: recognizing that many brain metastases are solitary refines the multiplicity teaching point into a more clinically accurate, nuanced rule rather than an absolute one. COMLEX: this nuance is why a solitary enhancing lesion in a patient with known cancer still typically requires biopsy or resection rather than assuming a benign or unrelated primary CNS process.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 47 → Obj 3 — metastatic-versus-primary differentiation, tested here as a board-style compartment and pattern vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl47_bloom_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Compare the molecular and pathologic features of astrocytoma, oligodendroglioma, and glioblastoma using modern integrated diagnostic terminology.","batch":3,"stem":"Two patients with IDH-wildtype glioblastoma are treated with identical surgical resection followed by radiation and temozolomide chemotherapy. One patient's tumor shows MGMT promoter methylation, while the other's does not. Which outcome is expected, and what mechanism explains this difference?","choices":{"A":"The methylated tumor responds worse to temozolomide, since MGMT promoter methylation instead activates, rather than silencing, this same DNA repair enzyme in glioblastoma cells","B":"The unmethylated tumor responds better to temozolomide, since MGMT promoter methylation silences a DNA repair enzyme that would otherwise reverse the chemotherapy's DNA-damaging effect","C":"Both tumors respond identically to temozolomide, since MGMT methylation status has no meaningful effect on chemotherapy sensitivity in glioblastoma","D":"The methylated tumor responds better to temozolomide, since MGMT promoter methylation silences a DNA repair enzyme that would otherwise reverse the chemotherapy's DNA-damaging effect","E":"MGMT methylation status predicts surgical resectability rather than chemotherapy response, making this comparison a nonsensical clinical question"},"correct":"D","explanation":"MGMT encodes a DNA repair enzyme that removes the specific alkyl DNA adducts created by temozolomide, reversing its cytotoxic effect; when the MGMT promoter is methylated, this repair enzyme is epigenetically silenced, so tumor cells cannot efficiently repair the chemotherapy-induced DNA damage, resulting in a substantially better treatment response compared with an unmethylated tumor that retains active MGMT repair capacity. Describing the unmethylated tumor as responding better reverses the actual, well-established direction of this predictive relationship. MGMT methylation status is not irrelevant to chemotherapy response, it is one of the most clinically important predictive biomarkers in glioblastoma management. Methylation silences, rather than activates, MGMT expression, so describing it as activating the repair enzyme misstates the underlying epigenetic mechanism. MGMT methylation status predicts chemotherapy response, not surgical resectability, which instead depends on tumor location and other anatomic factors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MGMT promoter methylation as a predictive biomarker for temozolomide response, via epigenetic silencing of a DNA repair enzyme, is one of the most clinically important molecular tests in glioblastoma management. COMLEX: this same biomarker is used in clinical practice to counsel patients on expected chemotherapy responsiveness and prognosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 47 → Obj 4 — integrated molecular glioma classification, tested here as a board-style biomarker and nomenclature vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl47_bloom_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Compare the molecular and pathologic features of astrocytoma, oligodendroglioma, and glioblastoma using modern integrated diagnostic terminology.","batch":3,"stem":"A biopsy from a diffusely infiltrating tumor shows an IDH1 mutation, ATRX loss, and TP53 mutation, but no necrosis and no microvascular proliferation on histology. Molecular testing additionally reveals homozygous deletion of CDKN2A. Which grade should this tumor be assigned under the 2021 WHO criteria, and why?","choices":{"A":"CNS WHO grade 2, since homozygous CDKN2A deletion is itself sufficient to assign the highest grade to an IDH-mutant astrocytoma even without necrosis or microvascular proliferation","B":"CNS WHO grade 4, since homozygous CDKN2A deletion is itself sufficient to assign the highest grade to an IDH-mutant astrocytoma even without necrosis or microvascular proliferation","C":"CNS WHO grade 3, since homozygous CDKN2A deletion is itself sufficient to assign the highest grade to an IDH-mutant astrocytoma even without necrosis or microvascular proliferation","D":"This tumor cannot be graded without first identifying necrosis or microvascular proliferation on repeat histologic sampling of the specimen","E":"CNS WHO grade 4, since homozygous CDKN2A deletion instead reclassifies this tumor as IDH-wildtype glioblastoma regardless of its confirmed IDH1 mutation status"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Under the 2021 WHO criteria, homozygous deletion of CDKN2A, a tumor suppressor gene, is itself a sufficient molecular criterion to assign CNS WHO grade 4 to an IDH-mutant astrocytoma, even in the absence of the classic high-grade histologic features of necrosis or microvascular proliferation, reflecting the modern shift toward molecular-inclusive grading rather than histology alone. Assigning grade 2 or grade 3 ignores the specific, well-established grade 4-defining significance of homozygous CDKN2A deletion in this molecular context. The tumor can, in fact, be definitively graded using this molecular finding alone, so claiming that grading is impossible without additional histologic sampling is incorrect. Homozygous CDKN2A deletion does not reclassify an IDH-mutant tumor as IDH-wildtype glioblastoma, IDH mutational status remains the primary criterion separating these two tumor categories, and this tumor unambiguously remains an IDH-mutant astrocytoma, just now assigned the highest grade within that category.","boardPrep":"Step 1: homozygous CDKN2A deletion as an independent, sufficient grade 4-defining molecular criterion for IDH-mutant astrocytoma is a nuanced but increasingly tested extension of the integrated molecular grading system. COMLEX: this molecular grading criterion carries direct prognostic and treatment-intensity implications, similar to the histologic grade 4 features it can substitute for.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 47 → Obj 4 — integrated molecular glioma classification, tested here as a board-style biomarker and nomenclature vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl47_bloom_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Compare the molecular and pathologic features of astrocytoma, oligodendroglioma, and glioblastoma using modern integrated diagnostic terminology.","batch":3,"stem":"A pathology resident notes that CNS tumor grades were previously reported using Roman numerals, such as grade III, and that a tumor's final diagnosis was traditionally based on histologic appearance alone. Which change made under the 2021 WHO classification reflects a fundamental shift in this approach?","choices":{"A":"CNS tumor grading now uses Arabic numerals, but diagnoses are still determined entirely by histologic appearance alone, with molecular testing serving no role in the final integrated diagnosis","B":"CNS tumor grading still uses Roman numerals exclusively, but diagnoses are now determined by an integrated combination of histologic and molecular features rather than histology alone","C":"CNS tumor grading and diagnostic terminology are entirely unchanged from previous editions, with this description simply reflecting a persistent common misconception about the current classification","D":"CNS tumor grading now uses Arabic numerals, and molecular features are used only to confirm a diagnosis already fully established by histology, never to independently establish it","E":"CNS tumor grading now uses Arabic numerals and diagnoses are determined by an integrated combination of histologic and molecular features rather than histology alone"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The 2021 WHO classification introduced two related but distinct changes: CNS tumor grades are now expressed using Arabic numerals, grade 1 through 4, rather than Roman numerals, and, more fundamentally, final tumor diagnoses are now determined by an integrated combination of histologic appearance and molecular or genetic features, rather than by histology alone, exemplified by IDH mutational status separating glioblastoma from astrocytoma and by molecular features like CDKN2A deletion independently establishing grade. Claiming molecular testing plays no role in the final diagnosis contradicts the central, defining shift of this classification edition. Claiming Roman numerals are still used exclusively misstates the actual, well-documented change to Arabic numerals. This is not simply an unchanged, persistent misconception, the shift to integrated molecular diagnosis represents a genuine, substantial change in CNS tumor classification. Claiming molecular features are used only to confirm, never to independently establish, a diagnosis understates their role, since molecular findings can independently define both diagnosis, IDH status, and grade, CDKN2A deletion, even without classic histologic features being present.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the 2021 WHO shift to Arabic-numeral grading and integrated histologic-plus-molecular diagnosis is the overarching conceptual framework underlying every specific molecular criterion tested throughout this objective. COMLEX: staying current with this nomenclature shift is directly relevant to interpreting modern pathology reports, which now routinely list molecular alterations alongside histologic grade.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 47 → Obj 4 — integrated molecular glioma classification, tested here as a board-style biomarker and nomenclature vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"}],"48":[{"id":"sdl48_q01","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Interpret characteristic imaging and histopathologic findings to identify major adult intracranial tumors, including glioblastoma, meningioma, vestibular schwannoma, CNS lymphoma, and hemangioblastoma.","batch":1,"stem":"A dural-based mass shows a whorled pattern of tumor cell growth on histology, with numerous concentric calcified psammoma bodies scattered throughout. Which adult intracranial tumor does this histologic description identify?","choices":{"A":"Glioblastoma, an astrocytic tumor","B":"Meningioma, a meningothelial tumor","C":"Vestibular schwannoma, a Schwann cell tumor","D":"Primary CNS lymphoma, a B-cell tumor","E":"Hemangioblastoma, a vascular tumor"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A whorled growth pattern with numerous psammoma bodies, concentric rings of calcification deposited over preexisting cell whorls, is the classic histologic signature of the transitional and psammomatous subtypes of meningioma, a dural-based tumor of meningothelial origin. Glioblastoma is an intra-axial, infiltrative glial tumor showing palisading necrosis and microvascular proliferation rather than whorls and psammoma bodies. Vestibular schwannoma shows Antoni A and Antoni B histologic patterns rather than whorls and calcified psammoma bodies. Primary CNS lymphoma shows sheets of malignant lymphoid cells with perivascular accumulation rather than a whorled, calcified pattern. Hemangioblastoma shows numerous thin-walled capillaries with vacuolated stromal cells rather than whorls and psammoma bodies.","boardPrep":"Step 1: whorled architecture plus psammoma bodies is one of the most reliably tested single-image histologic clues for meningioma. COMLEX: psammomatous meningiomas are especially common at the spinal level, where they also show a strong female predominance.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 47 → Obj 4 — glioblastoma's molecular signature already built there, now placed alongside the full adult intracranial tumor differential and each tumor's distinguishing imaging/histologic clue","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q02","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Interpret characteristic imaging and histopathologic findings to identify major adult intracranial tumors, including glioblastoma, meningioma, vestibular schwannoma, CNS lymphoma, and hemangioblastoma.","batch":1,"stem":"A tumor arising within the cerebellopontine angle produces alternating Antoni A and Antoni B histologic patterns and stains strongly positive for S100 protein. Which adult intracranial tumor does this histologic description identify?","choices":{"A":"Meningioma, a meningothelial dural tumor","B":"Glioblastoma, an astrocytic glial tumor","C":"Primary CNS lymphoma, a B-cell tumor","D":"Hemangioblastoma, a highly vascular tumor","E":"Vestibular schwannoma, a Schwann cell tumor"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Vestibular schwannoma classically arises from Schwann cells of the vestibular division of cranial nerve VIII within the cerebellopontine angle, and its defining histology shows alternating compact, spindled Antoni A areas and looser, myxoid Antoni B areas, with strong S100 immunoreactivity confirming Schwann cell origin. Meningioma shows a whorled pattern with psammoma bodies rather than alternating Antoni A and B zones, and it is not the tumor classically producing this specific cerebellopontine angle S100-positive histology. Glioblastoma shows palisading necrosis and microvascular proliferation and does not stain diffusely for S100 in this characteristic manner. Primary CNS lymphoma shows sheets of malignant lymphoid cells expressing B-cell markers such as CD20, not the Antoni A/B pattern or S100 positivity described here. Hemangioblastoma shows vacuolated stromal cells within a highly vascular network and stains for inhibin rather than S100 in this classic manner.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cerebellopontine angle location plus alternating Antoni A/Antoni B histology plus S100 positivity is the complete classic triad for vestibular schwannoma. COMLEX: bilateral vestibular schwannomas are essentially pathognomonic for neurofibromatosis type 2 and should always prompt genetic evaluation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 47 → Obj 4 — glioblastoma's molecular signature already built there, now placed alongside the full adult intracranial tumor differential and each tumor's distinguishing imaging/histologic clue","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q03","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Interpret characteristic imaging and histopathologic findings to identify major adult intracranial tumors, including glioblastoma, meningioma, vestibular schwannoma, CNS lymphoma, and hemangioblastoma.","batch":1,"stem":"A cerebellar mass consists of a mural nodule with numerous thin-walled capillaries and stromal cells that have vacuolated, lipid-rich cytoplasm; the stromal cells stain positive for erythropoietin, vascular endothelial growth factor, and inhibin. Which adult intracranial tumor does this histologic description identify?","choices":{"A":"Meningioma","B":"Vestibular schwannoma","C":"Glioblastoma","D":"Hemangioblastoma","E":"Primary CNS lymphoma"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hemangioblastoma classically forms a highly vascular mural nodule associated with a large cyst, most often in the cerebellum, composed of numerous thin-walled capillaries and neoplastic stromal cells with vacuolated, lipid-rich cytoplasm that stain positive for erythropoietin, vascular endothelial growth factor, and inhibin, a diagnostically useful marker for this tumor. Meningioma shows a whorled pattern with psammoma bodies rather than a highly vascular, cystic mural nodule with vacuolated stromal cells. Vestibular schwannoma shows Antoni A and Antoni B patterns with S100 positivity rather than this vascular, inhibin-positive stromal cell population. Glioblastoma shows palisading necrosis with microvascular proliferation, a different vascular pattern from the numerous distinct thin-walled capillaries characteristic of hemangioblastoma, and it does not stain for inhibin or produce erythropoietin. Primary CNS lymphoma shows sheets of malignant B cells rather than a vascular, cystic architecture with lipid-laden stromal cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a cystic cerebellar mass with a highly vascular mural nodule, inhibin-positive stromal cells, and secondary erythropoietin-driven polycythemia is the classic hemangioblastoma triad, especially when found in a patient with von Hippel-Lindau disease. COMLEX: roughly 10 percent of hemangioblastoma patients develop polycythemia from tumor erythropoietin overproduction, a useful laboratory clue.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 47 → Obj 4 — glioblastoma's molecular signature already built there, now placed alongside the full adult intracranial tumor differential and each tumor's distinguishing imaging/histologic clue","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q04","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Differentiate major pediatric tumors, including pilocytic astrocytoma, medulloblastoma, ependymoma, craniopharyngioma, and diffuse midline glioma, by age, location, presentation, and pathology.","batch":1,"stem":"A densely cellular cerebellar tumor in a child shows sheets of small, primitive cells with scant cytoplasm and hyperchromatic, crescent-shaped nuclei, forming Homer Wright rosettes, with a strong tendency to disseminate through the cerebrospinal fluid. Which pediatric tumor does this description identify?","choices":{"A":"Pilocytic astrocytoma","B":"Medulloblastoma","C":"Ependymoma","D":"Craniopharyngioma","E":"Diffuse midline glioma"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Medulloblastoma is a malignant embryonal tumor occurring exclusively in the cerebellum, composed of densely packed, primitive-appearing small cells with hyperchromatic, often crescent-shaped nuclei that form Homer Wright rosettes, and it has a strong propensity to disseminate through the subarachnoid space via the cerebrospinal fluid, producing drop metastases along the neuraxis. Pilocytic astrocytoma is a well-circumscribed, cystic, slow-growing CNS WHO grade 1 tumor with Rosenthal fibers rather than the densely cellular, Homer Wright rosette pattern described here. Ependymoma shows perivascular pseudorosettes and true rosettes with fibrillary processes rather than Homer Wright rosettes and does not show this same primitive small-cell morphology. Craniopharyngioma arises in the suprasellar region rather than the cerebellum and shows epithelial, often calcified, architecture rather than densely packed primitive small cells. Diffuse midline glioma arises in midline structures such as the pons, thalamus, or spinal cord and shows infiltrative astrocytic morphology rather than this specific Homer Wright rosette pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Homer Wright rosettes in a densely cellular cerebellar small round blue cell tumor of childhood is the classic histologic signature of medulloblastoma, and its strong tendency for CSF dissemination is a frequently tested clinical feature. COMLEX: Wnt-activated medulloblastomas carry a favorable prognosis, near 100 percent five-year survival, in sharp contrast to the more aggressive SHH-TP53-mutant and group 3 subtypes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 47 → Obj 1 — the pediatric posterior fossa epidemiologic pattern already established there, now resolved into the specific named pediatric tumors that produce it","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q05","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Differentiate major pediatric tumors, including pilocytic astrocytoma, medulloblastoma, ependymoma, craniopharyngioma, and diffuse midline glioma, by age, location, presentation, and pathology.","batch":1,"stem":"A suprasellar mass in a child shows a calcified, cystic architecture on imaging, and histology reveals nests of epithelial cells resembling tooth enamel-forming tissue, with the tumor cells harboring a CTNNB1 mutation. Which pediatric tumor does this description identify?","choices":{"A":"Craniopharyngioma","B":"Medulloblastoma","C":"Pilocytic astrocytoma","D":"Ependymoma","E":"Diffuse midline glioma"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Craniopharyngioma arises from remnants of Rathke pouch in the suprasellar region, classically producing a calcified, cystic mass on imaging, and the pediatric adamantinomatous subtype shows epithelial nests resembling the enamel organ of a developing tooth together with a characteristic CTNNB1, beta-catenin, mutation, distinguishing it from the papillary subtype seen in adults, which instead harbors BRAF V600E mutations. Medulloblastoma is restricted to the cerebellum by definition and does not arise in the suprasellar region or show this enamel-like epithelial histology. Pilocytic astrocytoma can occur near the third ventricle but shows a fibrillary, Rosenthal fiber-containing glial architecture rather than the enamel organ-like epithelial pattern described here. Ependymoma most often arises near the fourth ventricle in children and shows perivascular pseudorosettes rather than this specific calcified, enamel-like epithelial pattern. Diffuse midline glioma arises in midline structures such as the pons or thalamus and shows infiltrative astrocytic, not epithelial, morphology.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a calcified, cystic suprasellar mass with enamel organ-like epithelium and a CTNNB1 mutation is the classic pediatric craniopharyngioma pattern, distinct from the BRAF-mutant papillary subtype in adults. COMLEX: craniopharyngioma frequently compresses the optic chiasm and pituitary stalk, producing bitemporal hemianopia and endocrine dysfunction alongside its mass effect.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 47 → Obj 1 — the pediatric posterior fossa epidemiologic pattern already established there, now resolved into the specific named pediatric tumors that produce it","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q06","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Differentiate major pediatric tumors, including pilocytic astrocytoma, medulloblastoma, ependymoma, craniopharyngioma, and diffuse midline glioma, by age, location, presentation, and pathology.","batch":1,"stem":"A mass arising from the floor of the fourth ventricle in a child shows tumor cells with fibrillary processes forming perivascular pseudorosettes, in which a nuclear-free zone of radiating processes surrounds a central blood vessel. Which pediatric tumor does this description identify?","choices":{"A":"Medulloblastoma, an embryonal tumor","B":"Pilocytic astrocytoma, a glial tumor","C":"Craniopharyngioma, an epithelial tumor","D":"Ependymoma, an ependymal tumor","E":"Diffuse midline glioma, a glial tumor"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Ependymoma most often arises near the fourth ventricle in children, and its defining histologic feature is the perivascular pseudorosette, a nuclear-free zone of thin ependymal processes radiating toward a central blood vessel, sometimes accompanied by true rosettes resembling the embryologic ependymal canal. Medulloblastoma shows Homer Wright rosettes and densely packed primitive small cells rather than perivascular pseudorosettes radiating around a central vessel. Pilocytic astrocytoma shows a biphasic architecture with Rosenthal fibers rather than perivascular pseudorosettes, and while it can occur near the fourth ventricle region, this specific histologic pattern belongs to ependymoma. Craniopharyngioma arises in the suprasellar region, not the fourth ventricle floor, and shows epithelial rather than perivascular pseudorosette architecture. Diffuse midline glioma arises in midline structures such as the pons, thalamus, or spinal cord and shows diffusely infiltrative astrocytic morphology rather than this specific perivascular pseudorosette pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: perivascular pseudorosettes arising from the floor of the fourth ventricle in a child is the classic histologic and locational signature of ependymoma. COMLEX: posterior fossa ependymomas often present with obstructive hydrocephalus from fourth ventricle outflow obstruction, so hydrocephalus symptoms should prompt imaging of this specific region.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 47 → Obj 1 — the pediatric posterior fossa epidemiologic pattern already established there, now resolved into the specific named pediatric tumors that produce it","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q07","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Correlate inherited tumor syndromes with their genes, molecular pathways, and associated CNS tumors.","batch":1,"stem":"A child has multiple café-au-lait macules, Lisch nodules of the iris, and an optic nerve glioma. Mutation of which gene, encoding which protein, best explains this constellation of findings?","choices":{"A":"NF2, encoding merlin","B":"NF1, encoding neurofibromin","C":"TSC2, encoding tuberin","D":"VHL, encoding pVHL","E":"TSC1, encoding hamartin"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This constellation, café-au-lait macules, Lisch nodules of the iris, and optic nerve glioma, is characteristic of neurofibromatosis type 1, caused by mutation of the NF1 gene, which encodes neurofibromin, a tumor suppressor protein that negatively regulates the RAS signaling pathway. NF2, encoding merlin, instead produces bilateral vestibular schwannomas, meningiomas, and spinal ependymomas rather than café-au-lait macules, Lisch nodules, and optic glioma. TSC2, encoding tuberin, causes tuberous sclerosis complex, characterized by cortical tubers, subependymal giant cell astrocytomas, and seizures rather than this specific triad. VHL, encoding the VHL protein, causes von Hippel-Lindau disease, characterized by hemangioblastomas and renal cell carcinoma rather than café-au-lait macules and Lisch nodules. TSC1, encoding hamartin, is the other tuberous sclerosis gene and likewise does not produce this NF1-specific triad of findings.","boardPrep":"Step 1: café-au-lait macules, Lisch nodules, and optic nerve glioma as the classic NF1 triad, caused by neurofibromin loss and resulting RAS pathway disinhibition, is one of the highest-yield inherited tumor syndrome facts tested. COMLEX: NF1 also predisposes to peripheral neurofibromas and an increased lifetime risk of malignant peripheral nerve sheath tumor, findings worth screening for at routine visits.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 8 — vestibular schwannoma already flagged there as a high-yield vestibular condition, now explained through its NF2 genetic basis alongside the other inherited tumor syndromes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q08","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Correlate inherited tumor syndromes with their genes, molecular pathways, and associated CNS tumors.","batch":1,"stem":"An adult has bilateral vestibular schwannomas, multiple meningiomas, and a spinal cord ependymoma. Mutation of which gene, encoding which protein, best explains this constellation of tumors?","choices":{"A":"NF2, encoding merlin","B":"VHL, encoding the VHL protein","C":"NF1, encoding neurofibromin","D":"TSC2, encoding tuberin","E":"TSC1, encoding hamartin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This constellation, bilateral vestibular schwannomas together with multiple meningiomas and spinal cord ependymoma, is characteristic of neurofibromatosis type 2, caused by mutation of the NF2 gene on chromosome 22, which encodes merlin, a tumor suppressor protein whose loss is also found in roughly half of sporadic meningiomas. NF1, encoding neurofibromin, instead produces café-au-lait macules, Lisch nodules, and optic nerve glioma rather than this bilateral schwannoma-meningioma-ependymoma triad. VHL, encoding the VHL protein, causes hemangioblastomas and renal cell carcinoma rather than bilateral vestibular schwannomas. TSC2, encoding tuberin, causes tuberous sclerosis with cortical tubers and subependymal giant cell astrocytomas rather than this NF2-specific tumor triad. TSC1, encoding hamartin, is likewise a tuberous sclerosis gene and does not explain bilateral vestibular schwannomas.","boardPrep":"Step 1: bilateral vestibular schwannomas as essentially pathognomonic for NF2, together with the associated meningiomas and spinal ependymomas from merlin loss, is a heavily tested syndrome-gene-tumor pairing. COMLEX: the same NF2/merlin pathway is mutated in 50 to 60 percent of sporadic meningiomas, linking the inherited and sporadic forms of this tumor at the molecular level.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 8 — vestibular schwannoma already flagged there as a high-yield vestibular condition, now explained through its NF2 genetic basis alongside the other inherited tumor syndromes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q09","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Correlate inherited tumor syndromes with their genes, molecular pathways, and associated CNS tumors.","batch":1,"stem":"A child with seizures, autism spectrum features, and hypopigmented ash-leaf skin macules is found to have cortical tubers and a subependymal giant cell astrocytoma. Mutation of which pathway component, downstream of the TSC1/TSC2 gene complex, best explains the excessive cell growth seen in this tumor?","choices":{"A":"The mTOR kinase, which is normally inhibited by the hamartin-tuberin complex and drives unregulated cell growth when this inhibition is lost","B":"The RAS signaling pathway, which is normally inhibited by the hamartin-tuberin complex and drives unregulated cell growth when this inhibition is lost","C":"The hypoxia-inducible factor pathway, which is normally inhibited by the hamartin-tuberin complex and drives unregulated cell growth when this inhibition is lost","D":"The Wnt/beta-catenin pathway, which is normally inhibited by the hamartin-tuberin complex and drives unregulated cell growth when this inhibition is lost","E":"The sonic hedgehog pathway, which is normally inhibited by the hamartin-tuberin complex and drives unregulated cell growth when this inhibition is lost"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The TSC1 and TSC2 gene products, hamartin and tuberin, normally form a complex that inhibits the mTOR kinase, a key regulator of protein synthesis and cell growth, so mutations disrupting this complex lead to unregulated mTOR activity, explaining the voluminous cytoplasm and excessive growth characteristic of subependymal giant cell astrocytoma and the other hamartomatous lesions of tuberous sclerosis complex. The RAS pathway is instead the target of neurofibromin loss in neurofibromatosis type 1, not the hamartin-tuberin complex in tuberous sclerosis. The hypoxia-inducible factor pathway is regulated by the VHL protein in von Hippel-Lindau disease, not by hamartin-tuberin. The Wnt/beta-catenin pathway is implicated in a molecular subtype of medulloblastoma and in craniopharyngioma, not in the mechanism of tuberous sclerosis complex. The sonic hedgehog pathway is likewise implicated in a different molecular subtype of medulloblastoma, not in tuberous sclerosis pathogenesis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the hamartin-tuberin complex as a physiologic brake on mTOR, and its loss in tuberous sclerosis complex, is a high-yield mechanistic link between an inherited tumor syndrome and a specific druggable signaling pathway. COMLEX: mTOR inhibitors have produced dramatic clinical improvement in some tuberous sclerosis complex patients, a direct therapeutic application of this exact mechanism.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 8 — vestibular schwannoma already flagged there as a high-yield vestibular condition, now explained through its NF2 genetic basis alongside the other inherited tumor syndromes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q10","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Apply patient age, tumor location, imaging characteristics, histology, and molecular markers to select the most likely diagnosis in a COMLEX Level 1 clinical vignette.","batch":1,"stem":"When working through a board vignette describing a new intracranial mass, which single piece of information should typically be weighed first, before imaging pattern or histology, to narrow the differential most efficiently?","choices":{"A":"The specific enhancement pattern on contrast imaging, since it immediately separates the pediatric posterior fossa tumor differential from the adult supratentorial tumor differential","B":"The presence or absence of calcification on imaging, since it immediately separates the pediatric posterior fossa tumor differential from the adult supratentorial tumor differential","C":"The patient's age, since it immediately separates the pediatric posterior fossa tumor differential from the adult supratentorial tumor differential","D":"The patient's reported symptom duration in weeks, since it immediately separates the pediatric posterior fossa tumor differential from the adult supratentorial tumor differential","E":"The presence or absence of a headache as the presenting symptom, since it immediately separates the pediatric posterior fossa tumor differential from the adult supratentorial tumor differential"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Patient age is typically the single most efficient starting filter in a board vignette, since it immediately separates the pediatric differential, dominated by posterior fossa tumors such as pilocytic astrocytoma, medulloblastoma, and ependymoma, along with midline tumors such as craniopharyngioma and diffuse midline glioma, from the adult differential, dominated by supratentorial tumors such as glioblastoma, meningioma, metastasis, and primary CNS lymphoma, before imaging or histologic details are even considered. Enhancement pattern is a useful subsequent discriminator but does not by itself separate the pediatric from the adult differential the way age does. Calcification is a helpful supporting clue, present in oligodendroglioma and craniopharyngioma among others, but it is not the primary age-based filter that most efficiently narrows the initial differential. Symptom duration can suggest tumor grade or growth rate but does not itself separate the pediatric from the adult tumor differential. Headache is a nonspecific symptom common to virtually all mass lesions and does not provide the same immediate, efficient narrowing that patient age does.","boardPrep":"Step 1: starting a tumor vignette by anchoring on patient age before moving to location, imaging, and histology is the single most efficient board-vignette strategy, since it immediately halves the relevant differential. COMLEX: this same age-first strategy applies broadly across COMLEX neurology vignettes, not just intracranial tumors, since many CNS diseases show strong age-dependent epidemiology.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 5 — the board-vignette localization algorithm already built for midbrain anatomy, now applied to the full age-location-imaging-histology-molecular framework for intracranial tumor diagnosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q11","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Apply patient age, tumor location, imaging characteristics, histology, and molecular markers to select the most likely diagnosis in a COMLEX Level 1 clinical vignette.","batch":1,"stem":"After age has been used to narrow a board vignette's tumor differential to the correct age group, which piece of information should typically be applied next, before imaging or molecular markers, to narrow the differential further?","choices":{"A":"The patient's sex, since specific tumor types show strong predilections for particular anatomic sites within each age group","B":"The presence of a family history of cancer, since specific tumor types show strong predilections for particular anatomic sites within each age group","C":"Tumor location within the neuraxis, since specific tumor types show strong predilections for particular anatomic sites within each age group","D":"The specific chemotherapy regimen previously used, since specific tumor types show strong predilections for particular anatomic sites within each age group","E":"The patient's occupational history, since specific tumor types show strong predilections for particular anatomic sites within each age group"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Once age has established the correct broad category, tumor location within the neuraxis, cerebellum versus cerebral hemispheres versus suprasellar region versus fourth ventricle versus cerebellopontine angle, is the next most efficient discriminator, since specific tumor types show strong locational predilections within each age group, such as medulloblastoma in the cerebellar vermis or craniopharyngioma in the suprasellar region. Family history of cancer is relevant primarily when an inherited tumor syndrome is suspected, but it is not the general next-step discriminator applied to every vignette after age. Patient sex has only a modest influence on tumor type, such as the female predominance of meningioma, and does not provide the same broad, reliable narrowing that location does. Prior chemotherapy would only be relevant in specific scenarios such as therapy-related tumors and is not a routinely applied second-step discriminator in a new tumor vignette. Occupational history is occasionally relevant for certain environmental exposures but is not the standard second-step discriminator after age in intracranial tumor vignettes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the age-then-location sequence is the backbone strategy for efficiently narrowing intracranial tumor vignettes before ever reaching imaging or molecular details. COMLEX: combining age and location alone is often sufficient to reach a single most-likely diagnosis without needing histology or molecular markers at all.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 5 — the board-vignette localization algorithm already built for midbrain anatomy, now applied to the full age-location-imaging-histology-molecular framework for intracranial tumor diagnosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q12","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Apply patient age, tumor location, imaging characteristics, histology, and molecular markers to select the most likely diagnosis in a COMLEX Level 1 clinical vignette.","batch":1,"stem":"A board vignette provides a patient's age, tumor location, imaging enhancement pattern, and a specific immunohistochemical or genetic marker, all pointing toward the same single diagnosis. Which approach best reflects how these pieces of information should be integrated to reach that diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Only the molecular or immunohistochemical marker should be trusted, since age, location, and imaging findings are considered unreliable once a specific marker is provided","B":"Only the imaging enhancement pattern should be trusted, since age, location, and molecular findings are considered unreliable once an imaging description is provided","C":"Each piece of information should be treated as pointing to a separate, coexisting tumor, since a single tumor cannot simultaneously produce this many distinct findings","D":"Only the patient's age should be trusted, since location, imaging, and molecular findings are considered unreliable once a specific age is provided","E":"Each piece of information should be treated as converging, mutually reinforcing evidence for a single diagnosis, rather than as independent clues each pointing to a different tumor"},"correct":"E","explanation":"When a well-constructed vignette provides age, location, imaging pattern, and a molecular or immunohistochemical marker together, these should be treated as converging, mutually reinforcing lines of evidence that all point toward the same single diagnosis, exactly the integrative reasoning skill this objective is designed to build, since real intracranial tumors reliably show consistent age, location, imaging, and molecular profiles together. Trusting only the molecular marker while discarding age, location, and imaging discards genuinely useful corroborating information and is not how integrated diagnosis works. Trusting only the imaging pattern while discarding the other categories likewise discards useful corroborating information unnecessarily. Treating each finding as evidence of a separate, coexisting tumor misunderstands the vignette structure, since these details are designed to converge on one diagnosis, not describe multiple simultaneous tumors. Trusting only the patient's age while discarding location, imaging, and molecular findings ignores the substantial diagnostic value those additional details provide beyond age alone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: recognizing that age, location, imaging, and molecular markers are meant to converge on one diagnosis, not compete with one another, is the core integrative skill tested by the most detailed intracranial tumor vignettes. COMLEX: when a vignette's details seem to conflict, it is more often a sign of misreading one detail than evidence of two coexisting tumors.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 5 — the board-vignette localization algorithm already built for midbrain anatomy, now applied to the full age-location-imaging-histology-molecular framework for intracranial tumor diagnosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Interpret characteristic imaging and histopathologic findings to identify major adult intracranial tumors, including glioblastoma, meningioma, vestibular schwannoma, CNS lymphoma, and hemangioblastoma.","batch":2,"stem":"54-year-old woman -> incidental finding on MRI obtained after a minor head injury -> a well-circumscribed, dural-based, homogeneously enhancing mass along the parasagittal convexity, with a thin rim of adjacent dural enhancement described as a dural tail -> patient is entirely asymptomatic. Which imaging feature described here is most specifically characteristic of this tumor type, and what is the single most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"The dural tail sign, an imaging feature specific to extra-axial, dural-based tumors, most consistent with glioblastoma","B":"The homogeneous enhancement pattern alone, a feature specific to extra-axial, dural-based tumors, most consistent with vestibular schwannoma","C":"The dural tail sign, an imaging feature specific to extra-axial, dural-based tumors, most consistent with meningioma","D":"The parasagittal location alone, a feature specific to extra-axial, dural-based tumors, most consistent with primary CNS lymphoma","E":"The dural tail sign, an imaging feature specific to extra-axial, dural-based tumors, most consistent with hemangioblastoma"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The dural tail sign, a thin rim of enhancing dura tapering away from an extra-axial mass, reflects reactive dural thickening adjacent to a dural-based tumor and is most characteristic of meningioma, matching this patient's incidentally discovered, asymptomatic, well-circumscribed parasagittal convexity mass. Glioblastoma is an intra-axial, infiltrative tumor rather than an extra-axial, dural-based one, so it would not classically produce a dural tail sign. Vestibular schwannoma arises in the cerebellopontine angle rather than the parasagittal convexity, and while it can enhance homogeneously, this location and the dural tail sign together specifically favor meningioma. Primary CNS lymphoma is an intra-axial, periventricular tumor rather than a dural-based, parasagittal convexity mass with a dural tail sign. Hemangioblastoma is an intra-axial cerebellar tumor with a cystic mural nodule rather than a dural-based convexity mass with this specific dural tail imaging feature.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the dural tail sign as a classic, specific imaging clue for meningioma, together with its common presentation as an asymptomatic incidental finding, is a frequently tested imaging-plus-epidemiology pairing. COMLEX: most meningiomas require no intervention beyond periodic imaging surveillance when found incidentally and asymptomatic in an otherwise healthy patient.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 48 Obj 8 — This same age-location-imaging convergence is the exact integrative reasoning pattern formalized under the board vignette algorithm objective","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Interpret characteristic imaging and histopathologic findings to identify major adult intracranial tumors, including glioblastoma, meningioma, vestibular schwannoma, CNS lymphoma, and hemangioblastoma.","batch":2,"stem":"58-year-old man -> six months of progressive unilateral hearing loss and tinnitus, followed more recently by unsteady gait -> exam shows sensorineural hearing loss and mild facial hypesthesia on the affected side -> MRI shows an enhancing cerebellopontine angle mass with an ice-cream-cone-shaped extension into the internal auditory canal. Which cranial nerves are most likely compressed to produce this specific combination of findings, and what is the single most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Cranial nerves VIII and V, compressed as the mass extends from the internal auditory canal into the cerebellopontine angle, most consistent with meningioma","B":"Cranial nerves VII and IX, compressed as the mass extends from the internal auditory canal into the cerebellopontine angle, most consistent with vestibular schwannoma","C":"Cranial nerves VIII and V, compressed as the mass extends from the internal auditory canal into the cerebellopontine angle, most consistent with vestibular schwannoma","D":"Cranial nerves III and IV, compressed as the mass extends from the internal auditory canal into the cerebellopontine angle, most consistent with vestibular schwannoma","E":"Cranial nerves VIII and V, compressed as the mass extends from the internal auditory canal into the cerebellopontine angle, most consistent with hemangioblastoma"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This presentation, progressive unilateral sensorineural hearing loss and tinnitus from vestibulocochlear nerve compression, followed by trigeminal-distribution facial hypesthesia and gait imbalance from cerebellopontine angle mass effect, together with an ice-cream-cone-shaped mass extending into the internal auditory canal, is the classic presentation of vestibular schwannoma compressing cranial nerves VIII and V. Meningioma can also occur in the cerebellopontine angle, but the ice-cream-cone extension into the internal auditory canal specifically is the classic imaging signature of vestibular schwannoma rather than meningioma. Cranial nerves VII and IX are not the nerves whose compression produces this specific hearing loss and facial hypesthesia pattern, and facial nerve involvement is typically a late finding in vestibular schwannoma given its more resistant course compared to the vestibulocochlear nerve. Cranial nerves III and IV govern eye movements and are not affected by a cerebellopontine angle mass in this manner, making this an inconsistent pairing with the described findings. Hemangioblastoma is a vascular cerebellar parenchymal tumor rather than a cerebellopontine angle mass extending into the internal auditory canal, making it an inconsistent diagnosis here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: progressive unilateral sensorineural hearing loss as the earliest symptom of a cerebellopontine angle mass, with the ice-cream-cone internal auditory canal extension on imaging, is the single most classic vestibular schwannoma vignette. COMLEX: any patient with new, unexplained unilateral hearing loss deserves imaging of the internal auditory canal and cerebellopontine angle to exclude this diagnosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 8 — Vestibular schwannoma already flagged there as a high-yield vestibular condition, now given its full imaging and cranial nerve compression vignette","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Interpret characteristic imaging and histopathologic findings to identify major adult intracranial tumors, including glioblastoma, meningioma, vestibular schwannoma, CNS lymphoma, and hemangioblastoma.","batch":2,"stem":"35-year-old man -> known family history of retinal and cerebellar tumors across three generations -> new-onset headache and ataxia -> MRI shows a cystic cerebellar mass with an enhancing mural nodule -> laboratory studies incidentally reveal an elevated hemoglobin and hematocrit. Which underlying mechanism best explains the laboratory finding, and what is the single most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Tumor overproduction of erythropoietin, most consistent with a cerebellar hemangioblastoma occurring as part of von Hippel-Lindau disease","B":"Tumor overproduction of erythropoietin, most consistent with a cerebellar pilocytic astrocytoma occurring as part of von Hippel-Lindau disease","C":"Chronic hypoxia from elevated intracranial pressure, most consistent with a cerebellar hemangioblastoma occurring as part of von Hippel-Lindau disease","D":"Iatrogenic corticosteroid use for symptom management, most consistent with a cerebellar hemangioblastoma occurring as part of von Hippel-Lindau disease","E":"Tumor overproduction of erythropoietin, most consistent with a cerebellar medulloblastoma occurring as part of von Hippel-Lindau disease"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This patient's family history spanning multiple generations, cystic cerebellar mass with an enhancing mural nodule, and secondary polycythemia together point to cerebellar hemangioblastoma occurring as part of von Hippel-Lindau disease, in which loss of the VHL tumor suppressor protein leads to unchecked hypoxia-inducible factor activity, driving overexpression of erythropoietin by the neoplastic stromal cells and producing polycythemia in roughly 10 percent of affected patients. Pilocytic astrocytoma is not the tumor associated with von Hippel-Lindau disease or with erythropoietin overproduction, making this an inconsistent pairing despite the correct mechanism description. Chronic hypoxia from elevated intracranial pressure is not the actual mechanism of polycythemia in hemangioblastoma, which instead results from direct tumor erythropoietin production rather than a secondary hypoxic response. Corticosteroid use does not cause polycythemia and is not the mechanism at play in this inherited tumor syndrome. Medulloblastoma is not the tumor type associated with von Hippel-Lindau disease, which is specifically linked to hemangioblastoma, renal cell carcinoma, and pheochromocytoma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hemangioblastoma-associated polycythemia from direct tumor erythropoietin production is a classic paraneoplastic-style laboratory finding tied to von Hippel-Lindau disease. COMLEX: von Hippel-Lindau disease also predisposes to renal cell carcinoma and pheochromocytoma, so any patient with a cerebellar hemangioblastoma warrants systemic screening for these additional tumors.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 48 → Obj 7 — Von Hippel-Lindau disease and its VHL gene mechanism are detailed in full under the inherited tumor syndrome objective, of which this hemangioblastoma is a direct clinical consequence","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Differentiate major pediatric tumors, including pilocytic astrocytoma, medulloblastoma, ependymoma, craniopharyngioma, and diffuse midline glioma, by age, location, presentation, and pathology.","batch":2,"stem":"4-year-old boy -> three months of vomiting, most pronounced in the morning, and increasing unsteadiness while walking -> exam shows truncal ataxia, papilledema, and no cranial nerve deficits -> MRI shows a densely cellular, midline cerebellar vermis mass extending into and partially filling the fourth ventricle, with punctate areas of restricted diffusion. Which pediatric tumor best fits this presentation, and what accounts for the truncal, rather than limb, predominance of his ataxia?","choices":{"A":"Pilocytic astrocytoma, with truncal ataxia reflecting the tumor's origin in the midline cerebellar vermis, which governs axial and truncal coordination","B":"Ependymoma, with truncal ataxia reflecting the tumor's origin in the midline cerebellar vermis, which governs axial and truncal coordination","C":"Craniopharyngioma, with truncal ataxia reflecting the tumor's origin in the midline cerebellar vermis, which governs axial and truncal coordination","D":"Diffuse midline glioma, with truncal ataxia reflecting the tumor's origin in the midline cerebellar vermis, which governs axial and truncal coordination","E":"Medulloblastoma, with truncal ataxia reflecting the tumor's origin in the midline cerebellar vermis, which governs axial and truncal coordination"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This presentation, a densely cellular midline cerebellar vermis mass with restricted diffusion in a young child, obstructive hydrocephalus symptoms, and truncal ataxia, fits medulloblastoma, and the truncal predominance of his ataxia reflects the tumor's vermian, rather than lateral hemispheric, origin, since the vermis governs axial and truncal coordination while the cerebellar hemispheres govern appendicular limb coordination. Pilocytic astrocytoma is typically a well-circumscribed cystic mass with a mural nodule rather than this densely cellular, restricted-diffusion pattern, making it a less precise fit for this specific imaging description. Ependymoma more typically arises from the floor of the fourth ventricle itself rather than the cerebellar vermis, and it does not classically show this degree of diffusion restriction reflecting the extremely high cellularity of medulloblastoma. Craniopharyngioma arises in the suprasellar region, not the cerebellar vermis, and would not produce truncal ataxia through this mechanism. Diffuse midline glioma most often arises in the pons, thalamus, or spinal cord rather than the cerebellar vermis specifically, making it a less precise fit for this vermian mass description.","boardPrep":"Step 1: truncal, rather than appendicular, ataxia localizing specifically to the cerebellar vermis rather than the hemispheres is a classic anatomic reasoning point layered onto the medulloblastoma vignette. COMLEX: restricted diffusion on MRI reflects medulloblastoma's extremely high cellularity and is a useful imaging clue distinguishing it from other posterior fossa pediatric tumors.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 4 — Vermal versus hemispheric cerebellar lesion patterns already built there for vascular and degenerative disease, now extended to this classic pediatric tumor of the vermis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Differentiate major pediatric tumors, including pilocytic astrocytoma, medulloblastoma, ependymoma, craniopharyngioma, and diffuse midline glioma, by age, location, presentation, and pathology.","batch":2,"stem":"9-year-old girl -> six months of headache, decreased growth velocity noted at her pediatrician visits, and new bitemporal visual field loss on formal testing -> MRI shows a calcified, cystic suprasellar mass compressing the optic chiasm from below -> laboratory studies show low growth hormone and low cortisol levels. Which pediatric tumor best fits this presentation, and why does it produce both visual field loss and this specific pattern of endocrine dysfunction together?","choices":{"A":"Craniopharyngioma, which compresses the optic chiasm from below to produce bitemporal hemianopia while also compressing the pituitary stalk and gland to produce anterior pituitary hormone deficiency","B":"Medulloblastoma, which compresses the optic chiasm from below to produce bitemporal hemianopia while also compressing the pituitary stalk and gland to produce anterior pituitary hormone deficiency","C":"Pilocytic astrocytoma of the cerebellum, which compresses the optic chiasm from below to produce bitemporal hemianopia while also compressing the pituitary stalk and gland to produce anterior pituitary hormone deficiency","D":"Ependymoma, which compresses the optic chiasm from below to produce bitemporal hemianopia while also compressing the pituitary stalk and gland to produce anterior pituitary hormone deficiency","E":"Diffuse midline glioma, which compresses the optic chiasm from below to produce bitemporal hemianopia while also compressing the pituitary stalk and gland to produce anterior pituitary hormone deficiency"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Craniopharyngioma arises from Rathke pouch remnants in the suprasellar region, immediately below the optic chiasm and adjacent to the pituitary stalk and gland, so its growth classically compresses the chiasm from below to produce bitemporal hemianopia while simultaneously disrupting normal pituitary function, producing deficiencies such as decreased growth hormone, causing the growth velocity decline, and decreased cortisol, exactly matching this presentation. Medulloblastoma arises exclusively in the cerebellum and does not occur in the suprasellar region, so it cannot produce this specific combination of chiasmal compression and pituitary dysfunction. Pilocytic astrocytoma of the cerebellum, as specified here, is a posterior fossa tumor and would not produce suprasellar chiasmal or pituitary compression, though pilocytic astrocytoma can rarely occur near the optic pathway itself, that variant is not what is described in this vignette. Ependymoma most often arises near the fourth ventricle and would not produce this specific suprasellar compression pattern. Diffuse midline glioma most often arises in the pons, thalamus, or spinal cord and would not produce this specific combination of bitemporal hemianopia and anterior pituitary hormone deficiency from a suprasellar mass.","boardPrep":"Step 1: craniopharyngioma's suprasellar location explaining both bitemporal hemianopia, from optic chiasm compression, and anterior pituitary hormone deficiency, from pituitary stalk compression, together is a classic combined-finding vignette. COMLEX: growth velocity decline in a child is an important, often overlooked early clue to pituitary dysfunction that should prompt formal endocrine and visual field evaluation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 48 Obj 5 — This same suprasellar calcified imaging pattern is contrasted there against the adult tumor differential, reinforcing why age is the essential first filter in these vignettes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Differentiate major pediatric tumors, including pilocytic astrocytoma, medulloblastoma, ependymoma, craniopharyngioma, and diffuse midline glioma, by age, location, presentation, and pathology.","batch":2,"stem":"6-year-old boy -> two months of progressive double vision, facial asymmetry, and difficulty swallowing, with no headache and no change in behavior or personality -> MRI shows a diffusely infiltrating, poorly marginated mass expanding the pons without significant surrounding edema -> biopsy, obtained as part of a research protocol, shows an astrocytic tumor harboring an H3 K27M mutation. Which pediatric tumor best fits this presentation, and what does the specific combination of cranial nerve findings reflect anatomically?","choices":{"A":"Medulloblastoma, in which diplopia and facial asymmetry reflect direct infiltration of the abducens and facial nerve nuclei within the pons, while dysphagia reflects pontine corticobulbar fibers descending toward the medullary lower cranial nerve nuclei","B":"Diffuse midline glioma, in which diplopia and facial asymmetry reflect direct infiltration of the abducens and facial nerve nuclei within the pons, while dysphagia reflects pontine corticobulbar fibers descending toward the medullary lower cranial nerve nuclei","C":"Pilocytic astrocytoma, in which diplopia and facial asymmetry reflect direct infiltration of the abducens and facial nerve nuclei within the pons, while dysphagia reflects pontine corticobulbar fibers descending toward the medullary lower cranial nerve nuclei","D":"Craniopharyngioma, in which diplopia and facial asymmetry reflect direct infiltration of the abducens and facial nerve nuclei within the pons, while dysphagia reflects pontine corticobulbar fibers descending toward the medullary lower cranial nerve nuclei","E":"Ependymoma, in which diplopia and facial asymmetry reflect direct infiltration of the abducens and facial nerve nuclei within the pons, while dysphagia reflects pontine corticobulbar fibers descending toward the medullary lower cranial nerve nuclei"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This presentation, a diffusely infiltrating pontine mass in a child harboring an H3 K27M mutation, is diagnostic of diffuse midline glioma, previously termed diffuse intrinsic pontine glioma, and the combination of diplopia and facial asymmetry reflects direct tumor infiltration of the abducens nerve fascicles or nucleus and the facial nerve fascicles or nucleus, both of which genuinely reside within the pons, while the dysphagia instead reflects disruption of pontine corticobulbar fibers as they descend toward the lower cranial nerve nuclei, which themselves lie in the medulla rather than the pons. Medulloblastoma arises in the cerebellum rather than diffusely infiltrating the pons itself, and it does not characteristically harbor an H3 K27M mutation, making this an inconsistent diagnosis. Pilocytic astrocytoma is typically a well-circumscribed, cystic tumor rather than this diffusely infiltrating, poorly marginated pontine pattern, and it is not defined by an H3 K27M mutation. Craniopharyngioma arises in the suprasellar region rather than the pons and would not produce this combination of cranial nerve findings from direct pontine infiltration. Ependymoma most often arises near the fourth ventricle rather than diffusely infiltrating the pons itself, and it is not defined by an H3 K27M mutation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: an H3 K27M-mutant, diffusely infiltrating pontine mass in a child, producing multiple ipsilateral cranial nerve palsies from direct fascicular infiltration, is the classic diffuse midline glioma vignette. COMLEX: diffuse midline glioma carries a very poor prognosis due to its infiltrative, unresectable growth pattern within the pons, distinguishing its clinical course sharply from the far more favorable pilocytic astrocytoma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 2 — Pontine cranial nerve nuclei anatomy already established there for the abducens and facial nerves, now the anatomic substrate this diffusely infiltrating tumor directly invades","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q19","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Correlate inherited tumor syndromes with their genes, molecular pathways, and associated CNS tumors.","batch":2,"stem":"16-year-old boy -> multiple soft, painless skin nodules noted since early childhood, now numbering more than a dozen -> exam shows more than six café-au-lait macules greater than 1.5 centimeters in diameter, axillary freckling, and pigmented iris nodules on slit-lamp examination -> MRI shows a fusiform enlargement of the optic nerve. Which gene mutation best explains this patient's complete constellation of findings, and through what molecular mechanism does it predispose to this optic nerve finding specifically?","choices":{"A":"NF2 mutation, in which loss of neurofibromin's normal RAS-inactivating function leads to unchecked RAS signaling and predisposes to optic nerve glioma among other tumors","B":"TSC2 mutation, in which loss of neurofibromin's normal RAS-inactivating function leads to unchecked RAS signaling and predisposes to optic nerve glioma among other tumors","C":"VHL mutation, in which loss of neurofibromin's normal RAS-inactivating function leads to unchecked RAS signaling and predisposes to optic nerve glioma among other tumors","D":"NF1 mutation, in which loss of neurofibromin's normal RAS-inactivating function leads to unchecked RAS signaling and predisposes to optic nerve glioma among other tumors","E":"NF1 mutation, in which loss of merlin's normal cytoskeletal regulatory function leads to unchecked cell proliferation and predisposes to optic nerve glioma among other tumors"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This patient's complete presentation, multiple cutaneous neurofibromas, more than six large café-au-lait macules, axillary freckling, Lisch nodules, and an optic nerve glioma, is diagnostic of neurofibromatosis type 1, caused by NF1 mutation, and the loss of neurofibromin's normal function as a RAS GTPase-activating protein leads to unchecked RAS signaling, driving the characteristic tumor spectrum of this syndrome, including optic nerve glioma. NF2 mutation instead causes loss of merlin and produces bilateral vestibular schwannomas, meningiomas, and spinal ependymomas, not this specific cutaneous and optic nerve phenotype. TSC2 mutation disrupts hamartin-tuberin regulation of mTOR, producing cortical tubers and subependymal giant cell astrocytomas, an entirely different mechanism and phenotype from the one described here. VHL mutation disrupts hypoxia-inducible factor regulation, producing hemangioblastomas and renal cell carcinoma, not this NF1-specific cutaneous and optic nerve presentation. The final option correctly identifies NF1 as the gene but incorrectly attributes the mechanism to merlin loss, which is instead the NF2 gene product, misattributing the molecular mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the complete NF1 diagnostic criteria, multiple neurofibromas, six or more large café-au-lait macules, axillary or inguinal freckling, Lisch nodules, and optic glioma, together with the RAS pathway mechanism, is one of the most detailed and testable inherited tumor syndrome vignettes. COMLEX: optic nerve glioma in NF1 is typically a low-grade, slow-growing pilocytic astrocytoma variant, often managed with observation rather than immediate aggressive treatment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 5 — Optic pathway lesion correlations already built there for other causes of visual field loss, now extended to this specific tumor-syndrome cause of optic nerve enlargement","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q20","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Correlate inherited tumor syndromes with their genes, molecular pathways, and associated CNS tumors.","batch":2,"stem":"29-year-old woman -> recently diagnosed bilateral vestibular schwannomas on screening MRI after her father was found to have the same finding -> further workup reveals a spinal cord mass and two additional small meningiomas -> genetic testing confirms a germline mutation. Which gene is most likely mutated, and why does this same gene's loss also explain the majority of sporadic, nonfamilial meningiomas seen in the general population?","choices":{"A":"NF1, since sporadic meningiomas most commonly arise from acquired, non-inherited mutations in this same tumor suppressor gene, converging on the identical merlin-loss mechanism","B":"TSC1, since sporadic meningiomas most commonly arise from acquired, non-inherited mutations in this same tumor suppressor gene, converging on the identical merlin-loss mechanism","C":"VHL, since sporadic meningiomas most commonly arise from acquired, non-inherited mutations in this same tumor suppressor gene, converging on the identical merlin-loss mechanism","D":"TSC2, since sporadic meningiomas most commonly arise from acquired, non-inherited mutations in this same tumor suppressor gene, converging on the identical merlin-loss mechanism","E":"NF2, since sporadic meningiomas most commonly arise from acquired, non-inherited mutations in this same tumor suppressor gene, converging on the identical merlin-loss mechanism"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This patient's bilateral vestibular schwannomas, spinal cord ependymoma, and multiple meningiomas, together with an autosomal dominant family history, are diagnostic of neurofibromatosis type 2, caused by germline NF2 mutation, and this same gene, which encodes merlin, is also the most commonly acquired somatic mutation in sporadic meningiomas, explaining why 50 to 60 percent of nonfamilial meningiomas also show NF2 loss despite arising outside any inherited syndrome. NF1 causes a distinct phenotype of neurofibromas, café-au-lait macules, and optic glioma through neurofibromin and RAS pathway loss, not the bilateral vestibular schwannoma and meningioma pattern described here, and it is not the gene most commonly mutated in sporadic meningiomas. TSC1 causes tuberous sclerosis through hamartin loss and is not implicated in either this patient's syndrome or in sporadic meningioma pathogenesis. VHL causes hemangioblastomas and renal cell carcinoma through a distinct hypoxia-inducible factor mechanism and is not the gene underlying either this syndrome or most sporadic meningiomas. TSC2 causes tuberous sclerosis through tuberin loss and, like TSC1, is not implicated in this patient's presentation or in sporadic meningioma development.","boardPrep":"Step 1: NF2 mutation as the shared molecular link between the inherited syndrome and the majority of sporadic, nonfamilial meningiomas is a sophisticated but frequently tested integrative genetics fact. COMLEX: first-degree relatives of a patient newly diagnosed with NF2 should be offered genetic counseling and screening imaging given the autosomal dominant inheritance pattern illustrated by this family.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 48 → Obj 5 — Meningioma's classic dural tail imaging sign already established there, now linked at the molecular level to the same NF2/merlin pathway responsible for this patient's inherited tumor syndrome","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q21","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Correlate inherited tumor syndromes with their genes, molecular pathways, and associated CNS tumors.","batch":2,"stem":"11-year-old girl -> new-onset seizures and mild intellectual disability -> exam shows hypopigmented ash-leaf macules on the trunk and a rough, leathery shagreen patch over the lower back -> MRI shows several subependymal nodules and a larger enhancing mass near the foramen of Monro causing early hydrocephalus -> renal ultrasound incidentally shows bilateral angiomyolipomas. Which single finding on MRI indicates that a hamartomatous subependymal nodule has progressed to a true neoplasm requiring closer monitoring, and what is that neoplasm called?","choices":{"A":"The presence of the renal angiomyolipomas on ultrasound indicates transformation into a subependymal giant cell astrocytoma, distinct from the smaller, stable subependymal nodules","B":"The larger, enhancing mass near the foramen of Monro causing hydrocephalus represents transformation into a subependymal giant cell astrocytoma, distinct from the smaller, stable subependymal nodules","C":"The presence of the ash-leaf hypopigmented macules on skin exam indicates transformation into a subependymal giant cell astrocytoma, distinct from the smaller, stable subependymal nodules","D":"The presence of the leathery shagreen patch on the lower back indicates transformation into a subependymal giant cell astrocytoma, distinct from the smaller, stable subependymal nodules","E":"The larger, enhancing mass near the foramen of Monro causing hydrocephalus represents transformation into a cortical tuber, distinct entirely from the smaller, stable subependymal nodules"},"correct":"B","explanation":"While the smaller subependymal nodules seen throughout the ventricular system in tuberous sclerosis complex are typically stable hamartomas, a larger nodule that enlarges, enhances, and produces mass effect such as foramen of Monro obstruction and hydrocephalus indicates transformation into subependymal giant cell astrocytoma, a true, though usually low-grade, neoplasm arising from the same hamartomatous nodules and requiring closer monitoring or intervention. The renal angiomyolipomas are a separate, expected extracranial manifestation of tuberous sclerosis complex and do not themselves indicate neoplastic transformation of a subependymal nodule. The ash-leaf hypopigmented macules are a cutaneous manifestation of tuberous sclerosis complex present from an early age and do not indicate neoplastic transformation of any intracranial lesion. The shagreen patch is likewise a cutaneous finding of tuberous sclerosis complex and does not indicate neoplastic transformation of a subependymal nodule. Cortical tubers are a distinct hamartomatous cortical lesion, typically epileptogenic, rather than the specific periventricular mass causing foramen of Monro obstruction described here, which is instead subependymal giant cell astrocytoma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: recognizing that a growing, enhancing periventricular mass near the foramen of Monro in tuberous sclerosis complex signals transformation into subependymal giant cell astrocytoma is a high-yield distinction between stable hamartomas and true neoplasms within the same syndrome. COMLEX: surgical resection or mTOR inhibitor therapy is indicated for a symptomatic or enlarging subependymal giant cell astrocytoma, unlike the typically observation-only management of stable subependymal nodules.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 8 — The foramen of Monro's role connecting the lateral and third ventricles already established there, now the anatomic bottleneck that explains why a mass at this exact site causes early hydrocephalus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q22","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Apply patient age, tumor location, imaging characteristics, histology, and molecular markers to select the most likely diagnosis in a COMLEX Level 1 clinical vignette.","batch":2,"stem":"3-year-old boy -> two months of early morning vomiting and refusal to walk -> exam shows truncal ataxia and papilledema -> MRI shows a midline cerebellar mass with restricted diffusion, extending into the fourth ventricle, without any calcification -> CSF cytology from a subsequent lumbar puncture reveals malignant cells, consistent with disease spread beyond the primary site. Applying age, location, imaging, and this final clinical finding together, what is the single most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Medulloblastoma, since his young age, cerebellar vermis location, restricted diffusion from high cellularity, and cerebrospinal fluid dissemination all converge on this diagnosis","B":"Pilocytic astrocytoma, since his young age, cerebellar vermis location, restricted diffusion from high cellularity, and cerebrospinal fluid dissemination all converge on this diagnosis","C":"Craniopharyngioma, since his young age, cerebellar vermis location, restricted diffusion from high cellularity, and cerebrospinal fluid dissemination all converge on this diagnosis","D":"Diffuse midline glioma, since his young age, cerebellar vermis location, restricted diffusion from high cellularity, and cerebrospinal fluid dissemination all converge on this diagnosis","E":"Vestibular schwannoma, since his young age, cerebellar vermis location, restricted diffusion from high cellularity, and cerebrospinal fluid dissemination all converge on this diagnosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Applying the full integrative framework, this child's young age, midline cerebellar vermis location, restricted diffusion reflecting extremely high tumor cellularity, and cerebrospinal fluid dissemination on cytology all converge specifically on medulloblastoma, the classic embryonal tumor defined by exactly this combination of features. Pilocytic astrocytoma is typically well-circumscribed and cystic with a mural nodule, rarely shows this degree of diffusion restriction, and does not characteristically disseminate through the cerebrospinal fluid, making it a poor fit despite the correct age and general location. Craniopharyngioma arises in the suprasellar region rather than the cerebellum and does not disseminate through the cerebrospinal fluid, making it an inconsistent diagnosis here. Diffuse midline glioma most often arises in the pons, thalamus, or spinal cord rather than the cerebellar vermis, and while it carries a poor prognosis, it is not classically associated with early cerebrospinal fluid dissemination in the way medulloblastoma is. Vestibular schwannoma arises in the cerebellopontine angle in adults, not the cerebellar vermis in a young child, and does not disseminate through the cerebrospinal fluid, making it entirely inconsistent with this presentation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: integrating age, vermian location, high cellularity on diffusion imaging, and cerebrospinal fluid dissemination together to reach medulloblastoma is a complete worked example of the age-location-imaging-molecular integration strategy this objective is built around. COMLEX: staging with spinal MRI and CSF cytology is standard practice for any confirmed or suspected medulloblastoma given its well-documented propensity for craniospinal dissemination.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 48 → Obj 6 — Medulloblastoma's defining histologic and locational features already established there, now assembled into a single complete integrative board vignette","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q23","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Apply patient age, tumor location, imaging characteristics, histology, and molecular markers to select the most likely diagnosis in a COMLEX Level 1 clinical vignette.","batch":2,"stem":"62-year-old man -> six weeks of headache and rapidly progressive right-sided weakness -> known history of stage III colon cancer treated with resection and chemotherapy three years ago, currently in remission with no evidence of recurrence on recent surveillance imaging -> MRI shows a single, sharply demarcated, ring-enhancing mass at the gray-white junction in the left frontal lobe, with disproportionate surrounding edema. Applying age, tumor history, imaging pattern, and lesion count together, what is the single most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Metastatic disease from his prior colon cancer, since a metastasis-prone primary malignancy history combined with a solitary, sharply demarcated, gray-white junction lesion with disproportionate edema together favor this diagnosis over a new primary tumor","B":"A new primary glioblastoma unrelated to his prior cancer, since a metastasis-prone primary malignancy history combined with a solitary, sharply demarcated, gray-white junction lesion with disproportionate edema together favor this diagnosis over metastatic disease","C":"A new primary oligodendroglioma unrelated to his prior cancer, since a metastasis-prone primary malignancy history combined with a solitary, sharply demarcated, gray-white junction lesion with disproportionate edema together favor this diagnosis over metastatic disease","D":"Primary CNS lymphoma unrelated to his prior cancer, since a metastasis-prone primary malignancy history combined with a solitary, sharply demarcated, gray-white junction lesion with disproportionate edema together favor this diagnosis over metastatic disease","E":"A meningioma unrelated to his prior cancer, since a metastasis-prone primary malignancy history combined with a solitary, sharply demarcated, gray-white junction lesion with disproportionate edema together favor this diagnosis over metastatic disease"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Applying the full framework, this patient's history of a colon cancer, a recognized though less common source of brain metastases, together with the classic sharply demarcated, gray-white junction location and disproportionate edema pattern, together favor metastatic disease even in the setting of apparent systemic remission, since brain metastases can occur without concurrent systemic recurrence. A new primary glioblastoma would be expected to show a more infiltrative, less sharply demarcated margin than the description given here, and it does not explain why this specific imaging pattern would coincidentally resemble the classic metastatic appearance so precisely. A new primary oligodendroglioma would typically show a superficial cortical location with calcification rather than this sharply demarcated gray-white junction pattern, making it a poor fit. Primary CNS lymphoma classically favors deep periventricular white matter with homogeneous, not ring, enhancement, making it an inconsistent fit for this description. Meningioma is an extra-axial, dural-based tumor rather than an intraparenchymal gray-white junction mass, making it inconsistent with this imaging description regardless of his cancer history.","boardPrep":"Step 1: recognizing that a solitary brain lesion can still represent metastasis even after apparent systemic remission is an important integrative pearl that prevents premature reassurance based on surveillance imaging alone. COMLEX: a solitary lesion with a known prior cancer history still warrants tissue diagnosis when feasible, since a new primary CNS tumor, though less likely, remains part of the differential.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 47 → Obj 3 — Metastatic-versus-primary imaging differentiation already fully built there, now applied as one component of this complete integrative vignette","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_q24","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Apply patient age, tumor location, imaging characteristics, histology, and molecular markers to select the most likely diagnosis in a COMLEX Level 1 clinical vignette.","batch":2,"stem":"38-year-old woman -> new-onset seizure -> no prior medical history -> MRI shows a single, superficial, partially calcified mass involving the cortex and superficial white matter of the left frontal lobe, without significant enhancement -> biopsy shows cells with round nuclei and clear perinuclear halos within a delicate capillary network, and genetic testing confirms an IDH1 mutation with 1p/19q codeletion. Applying age, location, imaging, histology, and molecular findings together, what is the single most likely diagnosis, and what does this integration illustrate about tumor grading in current practice?","choices":{"A":"Glioblastoma, illustrating that a specific, reproducible molecular signature can confirm a diagnosis even when imaging shows minimal enhancement and clinical severity appears comparatively mild","B":"Oligodendroglioma, illustrating that a specific, reproducible molecular signature can confirm a diagnosis even when imaging shows minimal enhancement and clinical severity appears comparatively mild","C":"Metastatic disease, illustrating that a specific, reproducible molecular signature can confirm a diagnosis even when imaging shows minimal enhancement and clinical severity appears comparatively mild","D":"Primary CNS lymphoma, illustrating that a specific, reproducible molecular signature can confirm a diagnosis even when imaging shows minimal enhancement and clinical severity appears comparatively mild","E":"Meningioma, illustrating that a specific, reproducible molecular signature can confirm a diagnosis even when imaging shows minimal enhancement and clinical severity appears comparatively mild"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Integrating this patient's young adult age, superficial cortical and white matter location, calcification, minimal enhancement, perinuclear halo histology with a chicken-wire capillary network, and the defining IDH1 mutation with 1p/19q codeletion together confirms oligodendroglioma, and this case illustrates how a specific molecular signature allows confident diagnosis and grading even when the imaging appearance is comparatively unimpressive, reflecting this tumor's relatively favorable underlying biology. Glioblastoma is excluded by the IDH mutation, since glioblastoma is IDH-wildtype by definition, and its typical necrotic, heavily enhancing imaging appearance does not match this description. Metastatic disease does not carry an IDH mutation or 1p/19q codeletion, since these are diagnostic features specific to a primary glial tumor lineage, making this diagnosis inconsistent with the genetic findings. Primary CNS lymphoma is not associated with IDH mutation or 1p/19q codeletion and instead favors deep periventricular white matter with homogeneous enhancement, an imaging pattern that does not match this superficial, minimally enhancing mass. Meningioma is an extra-axial, dural-based tumor rather than an intraparenchymal cortical and white matter mass, and it is not defined by IDH mutation or 1p/19q codeletion, making it inconsistent with both the location and the molecular findings here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this complete worked example, age, location, calcification, halo histology, and IDH-plus-1p/19q molecular confirmation, is the fullest expression of the integrative board vignette strategy this objective is designed to teach. COMLEX: recognizing oligodendroglioma's comparatively favorable molecular profile helps set appropriate expectations when counseling a patient on prognosis relative to the other diffuse gliomas.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 47 → Obj 4 — Oligodendroglioma's defining IDH-mutation-plus-1p/19q-codeletion molecular signature already established there in full, now the capstone example of the integrative diagnostic algorithm this objective builds","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl48_bloom_q01","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Interpret characteristic imaging and histopathologic findings to identify major adult intracranial tumors, including glioblastoma, meningioma, vestibular schwannoma, CNS lymphoma, and hemangioblastoma.","batch":3,"stem":"A biopsy from a deep periventricular brain mass in an elderly immunosuppressed man shows sheets of large, atypical lymphoid cells with irregular, hyperchromatic nuclei, forming dense cuffs around blood vessels, with strong immunostaining for CD20. Which adult intracranial tumor does this histologic description identify?","choices":{"A":"Glioblastoma, an astrocytic tumor","B":"Primary CNS lymphoma, a B-cell tumor","C":"Meningioma, a meningothelial tumor","D":"Vestibular schwannoma, a Schwann cell tumor","E":"Hemangioblastoma, a vascular tumor"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Primary CNS lymphoma is composed of sheets of large, atypical lymphoid cells with irregular hyperchromatic nuclei that characteristically accumulate in dense cuffs around blood vessels, an angiocentric growth pattern, and strongly express the B-cell marker CD20, confirming its B-cell origin, matching this description precisely. Glioblastoma shows palisading necrosis and microvascular proliferation from an astrocytic, GFAP-positive lineage rather than this CD20-positive angiocentric lymphoid pattern. Meningioma shows a whorled pattern with psammoma bodies rather than sheets of large atypical lymphoid cells. Vestibular schwannoma shows alternating Antoni A and Antoni B zones with S100 positivity rather than this CD20-positive lymphoid pattern. Hemangioblastoma shows vacuolated stromal cells within a highly vascular capillary network rather than sheets of large atypical B lymphocytes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: angiocentric cuffing of large atypical CD20-positive B lymphocytes is the defining histologic signature of primary CNS lymphoma, completing the full adult tumor histology differential alongside meningioma, vestibular schwannoma, and hemangioblastoma. COMLEX: primary CNS lymphoma is notably steroid-responsive, so corticosteroids should be withheld before biopsy whenever possible to avoid obscuring the diagnostic histology.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 48 → Obj 5 — the adult tumor differential, tested here as a board-style histology and pathogenesis vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl48_bloom_q02","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Interpret characteristic imaging and histopathologic findings to identify major adult intracranial tumors, including glioblastoma, meningioma, vestibular schwannoma, CNS lymphoma, and hemangioblastoma.","batch":3,"stem":"A kidney transplant recipient on chronic immunosuppression develops a deep periventricular brain mass, and tumor tissue tests positive for a herpesvirus known to infect B lymphocytes. Which virus is most strongly implicated in this patient's tumor pathogenesis, and through what general mechanism does chronic immunosuppression contribute?","choices":{"A":"Cytomegalovirus, since impaired T-cell surveillance allows latently CMV-infected monocytes to transform into primary CNS lymphoma","B":"JC virus, since impaired T-cell surveillance allows JC virus-infected oligodendrocytes to proliferate into primary CNS lymphoma","C":"Epstein-Barr virus, since impaired T-cell surveillance allows latently EBV-infected B lymphocytes to proliferate into primary CNS lymphoma","D":"Human herpesvirus 8, since impaired T-cell surveillance allows HHV-8-infected endothelial cells to proliferate into primary CNS lymphoma","E":"Varicella zoster virus, since impaired T-cell surveillance allows VZV-infected ganglion neurons to transform into primary CNS lymphoma"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Primary CNS lymphoma arising in immunosuppressed patients, including transplant recipients and those with HIV/AIDS, is strongly associated with Epstein-Barr virus, which latently infects B lymphocytes; under normal circumstances, cytotoxic T cells keep EBV-infected B cells in check, but chronic immunosuppression impairs this surveillance, allowing EBV-driven B-cell proliferation to progress unchecked into malignant lymphoma within the CNS. Cytomegalovirus is a distinct herpesvirus associated with retinitis and other opportunistic infections in immunosuppressed patients, not with the pathogenesis of primary CNS lymphoma. JC virus causes progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy, a demyelinating disease, not a B-cell lymphoma, in immunosuppressed patients. Human herpesvirus 8 is associated with Kaposi sarcoma and primary effusion lymphoma, not primary CNS lymphoma. Varicella zoster virus causes chickenpox and shingles and is not the virus implicated in this specific tumor's pathogenesis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Epstein-Barr virus as the key oncogenic driver of primary CNS lymphoma in immunosuppressed patients, via impaired T-cell surveillance of latently infected B cells, is a high-yield mechanistic link between virology and oncology. COMLEX: EBV viral load in the cerebrospinal fluid can serve as a useful, less invasive diagnostic adjunct in this specific clinical scenario.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 48 → Obj 5 — the adult tumor differential, tested here as a board-style histology and pathogenesis vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl48_bloom_q03","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Interpret characteristic imaging and histopathologic findings to identify major adult intracranial tumors, including glioblastoma, meningioma, vestibular schwannoma, CNS lymphoma, and hemangioblastoma.","batch":3,"stem":"A pathologist explains that within a hemangioblastoma, the numerous thin-walled capillaries are lined by essentially normal, reactive endothelial cells, while the true neoplastic cell population lies elsewhere within the tumor. Which cell type is the actual neoplastic component of hemangioblastoma?","choices":{"A":"The vacuolated, lipid-laden stromal cells interspersed among the capillary network, which are the true neoplastic cell type","B":"The endothelial cells lining the numerous thin-walled capillaries themselves, which are the true neoplastic cell type","C":"Pericytes surrounding the capillary basement membrane, which are the true neoplastic cell type","D":"Infiltrating reactive T lymphocytes within the tumor stroma, which are the true neoplastic cell type","E":"Ependymal cells lining an adjacent ventricular surface, which are the true neoplastic cell type"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Despite hemangioblastoma's highly vascular appearance, the numerous capillaries themselves are lined by non-neoplastic, reactive endothelial cells recruited by the tumor's angiogenic signaling, while the true neoplastic cell population consists of the vacuolated, lipid-laden stromal cells interspersed between the vessels, which produce erythropoietin, vascular endothelial growth factor, and stain for inhibin, driving both the tumor's vascular proliferation and its paraneoplastic polycythemia. The endothelial cells themselves, though abundant, are reactive rather than the neoplastic component, a commonly confused point given the tumor's intensely vascular appearance. Pericytes are not the recognized neoplastic cell type in hemangioblastoma. Infiltrating T lymphocytes are inflammatory bystander cells, not the tumor's neoplastic component. Ependymal cells are unrelated to hemangioblastoma's cellular composition and are the cell of origin for a distinct tumor type, ependymoma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: identifying the vacuolated stromal cell, not the abundant surrounding capillary endothelium, as the true neoplastic component of hemangioblastoma is a frequently misunderstood but testable pathology detail. COMLEX: this stromal cell's VHL-driven overproduction of erythropoietin and VEGF directly explains both the tumor's vascularity and its associated polycythemia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 48 → Obj 5 — the adult tumor differential, tested here as a board-style histology and pathogenesis vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl48_bloom_q04","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Differentiate major pediatric tumors, including pilocytic astrocytoma, medulloblastoma, ependymoma, craniopharyngioma, and diffuse midline glioma, by age, location, presentation, and pathology.","batch":3,"stem":"A cerebellar tumor from a child shows a biphasic histologic pattern, with compact, elongated bipolar cells alternating with loosely arranged microcystic areas, and numerous eosinophilic, corkscrew-shaped Rosenthal fibers are present throughout. Genetic testing identifies a KIAA1549-BRAF fusion. Which pediatric tumor does this description identify?","choices":{"A":"Medulloblastoma, a well-circumscribed, low-grade glial tumor","B":"Pilocytic astrocytoma, a well-circumscribed, low-grade glial tumor","C":"Ependymoma, a well-circumscribed, low-grade glial tumor","D":"Craniopharyngioma, a well-circumscribed, low-grade glial tumor","E":"Diffuse midline glioma, a well-circumscribed, low-grade glial tumor"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pilocytic astrocytoma is a CNS WHO grade 1 glial tumor with a characteristic biphasic architecture, alternating compact bipolar areas and looser microcystic zones, containing eosinophilic Rosenthal fibers, and is driven in the great majority of cases by a KIAA1549-BRAF gene fusion that activates the MAP kinase pathway, distinguishing it molecularly from the diffuse gliomas. Medulloblastoma shows densely packed primitive small cells with Homer Wright rosettes rather than this biphasic, Rosenthal fiber-containing pattern, and it is not defined by a KIAA1549-BRAF fusion. Ependymoma shows perivascular pseudorosettes rather than this biphasic pilocytic pattern and is not defined by this specific BRAF fusion. Craniopharyngioma shows epithelial, enamel organ-like architecture rather than a biphasic glial pattern with Rosenthal fibers. Diffuse midline glioma shows diffusely infiltrating astrocytic morphology with an H3 K27M mutation rather than this well-circumscribed, biphasic, BRAF fusion-driven pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the biphasic architecture, Rosenthal fibers, and KIAA1549-BRAF fusion together are the complete classic histologic and molecular signature of pilocytic astrocytoma, the most favorable-prognosis pediatric glioma. COMLEX: because this tumor is driven by a single MAP kinase pathway fusion rather than the multistep mutational cascade of diffuse gliomas, complete surgical resection is frequently curative.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 48 → Obj 6 — the pediatric tumor differential, tested here as a board-style molecular-mechanism vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl48_bloom_q05","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Differentiate major pediatric tumors, including pilocytic astrocytoma, medulloblastoma, ependymoma, craniopharyngioma, and diffuse midline glioma, by age, location, presentation, and pathology.","batch":3,"stem":"A 7-year-old with a midline cerebellar medulloblastoma undergoes molecular classification. Groups include Wnt-activated, SHH-activated with TP53 mutation, SHH-activated without TP53 mutation, group 3, and group 4. According to Robbins, which group carries the best prognosis overall?","choices":{"A":"Wnt-activated medulloblastoma","B":"SHH-activated, TP53-mutant group","C":"Group 3 medulloblastoma","D":"Group 4 medulloblastoma","E":"SHH-activated, TP53-wildtype"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Robbins Ch 28 states that SHH-activated/TP53-mutant and group 3 tumors have the worst prognosis and Wnt tumors the best prognosis overall, with Wnt-activated medulloblastomas associated with nearly 100% survival at 5 years; Wnt tumors often lie near the anterior fourth ventricle or middle cerebellar peduncle. SHH-activated TP53-mutant and group 3 tumors are the two groups Robbins names as worst. Group 4 and SHH-activated TP53-wildtype tumors are not the group Robbins names as most favorable.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Medulloblastoma: malignant embryonal tumor of the cerebellum, Homer Wright rosettes, CSF spread; about 20 percent of pediatric brain tumors. COMLEX: Image the whole neuraxis because subarachnoid drop metastases are common.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 48 → Obj 7 — inherited tumor syndromes and molecular pathways in CNS tumors","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl48_bloom_q06","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Differentiate major pediatric tumors, including pilocytic astrocytoma, medulloblastoma, ependymoma, craniopharyngioma, and diffuse midline glioma, by age, location, presentation, and pathology.","batch":3,"stem":"The H3 K27M mutation defining diffuse midline glioma alters a histone tail residue that is normally trimethylated by the PRC2 complex to silence gene expression. Which broader molecular consequence follows from this specific mutation, beyond simply serving as a diagnostic marker?","choices":{"A":"The mutant histone globally enhances PRC2-mediated H3K27 trimethylation, causing widespread epigenetic silencing of genes that would normally remain actively expressed","B":"The mutant histone has no functional consequence beyond serving as a diagnostic immunohistochemical marker, with no actual effect on chromatin structure or gene expression in this tumor","C":"The mutant histone directly activates the RAS signaling pathway independent of any effect on chromatin structure or histone methylation","D":"The mutant histone globally inhibits PRC2-mediated H3K27 trimethylation, causing widespread epigenetic derepression of genes that would normally be silenced","E":"The mutant histone directly increases MGMT promoter methylation specifically, independent of any broader effect on genome-wide chromatin structure"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The H3 K27M mutation acts in a dominant-negative fashion to globally inhibit the PRC2 complex's ability to trimethylate histone H3 at lysine 27 across the genome, not just at the mutant histone itself, causing widespread epigenetic derepression, or \"loosening,\" of genes that would normally be silenced by this repressive mark, a mechanism that drives the tumor's aggressive behavior well beyond serving as a mere diagnostic label. Describing the mutation as enhancing, rather than inhibiting, PRC2 function reverses its actual, well-established dominant-negative mechanism. The mutation is not functionally silent, it produces a genuine, significant genome-wide epigenetic consequence that is central to this tumor's pathogenesis, not merely a diagnostic curiosity. The mutation's primary mechanism involves histone methylation and chromatin structure, not direct RAS pathway activation, which is instead the mechanism disrupted in neurofibromatosis type 1. The mutation does not act through a specific effect on MGMT promoter methylation, that is an unrelated biomarker relevant to glioblastoma chemotherapy response, not to diffuse midline glioma's H3 K27M mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: H3 K27M as a dominant-negative inhibitor of PRC2-mediated histone methylation, producing genome-wide epigenetic derepression, is an increasingly tested mechanistic detail beyond simply recognizing it as a diagnostic marker. COMLEX: this epigenetic mechanism is an active area of targeted drug development, since reversing this derepression represents a potential therapeutic strategy for an otherwise devastating diagnosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 48 → Obj 6 — the pediatric tumor differential, tested here as a board-style molecular-mechanism vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl48_bloom_q07","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Correlate inherited tumor syndromes with their genes, molecular pathways, and associated CNS tumors.","batch":3,"stem":"A child with a strong family history of early-onset breast cancer, sarcoma, and adrenocortical carcinoma across multiple generations develops a rare intraventricular tumor arising from choroid plexus epithelium. Germline mutation of which gene best explains this broad, multi-cancer family history together with this specific CNS tumor?","choices":{"A":"PTEN, causing Li-Fraumeni syndrome, which predisposes to choroid plexus carcinoma among a wide range of other malignancies","B":"NF1, causing Li-Fraumeni syndrome, which predisposes to choroid plexus carcinoma among a wide range of other malignancies","C":"VHL, causing Li-Fraumeni syndrome, which predisposes to choroid plexus carcinoma among a wide range of other malignancies","D":"TP53, causing Li-Fraumeni syndrome, which predisposes to choroid plexus carcinoma among a wide range of other malignancies","E":"APC, causing Li-Fraumeni syndrome, which predisposes to choroid plexus carcinoma among a wide range of other malignancies"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Li-Fraumeni syndrome results from a germline mutation in TP53, a broadly acting tumor suppressor gene, producing a strikingly wide spectrum of early-onset malignancies across generations, including breast cancer, sarcoma, adrenocortical carcinoma, and, within the CNS, choroid plexus carcinoma along with certain gliomas, reflecting TP53's fundamental role in genome stability across nearly all cell types. NF1 causes a distinct, narrower phenotype of neurofibromas, café-au-lait macules, and optic glioma through neurofibromin loss, not this broad multi-cancer family pattern or choroid plexus carcinoma specifically. VHL causes hemangioblastoma, renal cell carcinoma, and pheochromocytoma, not this specific breast cancer, sarcoma, and choroid plexus carcinoma combination. PTEN mutation causes Cowden syndrome, associated with Lhermitte-Duclos disease and various hamartomas, not this specific Li-Fraumeni tumor spectrum. APC mutation causes familial adenomatous polyposis and is associated with a distinct CNS tumor pattern, not this specific TP53-driven multi-cancer syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Li-Fraumeni syndrome's germline TP53 mutation producing an unusually broad, early-onset, multigenerational cancer spectrum, including choroid plexus carcinoma, rounds out the inherited tumor syndrome differential beyond NF1, NF2, VHL, and tuberous sclerosis complex. COMLEX: TP53's central role as \"the guardian of the genome\" makes Li-Fraumeni syndrome a useful integrative link between CNS tumor genetics and general oncology principles.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 48 → Obj 7 — inherited tumor syndromes, tested here as a board-style additional-syndrome vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 3 (Apply)"},{"id":"sdl48_bloom_q08","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Correlate inherited tumor syndromes with their genes, molecular pathways, and associated CNS tumors.","batch":3,"stem":"An adult with mucocutaneous hamartomas, macrocephaly, and a history of breast and thyroid cancer is found on MRI to have a slowly enlarging, dysplastic cerebellar mass composed of abnormally thickened, hypertrophic cerebellar folia. Germline mutation of which gene, and which specific cerebellar lesion, best explain this presentation?","choices":{"A":"PTEN mutation, causing Cowden syndrome, in which the dysplastic cerebellar lesion is called subependymal giant cell astrocytoma","B":"TSC2 mutation, causing Cowden syndrome, in which the dysplastic cerebellar lesion is called Lhermitte-Duclos disease","C":"VHL mutation, causing Cowden syndrome, in which the dysplastic cerebellar lesion is called Lhermitte-Duclos disease","D":"NF2 mutation, causing Cowden syndrome, in which the dysplastic cerebellar lesion is called Lhermitte-Duclos disease","E":"PTEN mutation, causing Cowden syndrome, in which the dysplastic cerebellar lesion is called Lhermitte-Duclos disease"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Cowden syndrome results from germline mutation of PTEN, a tumor suppressor that normally restrains the PI3K/AKT/mTOR pathway, and it is associated with mucocutaneous hamartomas, macrocephaly, and an increased risk of breast, thyroid, and endometrial cancer, along with a highly characteristic cerebellar lesion called Lhermitte-Duclos disease, a dysplastic gangliocytoma producing markedly thickened, hypertrophic cerebellar folia. Attributing the correct gene, PTEN, and syndrome, Cowden syndrome, but mislabeling the cerebellar lesion as subependymal giant cell astrocytoma is incorrect, since that lesion instead belongs to tuberous sclerosis complex, an entirely different syndrome. TSC2 mutation causes tuberous sclerosis complex, not Cowden syndrome, and does not produce this specific breast and thyroid cancer risk profile. VHL mutation causes von Hippel-Lindau disease, associated with hemangioblastoma and renal cell carcinoma, not Cowden syndrome or Lhermitte-Duclos disease. NF2 mutation causes neurofibromatosis type 2, associated with bilateral vestibular schwannomas, not Cowden syndrome or this dysplastic cerebellar lesion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cowden syndrome's PTEN mutation, mucocutaneous hamartomas, breast and thyroid cancer risk, and its distinctive Lhermitte-Duclos disease cerebellar lesion together form a complete, testable syndrome-gene-lesion pairing distinct from the other inherited tumor syndromes. COMLEX: recognizing Lhermitte-Duclos disease's thickened folial pattern on imaging should prompt systemic screening for the broader Cowden syndrome cancer spectrum.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 48 → Obj 7 — inherited tumor syndromes, tested here as a board-style additional-syndrome vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl48_bloom_q09","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Correlate inherited tumor syndromes with their genes, molecular pathways, and associated CNS tumors.","batch":3,"stem":"A teenager with hundreds of colorectal polyps identified on screening colonoscopy, prompted by his father's history of the same finding, is subsequently found to have a cerebellar medulloblastoma. Germline mutation of which gene links these two seemingly unrelated findings within a single syndrome?","choices":{"A":"TP53, causing Turcot syndrome, in which the same gene predisposes to both colorectal polyposis and medulloblastoma","B":"NF1, causing Turcot syndrome, in which the same gene predisposes to both colorectal polyposis and medulloblastoma","C":"PTEN, causing Turcot syndrome, in which the same gene predisposes to both colorectal polyposis and medulloblastoma","D":"APC, causing Turcot syndrome, in which the same gene predisposes to both colorectal polyposis and medulloblastoma","E":"VHL, causing Turcot syndrome, in which the same gene predisposes to both colorectal polyposis and medulloblastoma"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Turcot syndrome describes the co-occurrence of colorectal polyposis with a primary CNS tumor, and when caused by germline APC mutation, the same familial adenomatous polyposis gene, it classically pairs colorectal polyps with medulloblastoma, linking these two seemingly distinct findings through this single shared tumor suppressor gene, which normally regulates the Wnt/beta-catenin pathway. TP53 mutation causes Li-Fraumeni syndrome, a distinct multi-cancer syndrome not classically defined by this specific colorectal polyposis and medulloblastoma pairing. NF1 mutation causes neurofibromatosis type 1, associated with neurofibromas and optic glioma, not colorectal polyposis or this Turcot syndrome pairing. PTEN mutation causes Cowden syndrome, associated with Lhermitte-Duclos disease, not colorectal polyposis and medulloblastoma. VHL mutation causes von Hippel-Lindau disease, associated with hemangioblastoma and renal cell carcinoma, not this specific colorectal and medulloblastoma combination.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Turcot syndrome's APC-driven pairing of colorectal polyposis with medulloblastoma, or with glioblastoma in its mismatch-repair-associated variant, is a useful, if less commonly tested, addition connecting gastrointestinal and CNS tumor genetics. COMLEX: recognizing a family history of colorectal polyposis in a patient with a new CNS tumor should prompt consideration of this specific syndromic link.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 48 → Obj 7 — inherited tumor syndromes, tested here as a board-style additional-syndrome vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl48_bloom_q10","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Apply patient age, tumor location, imaging characteristics, histology, and molecular markers to select the most likely diagnosis in a COMLEX Level 1 clinical vignette.","batch":3,"stem":"A board vignette describes a 45-year-old man, an age and posterior fossa location combination that does not fit the typical pediatric-versus-adult epidemiologic split emphasized as the first-step filter. His MRI shows a cystic cerebellar mass with an enhancing mural nodule, and his uncle had a similar tumor along with renal cell carcinoma. How should this age-atypical presentation be handled within the integrative diagnostic framework?","choices":{"A":"The atypical age should be treated as a data-entry error in the vignette and disregarded entirely in favor of assuming the most common pediatric posterior fossa tumor","B":"The atypical age should prompt consideration of an inherited syndrome, since this family history of hemangioblastoma with renal cell carcinoma strongly suggests von Hippel-Lindau disease","C":"The atypical age definitively excludes any posterior fossa tumor diagnosis altogether, since posterior fossa tumors occur exclusively in children under this framework","D":"The family history should be treated as an irrelevant detail, since inherited tumor syndromes play no role in adult posterior fossa tumors","E":"The cystic architecture and enhancing mural nodule should instead be reinterpreted as pilocytic astrocytoma regardless of this patient's age, family history, or additional renal findings"},"correct":"B","explanation":"While age is a powerful initial filter, a posterior fossa tumor in an adult is not itself impossible, and when combined with a family history of the same tumor type and renal cell carcinoma, this atypical presentation should specifically prompt consideration of an inherited tumor syndrome, in this case von Hippel-Lindau disease, in which hemangioblastoma, a normally adult-occurring posterior fossa tumor, appears alongside renal cell carcinoma across generations, illustrating that the age-location framework is a starting heuristic to be refined by additional history, not an absolute, unbreakable rule. Dismissing the age as a data-entry error ignores genuinely important clinical information rather than integrating it appropriately. Posterior fossa tumors are not exclusive to children, hemangioblastoma itself is predominantly an adult tumor, so this categorical exclusion misapplies the age-location framework. The family history is highly relevant here, not irrelevant, since it is precisely what resolves this atypical presentation into a specific syndromic diagnosis. Reinterpreting this as pilocytic astrocytoma ignores the patient's age, family history, and renal findings, all of which point specifically toward hemangioblastoma and von Hippel-Lindau disease instead.","boardPrep":"Step 1: recognizing when an atypical age-location combination should prompt consideration of an inherited tumor syndrome, rather than being dismissed or forced into the usual pattern, is an important refinement of the basic age-first integrative strategy. COMLEX: family history should always be actively elicited when a tumor presentation does not fit its usual expected age or location pattern.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 48 → Obj 8 — the integrative board-vignette algorithm, tested here as a board-style discordance and capstone vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl48_bloom_q11","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Apply patient age, tumor location, imaging characteristics, histology, and molecular markers to select the most likely diagnosis in a COMLEX Level 1 clinical vignette.","batch":3,"stem":"A board vignette describes a 68-year-old man with a supratentorial mass whose imaging and age strongly suggest glioblastoma, but molecular testing unexpectedly returns an IDH1 mutation. How should this discordance between the age-based impression and the molecular finding be resolved?","choices":{"A":"The age-based impression should take priority, and the molecular finding should be dismissed as a laboratory error, since glioblastoma is definitionally impossible to reclassify once initially suspected based on age","B":"Both findings should be considered equally weighted and mutually exclusive, requiring an entirely separate third diagnosis that accounts for neither the age nor the molecular result on its own","C":"The molecular finding should be ignored entirely in older patients, since IDH mutational status only applies diagnostically to patients under 55 years of age in current practice","D":"This discordance indicates a laboratory contamination event that should prompt repeat testing before any further diagnostic consideration of the case is undertaken","E":"The molecular finding should take priority, reclassifying the tumor as a high-grade IDH-mutant astrocytoma rather than glioblastoma, since IDH status is the defining criterion regardless of age"},"correct":"E","explanation":"When a molecular finding and an age-based clinical impression genuinely conflict, the molecular finding should generally take priority for final diagnostic classification, since IDH mutational status is the defining criterion separating glioblastoma from astrocytoma regardless of patient age, so this tumor should be reclassified as a high-grade IDH-mutant astrocytoma, illustrating that while age is a powerful and efficient initial filter, it is ultimately a probabilistic heuristic that molecular testing can override when the two are discordant. Dismissing a validated molecular finding as a laboratory error simply because it conflicts with an age-based expectation abandons genuinely useful, more specific diagnostic information in favor of a less specific heuristic. These findings are not mutually exclusive in a way that requires an entirely separate third diagnosis, the correct approach is to let the more specific molecular finding refine, not replace with something unrelated, the working diagnosis. IDH mutational testing remains diagnostically meaningful across all adult age groups, it is not restricted to younger patients only. While repeat testing is occasionally reasonable for confirmation, the discordance itself does not automatically indicate contamination, IDH-mutant tumors, though less common, genuinely do occur in older patients.","boardPrep":"Step 1: recognizing that molecular findings generally take precedence over age-based expectations when the two are genuinely discordant reinforces that the integrative framework is a probabilistic tool, not a rigid, unbreakable hierarchy. COMLEX: this same principle, letting more specific evidence override a more general heuristic when they conflict, is a broadly transferable clinical reasoning skill beyond neuro-oncology alone.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 48 → Obj 8 — the integrative board-vignette algorithm, tested here as a board-style discordance and capstone vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"},{"id":"sdl48_bloom_q12","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Apply patient age, tumor location, imaging characteristics, histology, and molecular markers to select the most likely diagnosis in a COMLEX Level 1 clinical vignette.","batch":3,"stem":"A 52-year-old man presents with six months of progressive unilateral hearing loss, and MRI reveals an enhancing cerebellopontine angle mass extending into the internal auditory canal. His younger sister was diagnosed with a similar tumor on her opposite side two years ago. Applying age, location, imaging, and this family history together, what is the single most likely diagnosis, and which additional finding would most strongly support it?","choices":{"A":"Sporadic, unilateral vestibular schwannoma unrelated to his sister's tumor, most strongly supported by finding a second vestibular schwannoma on his own opposite side","B":"Meningioma of the cerebellopontine angle, most strongly supported by finding a second vestibular schwannoma on his own opposite side","C":"Vestibular schwannoma as part of neurofibromatosis type 2, most strongly supported by finding a second vestibular schwannoma on his own opposite side","D":"Glioblastoma extending into the internal auditory canal, most strongly supported by finding a second vestibular schwannoma on his own opposite side","E":"Hemangioblastoma of the cerebellopontine angle, most strongly supported by finding a second vestibular schwannoma on his own opposite side"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Integrating this patient's adult age, classic cerebellopontine angle location with internal auditory canal extension, and, critically, a sibling with a contralateral vestibular schwannoma, points toward neurofibromatosis type 2 rather than a sporadic tumor, and the finding that would most strongly confirm this would be identifying a second vestibular schwannoma on his own opposite side, since bilateral vestibular schwannomas are essentially pathognomonic for this inherited syndrome. Treating his presentation as sporadic and unrelated to his sister's tumor ignores the highly suggestive family history, which should specifically raise suspicion for an inherited syndrome rather than being dismissed as coincidental. Meningioma can occur in the cerebellopontine angle, but the internal auditory canal extension and this specific family history pattern more strongly favor vestibular schwannoma and NF2 specifically. Glioblastoma is a supratentorial, intra-axial tumor and would not classically arise in the cerebellopontine angle with internal auditory canal extension. Hemangioblastoma is a cerebellar parenchymal tumor with a cystic mural nodule, not a cerebellopontine angle mass extending into the internal auditory canal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: this complete integration, adult age, classic cerebellopontine angle location and imaging, and a family history of a contralateral tumor in a sibling, converging on NF2-associated vestibular schwannoma, is a capstone example of using family history as a decisive piece of integrative evidence. COMLEX: identifying one family member with NF2-associated bilateral vestibular schwannomas should prompt genetic counseling and screening imaging for all first-degree relatives, exactly as illustrated by this sibling pair.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 48 → Obj 8 — the integrative board-vignette algorithm, tested here as a board-style discordance and capstone vignette","isHighYield":true,"bloomLevel":"Level 4 (Analyze)"}]};
